window.EF_CRUX_DOC_PAGES=["ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Constitutional Development of India\nCONSTITUTIONAL DEVELOPMENT OF INDIA —\nMEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 1 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — sirf wahi dates, Acts, firsts, titles aur exam-confusion points hain jo baar-baar bhoolte ya\nswap hote hain.\n1. Do Era + 12 Acts — Pehle Skeleton Yaad Karo\n• Company Rule (1773–1858) = 5 major Acts\n• Crown Rule (1858–1947) = 7 major Acts\n• Total = 12 major Acts\n TRICK — “5 Company, 7 Crown”\n💡\nBas 5–7 ka pair yaad rakho: pehle Company ke 5 Acts, phir Crown ke 7 Acts.\nCompany sequence: Regulating 1773\n Pitts 1784\n Charter 1813\n Charter 1833\n Charter 1853.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nCrown sequence: 1858\n 1861\n 1892\n 1909\n 1919\n 1935\n 1947.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n2. Company Rule — 5 Acts Ka Fast Memory Ladder\n• 1773 Regulating Act\n Governor General of Bengal + Supreme Court\n→\n• 1784 Pitts India Act\n Dual Government\n→\n• 1813 Charter Act\n Trade monopoly partly ended + ₹1 lakh education + missionaries\n→\n• 1833 Charter Act\n Governor General of India + Law Commission + monopoly completely ended\n→\n• 1853 Charter Act\n Legislative/Admin separation + Civil Services open competition\n→\n TRICK — “73–84–13–33–53” key-word chain\n💡\n73 = Bengal title, 84 = Dual control, 13 = Education + partial monopoly end, 33 = India title + complete monopoly end,\n53 = Competition + separation.\nBolke revise karo: “73 Bengal, 84 Dual, 13 Education, 33 India, 53 ICS.”\nRegulating Act 1773 — Bengal Wala First\n• Governor of Bengal\n Governor General of Bengal\n→\n• First Governor General of Bengal = Lord Warren Hastings\n• Supreme Court = Calcutta, 1774\n• First Chief Justice = Sir Elijah Impey\n TRICK — “73 Hastings, 74 Court”\n💡\nAct 1773 ko Hastings se jodo; agle saal 1774 mein Calcutta Supreme Court.\nBengal wala title 1773 hai — India wala title 1833 mein aayega.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nGovernor General of Bengal = Warren Hastings (1773).\nGovernor General of India = William Bentinck (1833). Dono titles ko swap mat karna.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Constitutional Development of India\nPitts India Act 1784 — Do Body, Do Kaam\n• Commercial matters = Court of Directors\n• Political matters = Board of Control\n• Governor General's Council = 4\n 3\n→\n• Company territory ko “British-Possession in India” kaha gaya\n TRICK — “Company = Commerce, Board = Politics”\n💡\nCourt of Directors ko Company/commercial side samjho.\nBoard of Control ko British Government/political control side samjho.\n1784 ke saath 4\n3 bhi chipka lo.\n→\n3. Charter Acts 1813–1833–1853 — Teen Bhai, Teen Kaam\n• 1813\n Monopoly partly ended; Tea + China exception; ₹1 lakh education; missionaries allowed\n→\n• 1833\n Tea + China monopoly bhi ended; GG of India; Bentinck; Law Commission 1834, Macaulay\n→\n• 1853\n Legislative vs Administrative functions separate; Court of Directors 24\n18; Civil Services open\n→\n→\ncompetition; local representation\n TRICK — “13 = Partial, 33 = Complete, 53 = Competition”\n💡\n1813: monopoly sirf partly khatam.\n1833: monopoly completely khatam + Bengal se India title.\n1853: Civil Services competition + legislative/admin separation.\nShort chant: “13 Partial, 33 Complete, 53 Compete.”\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n“Trade monopoly abolished” dekh kar wording check karo: Tea/China exception ke saath = 1813; completely abolished\n= 1833.\nCourt of Directors 24\n18 = 1853; Governor General's Council 4\n3 = 1784.\n→\n→\n4. Crown Rule Start — 1858: Company Gayi, Crown Aaya\n• Government of India Act 1858\n Company rule end; rule British Crown ko transfer\n→\n• Governor General of India\n Viceroy of India\n→\n• First Viceroy = Lord Canning\n• Secretary of State for India post created\n• Court of Directors + Board of Control abolished\n TRICK — “Company\n Crown, GG\n Viceroy”\n💡\n→\n→\n1858 ko ek bada title-switch samjho: Company se Crown aur Governor General se Viceroy.\nCanning same person the — pehle Governor General of India, phir first Viceroy.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nFirst Viceroy = Lord Canning. Naya insaan nahi aaya; title change hua.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Constitutional Development of India\n5. Indian Councils Acts — 61, 92, 09\n• 1861\n Portfolio system + Viceroy ordinance power + legislative councils expansion\n→\n• 1892\n Indirect elections + questions + budget debate + non-official members\n→\n• 1909\n Morley-Minto + Separate Electorate for Muslims + first Indian in Viceroy's Executive Council:\n→\nSatyendra Prasad Sinha\n TRICK — “61 Portfolio\n 92 Budget\n 09 Muslim Separate”\n💡\n→\n→\nTeen Councils Acts ko sirf ek ladder mein yaad karo:\n1861 = Portfolio, 1892 = Budget/Indirect election, 1909 = Muslim separate electorate.\nShort code: P-B-S\n Portfolio, Budget, Separate.\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSeparate Electorate for Muslims first introduced = 1909.\n1919 mein ye expand hua — introduction aur expansion ko swap mat karna.\n6. 1909 vs 1919 — Naam Bhi Similar, Feature Bhi Trap\n• 1909 Morley-Minto\n Separate electorate for Muslims; Councils expansion; Satyendra Prasad Sinha\n→\n• 1919 Montagu-Chelmsford\n Provincial Dyarchy; Central Bicameralism; Direct elections (limited\n→\nfranchise); separate electorate expanded\n TRICK — “Minto = Muslim; Montagu = Two Levels”\n💡\nMinto ke M se Muslim yaad karo\n 1909 Separate Electorate.\n→\n1919 mein do alag levels: Province = Dyarchy, Centre = Bicameral.\nBolke check karo: “19 Province-Dyarchy, Centre-Bicameral.”\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nDyarchy = Provincial level (1919), Central level nahi.\nBicameral legislature = Central level (1919). Dono same Act, par levels alag.\n7. 1935 & 1947 — Blueprint Se British Exit Tak\n• Government of India Act 1935\n All India Federation provision\n→\n• 3 Lists = Federal, Provincial, Concurrent\n• Federal Judiciary + RBI establishment\n• Bicameral system provinces tak; separate electorates further expanded\n• India Independence Act 1947\n British rule end; Viceroy + Secretary of State posts abolished\n→\n• Princely States: India / Pakistan / Independent option\n• Constituent Assembly ko Constitution banane aur adopt karne ki power\n TRICK — “35 = 3 Lists + RBI; 47 = Finish”\n💡\n1935 ko ‘3’ se jodo: 3 Lists. Saath mein RBI aur federation blueprint.\n1947 = Finish British rule + Constituent Assembly power.\nShort chain: “35 Lists-RBI, 47 Liberty.”","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Constitutional Development of India\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nRBI = Government of India Act 1935. 1947 ya Constitution ke baad nahi.\nBicameral Centre = 1919; bicameral provinces tak = 1935.\n8. Important Firsts — Title Ladder\n• First Governor General of Bengal\n Warren Hastings\n Regulating Act 1773\n→\n→\n• First Chief Justice, Supreme Court\n Sir Elijah Impey\n 1774\n→\n→\n• First Governor General of India\n Lord William Bentinck\n Charter Act 1833\n→\n→\n• Law Commission Chairman\n Lord Macaulay\n 1834\n→\n→\n• First Viceroy of India\n Lord Canning\n Government of India Act 1858\n→\n→\n• First Indian in Viceroy's Executive Council\n Satyendra Prasad Sinha\n 1909\n→\n→\n TRICK — “H-I-B-M-C-S” people chain\n💡\nHastings\n Impey\n Bentinck\n Macaulay\n Canning\n Sinha.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nRole chain bolo: Bengal GG\n Chief Justice\n India GG\n Law Commission\n Viceroy\n First Indian Executive\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nCouncillor.\nNaam se zyada role-order yaad rakho.\n9. Numbers Jo Exam Mein Swap Hote Hain\n• 1773 Act\n Supreme Court actually established 1774\n→\n• 1784 Pitts\n Governor General's Council 4\n3\n→\n→\n• 1813 Charter\n ₹1 lakh annually for education\n→\n• 1834\n Law Commission, Chairman Macaulay\n→\n• 1853 Charter\n Court of Directors 24\n18\n→\n→\n TRICK — “4\n3 Pitt; 24\n18 Charter”\n💡\n→\n→\nChhota reduction 4\n3 = Pitts 1784.\n→\nBada reduction 24\n18 = Charter 1853.\n→\nEducation number = ₹1 lakh = Charter 1813; Court year = 1774.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“5 Company + 7 Crown\n 73 Bengal-Hastings/74 Court\n 84 Dual + 4\n3\n 13 Partial monopoly + ₹1L\n 33 India-\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nBentinck + Macaulay + Complete monopoly\n 53 ICS + separation + 24\n18\n 58 Crown-Canning-Viceroy\n 61 Portfolio\n→\n→\n→\n→\n 92 Budget + Indirect\n 09 Minto-Muslim Separate\n 19 Province Dyarchy + Centre Bicameral + Direct\n 35 3 Lists +\n→\n→\n→\n→\nRBI + Provincial Bicameral\n 47 British Finish + CA Power.”\n→\nIs chain ko 2–3 baar bolne se poora chapter chronological order mein recall ho jaata hai.\n Top 3:\n⚠\nBengal GG vs India GG | 1813 partial vs 1833 complete monopoly | 1909 Separate Electorate vs 1919\nDyarchy/Bicameral.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Making of the Constitution\nMAKING OF THE CONSTITUTION — MEMORY TRICK\nSHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 2 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — dates, CA numbers, committees, key people, Cabinet Mission, original Constitution aur Interim\nCabinet ke wahi facts hain jo exam mein confuse hote hain.\n1. Background Timeline — Demand Se Cabinet Mission Tak\n• 1895 — Swaraj Bill (Bal Gangadhar Tilak)\n Constituent Assembly ka pehla mention\n→\n• 1928 — Nehru Report\n Chairman: Motilal Nehru\n→\n• 1934 — M.N. Roy\n Constituent Assembly ki pehli formal demand\n→\n• 1935 — INC\n Constituent Assembly ki demand\n→\n• 1940 — August Offer\n Viceroy Lord Linlithgow; Individual Satyagraha: Vinoba Bhave 1st, Jawaharlal Nehru\n→\n2nd\n• 1942 — Cripps Mission\n CA formation accepted; Congress complete independence chahta tha\n→\n• 1946 — Cabinet Mission\n CA formation ka practical road clear\n→\n TRICK — “95–28–34–35–40–42–46” ladder\n💡\nSequence ko event-word ke saath bolo: “Tilak\n Nehru\n Roy\n INC\n August\n Cripps\n Cabinet.”\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nSabse important first-trap: 1895 = pehla mention, 1934 = pehli formal demand, 1935 = INC demand.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n“First demand” wording dekhkar context check karo: pehla mention = Swaraj Bill 1895 (Tilak); formal demand = M.N.\nRoy 1934; INC demand = 1935.\n2. Cabinet Mission 1946 — Teen Aadmi, Ek Mission\n• Pethick Lawrence\n Secretary of State for India; Chairman\n→\n• Stafford Cripps\n Trade Board\n→\n• A.V. Alexander\n Naval Board\n→\n• Constituent Assembly formation accepted by Congress & Muslim League\n• Separate Pakistan proposal rejected\n• Muslim League ki communal electorate demand accepted\n TRICK — “P-C-A = Pethick–Cripps–Alexander”\n💡\nTeen members ko P-C-A order mein yaad karo.\nRole hook: Pethick = Chairman, Cripps = Trade, Alexander = Naval.\nDecision hook: CA = YES, Separate Pakistan = NO, Communal Electorate = YES.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCabinet Mission ne separate Pakistan proposal REJECT kiya, lekin communal electorate demand accept ki. Dono ko\nopposite direction mein yaad rakho.\n3. Constituent Assembly Seats — 389 Se 299, Sign 284\n• Before Partition total seats = 389\n• Elected = 296 (292 + 4 Chief Commissioner areas)","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Making of the Constitution\n• Nominated Princely States = 93\n• Representation = 1 seat per 10 lakh people\n• After Partition total = 299\n• Final Constitution par signatures = 284\n• Women members = 15\n• Election results: Congress 208, Muslim League 73, Others 15\n TRICK — “389 > 299 > 284” shrink chain\n💡\nPehle Assembly = 389, Partition ke baad = 299, sign karne wale = 284.\nSide numbers: 296 elected + 93 nominated = 389; women = 15.\nSeats yaad karte waqt sirf ye descending picture banao: 389 > 299 > 284.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n299 final seats the, lekin Constitution par 284 members ne sign kiya — 299 ko signature count mat bana dena.\n4. CA Key Positions — Temporary, Permanent, Advisor\n• Temporary President = Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha\n• Permanent President = Dr. Rajendra Prasad\n• Vice-President = H.C. Mukherjee\n• Constitutional Advisor = B.N. Rau\n• First Parliament = Constituent Assembly itself\n TRICK — “S-R-M-R” role chain\n💡\nSinha = Start/Temporary, Rajendra = Regular/Permanent, Mukherjee = Vice, Rau = Advisor.\nSound hook: “Sinha starts, Rajendra rules, Mukherjee vice, Rau advises.”\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nTemporary President = Sachchidananda Sinha; Permanent President = Rajendra Prasad. Dono alag posts/facts hain.\n5. Sessions & Big Numbers — 11, 165, 2-11-18, 64\n• Total Sessions = 11\n• Working Days = 165\n• First Meeting = 9 Dec 1946; 211 members present\n• First Session = 9–23 Dec 1946\n• Last Session (11th) = 14–26 Nov 1949\n• Total Duration = 2 years 11 months 18 days\n• Total Expense\n ₹64 lakhs\n≈\n• Symbol = Elephant\n• Mahatma Gandhi and Jinnah = NOT members of Constituent Assembly\n TRICK — “11–165–2/11/18–64–Elephant”\n💡\nCA ka number-pack ek saath bolo: “11 sessions, 165 days, 2y-11m-18d, 64 lakh, Elephant.”\nNegative fact ko alag stamp karo: Gandhi + Jinnah = CA members nahi.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Making of the Constitution\n6. Women + Original Constitution — Who Did What\n• Sucheta Kriplani\n 1st Woman Chief Minister of India (UP)\n→\n• Sarojini Naidu\n 1st Woman Governor of India (UP)\n→\n• Amrit Kaur\n 1st Health Minister of India\n→\n• English Calligrapher = Prem Behari Narain Raizada\n• Hindi Calligrapher = Vasant Krishna Vaidya\n• Illustrators = Nandalal Bose + Beohar Rammanohar Sinha\n• National Flag Designer = Pingali Venkayya\n TRICK — “English Prem, Hindi Vasant; Art Nandalal-Beohar; Flag Pingali”\n💡\nCalligraphy pair ko language ke saath yaad karo: English = Prem Behari, Hindi = Vasant Krishna.\nPicture/art = Nandalal + Beohar; Flag = Pingali.\nWomen hook: Sucheta-CM, Sarojini-Governor, Amrit-Health.\n7. 8 Major Committees — Chairman Matching\n• Drafting Committee\n Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n→\n• Steering Committee\n Dr. Rajendra Prasad\n→\n• Union Powers Committee\n Jawaharlal Nehru\n→\n• Provincial Committee\n Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\n→\n• Flag Committee\n Dr. Rajendra Prasad\n→\n• Minorities Committee\n H.C. Mukherjee\n→\n• House Committee\n Pattabhi Sitaramayya\n→\n• Rules of Procedure Committee\n Dr. Rajendra Prasad\n→\n TRICK — “Rajendra = S-F-R” triple\n💡\nRajendra Prasad ne 3 committees chair ki: Steering, Flag, Rules of Procedure.\nShort code: S-F-R = Steering–Flag–Rules.\nBaaki anchor: Drafting-Ambedkar, Union-Nehru, Provincial-Patel, Minorities-Mukherjee, House-Pattabhi.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nRajendra Prasad = 3 committees, sirf Flag ya Steering nahi. S-F-R teeno yaad rakho.\n8. Drafting Committee — 7 Members + 2 Replacements\n• Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n Chairman\n→\n• Krishnaswamy Iyer\n• N. Gopalaswami Ayyangar\n• Saadullah Khan\n• B.L. Mitter\n replaced by N. Madhav Rao\n→\n• D.P. Khaitan\n passed away; replaced by T.T. Krishnamachari\n→\n• K.M. Munshi\n TRICK — “A-I-A-S-M-K-M” base chain\n💡\nAmbedkar\n Iyer\n Ayyangar\n Saadullah\n Mitter\n Khaitan\n Munshi.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nReplacement pair alag se yaad karo: Mitter\n Madhav Rao; Khaitan\n Krishnamachari.\n→\n→\nMnemonic line: “Ambedkar-Iyer-Ayyangar-Saadullah, Mitter-Khaitan-Munshi.”","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Making of the Constitution\n9. Critical Dates — Adopted vs Enforced\n• 9 Dec 1946\n First Meeting of Constituent Assembly\n→\n• 22 Jul 1947\n National Flag adopted\n→\n• 4 Nov 1948\n Draft Constitution presented by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n→\n• 26 Nov 1949\n Constitution ADOPTED (Constitution Day)\n→\n• 24 Jan 1950\n National Anthem & National Song adopted; Rajendra Prasad became First President\n→\n• 26 Jan 1950\n Constitution ENFORCED (Republic Day)\n→\n TRICK — “9D–22J–4N–26N–24J–26J” date chain\n💡\nEvent words ke saath bolo: Meeting\n Flag\n Draft\n Adopted\n Anthem/Song\n Enforced.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nSabse important pair: 26 Nov = Adopted; 26 Jan = Enforced.\n26 January choose hone ka source-link: 26 Jan 1930 Independence Day observance (Purna Swaraj connection).\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nConstitution Day = 26 November 1949 (Adopted). Republic Day = 26 January 1950 (Enforced). Same ‘26’, month alag.\n10. Interim Cabinet 1946 — Portfolio Hooks\n• Jawaharlal Nehru\n Vice President of Executive Council; External Affairs\n→\n• Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\n Home, Information & Broadcasting\n→\n• Baldev Singh\n Defence\n→\n• Jagjivan Ram\n Labour\n→\n• Liaquat Ali Khan\n Finance\n→\n• Ghaznafar Ali\n Health\n→\n• C. Rajagopalachari\n Education\n→\n• Rajendra Prasad\n Food & Agriculture\n→\n• Jogendra Nath Mandal\n Law\n→\n• Abdur Rab Nishtar\n Communication\n→\n• Asaf Ali\n Railways & Transport\n→\n• T.T. Chundrigar\n Commerce\n→\n• John Mathai\n Industries & Supplies\n→\n TRICK — “Big 6 portfolio anchors”\n💡\nNehru = External, Patel = Home, Baldev = Defence, Jagjivan = Labour, Liaquat = Finance, Rajendra Prasad = Food &\nAgriculture.\nBaaki chain: Ghaznafar-Health\n Rajagopalachari-Education\n Mandal-Law\n Nishtar-Communication\n Asaf-\n→\n→\n→\n→\nRailways\n Chundrigar-Commerce\n Mathai-Industries.\n→\n→\nSpecial hook: Liaquat Ali Khan = Finance in 1946 Interim Cabinet.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n1895 Tilak mention\n 1928 Motilal\n 1934 Roy formal\n 1935 INC\n 1940 August (Vinoba1/Nehru2)\n 1942 Cripps\n 1946 Cabinet\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nPCA\n CA 389>299>284; women15; Sinha Temp/Rajendra Perm/Mukherjee Vice/Rau Advisor\n 11/165/2y11m18d/₹64L/Elephant\n\n→\n→\n→\nAmbedkar-Draft; Rajendra-SFR; Nehru-Union; Patel-Provincial\n Draft7 + Mitter-Madhav + Khaitan-Krishnamachari\n 9D46 Meet;\n→\n→\n22J47 Flag; 4N48 Draft; 26N49 Adopt; 24J50 Anthem/Song; 26J50 Enforce.\n Top 3: 1895 mention vs 1934 formal | 389 vs 299 vs 284 | 26 Nov Adopted vs 26 Jan Enforced.\n⚠","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Preamble & Features of the Constitution\nPREAMBLE & FEATURES OF THE CONSTITUTION - MEMORY\nTRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 3 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein - Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai - Preamble ke source, JELF objectives, 42nd Amendment, expert quotes, case laws aur Federal vs Unitary ke\nwahi facts hain jo exam mein baar-baar confuse hote hain.\n1. Preamble Ka Skeleton - Nature + Objectives\n• Nature of State = Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic, Republic\n• Objectives = J-E-L-F: Justice, Equality, Liberty, Fraternity\n• Fraternity = dignity of individual + unity and integrity of Nation\nTRICK - “3S + DR, then JELF”\nNature ko bolo: Sovereign - Socialist - Secular - Democratic - Republic = 3S + D + R.\nObjectives ko source ke order mein JELF bolo: Justice - Equality - Liberty - Fraternity.\nPreamble ka short skeleton: “3S-DR gives JELF.”\n2. Preamble Ke Sources - Concept, Language, Basis\n• Preamble text concept = American Constitution\n• Language style = Australian Constitution\n• Inspiration / Basis = Objective Resolution, J.L. Nehru, 13 Dec 1946\n• Preamble = Introduction / Preface; Non-Justiciable; neither gives power nor creates a hurdle to government\nTRICK - “America Concept, Australia Language, Nehru Basis”\nDo A ko alag rakho: America = concept; Australia = language style.\nThird anchor = Nehru ka Objective Resolution = actual basis/inspiration.\nShort code: A-A-N = America, Australia, Nehru.\nEXAM TRAP\nConcept = American aur language style = Australian. Dono ko swap mat karna. Basis = Objective Resolution, 13 Dec 1946.\n3. JELF Objectives - Justice Aur Liberty Ka Number Trap\n• Justice = Social, Economic, Political\n• Economic Justice = 1917 Russian Revolution se inspired\n• Equality = no special privileges + equal before law\n• Liberty = Thought, Expression, Belief, Faith, Worship (5 types)\n• Liberty = French Revolution se inspired\n• Fraternity = brotherhood + dignity + unity & integrity\nTRICK - “Justice = SEP; Liberty = T-E-B-F-W”\nJustice ke 3 types: Social - Economic - Political = S-E-P.\nLiberty ke 5: Thought - Expression - Belief - Faith - Worship. Ek spoken chain banao: “Think, Express, Believe, Faith, Worship.”\nInspiration pair: Economic Justice = Russia 1917; Liberty = France.\nEXAM TRAP\nRussian Revolution ko poori Justice se blindly mat jodo - source specifically Economic Justice ko 1917 Russian Revolution se\nconnect karta hai. Liberty = French Revolution.\n4. Nature of State - 5 Words Ka Meaning\n• Sovereign = internal & external decisions mein free from external influence","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Preamble & Features of the Constitution\n• Socialist = democratic socialism; mixed economy (public + private)\n• Secular = no state religion; equal respect for all religions\n• Democratic = power with people; India = Indirect Democracy through elected representatives\n• Republic = Head of State elected, NOT hereditary\nTRICK - “3S + DR meaning ladder”\nSovereign = Free; Socialist = Mixed; Secular = Equal religion; Democratic = People; Republic = Elected head.\nOne-line recall: “Free - Mixed - Equal - People - Elected.”\nDirect Democracy ka example source mein Switzerland; India = Indirect Democracy.\nEXAM TRAP\nRepublic ka matlab elected Head of State hai, not hereditary. Democratic ka matlab log representatives choose karte hain -\ndono meanings alag hain.\n5. 42nd Amendment 1976 - Sirf Ek Baar, 3 Words\n• Preamble amended only ONCE = 42nd Amendment, 1976\n• Added: Socialist, Secular, Integrity\n• Original: Sovereign Democratic Republic -> after amendment: Sovereign Socialist Secular Democratic Republic\n• Original: Unity of the Nation -> Unity and Integrity of the Nation\nTRICK - “42 = SSI insertion”\n3 added words ka short code: S-S-I = Socialist, Secular, Integrity.\n42nd ko “one amendment, three insertions” ke saath yaad karo.\nSocialist + Secular nature line mein aaye; Integrity fraternity wali unity line mein aaya.\nEXAM TRAP\nPreamble sirf ONCE amend hua hai - 42nd Amendment (1976). Added words = Socialist, Secular, Integrity.\n6. Expert Quotes - Naam Ko Object Se Jodo\n• Ernest Baker = “Key to the Constitution”\n• Krishnaswami Ayyar = “Long-Term Dreams”\n• N.A. Palkhivala = “Identity card of the Constitution”\n• K.M. Munshi = “Horoscope of the Republic”\n• Thakur Bhargava Das = “Soul of the Constitution”\nTRICK - “Key - Dreams - ID - Horoscope - Soul” visual story\nBaker ke haath mein KEY socho; Ayyar sapne dekh rahe hain; Palkhivala ID card dikha rahe hain;\nMunshi horoscope padh rahe hain; Bhargava “soul” ki baat kar rahe hain.\nObject yaad aaya to expert ka naam recall karo.\n7. Landmark Cases - 1960 NO Se 1973 YES Tak\n• Berubari Union Case 1960 = Preamble NOT part of Constitution; cannot be amended\n• Sajjan Singh 1965 = Berubari reconsideration suggested\n• Golaknath 1967 = basic spirit eternal & non-negotiable\n• Kesavananda Bharati 1973 = Preamble IS part; can be amended but basic structure cannot be changed\n• S.R. Bommai 1994 = Preamble integral part\n• LIC v. Union of India 1995 = integral part; yes","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Preamble & Features of the Constitution\nTRICK - “60 NO -> 73 YES -> 94/95 Confirm”\nCase-law ka sabse important turn: Berubari 1960 = NOT part; Kesavananda 1973 = IS part.\nKesavananda formula: Preamble amend ho sakta hai, Basic Structure nahi.\n1994 Bommai + 1995 LIC ne integral-part position ko reinforce kiya.\nEXAM TRAP\nKesavananda Bharati (1973) ko “Preamble amend nahi ho sakta” mat samajhna. Source ke mutabik amendment ho sakta hai,\nlekin Basic Structure nahi badla ja sakta.\n8. 11 Salient Features - 4 Buckets Mein Compress Karo\n• Government: Parliamentary System; Three-Tier Governance; Quasi-Federal with Unitary Bias\n• Constitutional design: Blend of Rigidity & Flexibility; Rule of Law; Supremacy of Constitution\n• Citizens: Universal Adult Franchise; Single Citizenship; Special Provisions for SC/ST & Minorities; Fundamental Rights &\nDuties\n• Guard: Independent & Impartial Judiciary\nTRICK - “Government - Design - Citizens - Guard”\n11 points ko 4 memory boxes mein rakho: Govt / Design / Citizens / Guard.\nGovt = Parliamentary + 3-tier + Quasi-federal. Design = Rigid-Flexible + Rule of Law + Supremacy.\nCitizens = Franchise + Single Citizenship + Special Provisions + FR/Duties. Guard = Independent Judiciary.\n9. Federal vs Unitary - Do Side Ka Balance\n• Federal: Division of Powers; Written Constitution; Bicameral Legislature; Rajya Sabha; Separate Centre/State\nGovernments; Complex Amendment\n• Unitary: Strong Centre; President Rule (Art. 356); Single Citizenship; All-India Services; Governor appointed by\nPresident; Centre stronger during emergencies\n• Conclusion = Quasi-Federal with Unitary Bias\nTRICK - “Normal time = Federal frame, Emergency = Centre remote”\nFederal side ko structure se yaad karo: Powers divided + written document + two houses + two governments + harder\namendment.\nUnitary side ko Centre-control se yaad karo: Strong Centre + 356 + Single Citizenship + All-India Services + Governor +\nEmergency power.\nIsliye source ka final label: Quasi-Federal with Unitary Bias.\nEXAM TRAP\nIndia ko source “Quasi-Federal with Unitary Bias” bolta hai - purely federal ya purely unitary nahi.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN - Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Preamble skeleton 3S+DR -> JELF -> Sources A-A-N (America concept, Australia language, Nehru basis) -> Justice SEP / Liberty\n5 -> State meanings Free-Mixed-Equal-People-Elected -> 42nd = SSI, once -> Quotes Key-Dreams-ID-Horoscope-Soul -> Cases\n1960 NO, 1973 YES, 94/95 confirm -> Features 4 buckets Govt-Design-Citizens-Guard -> Federal structure + Unitary Centre =\nQuasi-Federal with Unitary Bias.”\nTop 3 traps: Concept America vs Language Australia | 42nd = only once + SSI | Berubari 1960 NOT part vs Kesavananda 1973\nIS part.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Sources, Parts, Articles & Schedules\nSOURCES, PARTS, ARTICLES & SCHEDULES — MEMORY\nTRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 4 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — sirf country-source matching, key Parts/Articles, 12 Schedules, 7th Schedule lists aur exam traps ko\nfast recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Big Picture — 11 Countries, 22 Parts, 12 Schedules\n• Constitutional Sources = 11 countries / sources\n• Parts = 22\n• Schedules = 12 (Original Constitution mein 8)\n• 7th Schedule = 3 Lists: Union, State, Concurrent\n TRICK — “11–22–12–3” number spine\n💡\nPoore chapter ke 4 master numbers: 11 Sources\n 22 Parts\n 12 Schedules\n 3 Lists.\n→\n→\n→\nRevision start karte hi ye number spine bolo.\n2. Constitutional Sources — Sabse Important Country Matches\n• USA\n Fundamental Rights, Vice-President, Independent Judiciary, Impeachment, Judicial Review\n→\n• UK\n Parliamentary System, Single Citizenship, Cabinet System, Rule of Law, Parliamentary Privileges\n→\n• Ireland\n DPSP, Presidential Election Method, Rajya Sabha nomination\n→\n• Australia\n Concurrent List, Preamble language, Freedom of Trade, Joint Sitting\n→\n• Canada\n Governors appointment, Residuary Powers to Centre, Strong-Centre Federalism\n→\n• Former Soviet Union\n Fundamental Duties, Five-Year Plans, Socio-Economic-Political Justice\n→\n• Germany\n Emergency Powers + Suspension of Fundamental Rights\n→\n• France\n Republic + Liberty, Equality, Fraternity\n→\n• South Africa\n Constitutional Amendment Procedure + Rajya Sabha election\n→\n• Japan\n Procedure Established by Law\n→\n• Govt. of India Act 1935\n Federal System, Judiciary, PSC, Governor's Post, Administrative Details\n→\n TRICK — “R-D-D” core trio\n💡\nRights = USA, DPSP = Ireland, Duties = USSR.\nYe teen sabse direct aur sabse zyada poochhe jaane wale source matches hain.\n TRICK — “A-C-G-S-J” tail chain\n💡\nAustralia = Concurrent/Joint Sitting, Canada = Centre residuary, Germany = Emergency, South Africa = Amendment,\nJapan = Procedure by Law.\nShort line: “Australia Concurrent, Canada Centre, Germany Emergency, South Africa Amendment, Japan Law.”\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nFundamental Rights = USA; Fundamental Duties = USSR; DPSP = Ireland. Teenon ko swap mat karna.\nAmendment Procedure = South Africa, Emergency Powers = Germany.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Sources, Parts, Articles & Schedules\n3. Parts 1–4A — Foundation Block\n• Part 1 (Articles 1–4)\n Union & its Territory\n→\n• Part 2 (5–11)\n Citizenship\n→\n• Part 3 (12–35)\n Fundamental Rights\n→\n• Part 4 (36–51)\n DPSP\n→\n• Part 4A (51A)\n Fundamental Duties\n→\n TRICK — “1 Territory, 2 Citizenship, 3 Rights, 4 Policy, 4A Duty”\n💡\nPart numbers ko subject progression ki tarah bolo.\nMost important anchor: Part 3 = FR, Part 4 = DPSP, Part 4A = Duties.\n4. Key Parts & Articles — Exam Anchors\n• Fundamental Rights\n Articles 12–35, Part 3\n→\n• DPSP\n Articles 36–51, Part 4\n→\n• Fundamental Duties\n Article 51A, Part 4A\n→\n• Panchayats\n Part 9, Articles 243–243O\n→\n• Municipalities\n Part 9A\n→\n• Elections\n Part 15, Articles 324–329(A)\n→\n• Emergency\n Part 18, Articles 352–360\n→\n• Amendment\n Part 20, Article 368\n→\n TRICK — “3–4–4A / 15–18–20” anchor chain\n💡\n3 = Rights, 4 = DPSP, 4A = Duties.\n15 = Elections, 18 = Emergency, 20 = Amendment.\nArticle hooks: 51A Duty, 368 Amendment.\n5. Parts 5–22 — Subject Ladder\n• Part 5\n Union | Part 6\n States | Part 7\n Repealed | Part 8\n Union Territories\n→\n→\n→\n→\n• Part 9\n Panchayats | 9A\n Municipalities | 9B\n Cooperative Societies\n→\n→\n→\n• Part 10\n Scheduled & Tribal Areas | Part 11\n Centre-State Relations\n→\n→\n• Part 12\n Finance/Property/Contracts/Suits | Part 13\n Trade & Commerce\n→\n→\n• Part 14\n Services | 14A\n Tribunals | Part 15\n Elections\n→\n→\n→\n• Part 16\n Special Provisions for Certain Classes | Part 17\n Official Language\n→\n→\n• Part 18\n Emergency | Part 19\n Miscellaneous | Part 20\n Amendment\n→\n→\n→\n• Part 21\n Temporary/Transitional/Special | Part 22\n Short Title/Commencement/Hindi text/Repeals\n→\n→\n TRICK — “5 Union > 6 States > 8 UT > 9 Local > 11 Relations > 18 Emergency > 20 Amendment”\n💡\nSaare 22 Parts ratne ke bajay anchor points yaad karo aur beech ka ladder connect karo.\nLocal-government block = 9, 9A, 9B; emergency-amendment block = 18, 20.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Sources, Parts, Articles & Schedules\n6. 12 Schedules — TEARS OF OLD PM\n• 1st\n Territories\n→\n• 2nd\n Emoluments / Salaries\n→\n• 3rd\n Affirmations / Oaths\n→\n• 4th\n Rajya Sabha seat allocation\n→\n• 5th\n Scheduled Areas & Tribes\n→\n• 6th\n Other Tribal Areas (Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, Mizoram)\n→\n• 7th\n Federal Lists: Union, State, Concurrent\n→\n• 8th\n Official Languages\n→\n• 9th\n Land Reforms\n→\n• 10th\n Defection\n→\n• 11th\n Panchayat subjects\n→\n• 12th\n Municipality subjects\n→\n TRICK — “TEARS OF OLD PM”\n💡\nT = Territories, E = Emoluments, A = Affirmation, R = Rajya Sabha, S = Scheduled Areas,\nO = Other Tribal, F = Federal Lists, O = Official Languages, L = Land Reforms, D = Defection, P = Panchayats, M =\nMunicipalities.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nOriginal Constitution = 8 Schedules; Present = 12. Later added: 9th, 10th, 11th, 12th.\n7. Later-added Schedules — 9, 10, 11, 12\n• 9th Schedule\n 1st Amendment, 1951\n Land Reforms\n→\n→\n• 10th Schedule\n 52nd Amendment, 1985\n Anti-Defection\n→\n→\n• 11th Schedule\n 73rd Amendment, 1992\n 29 Panchayat subjects\n→\n→\n• 12th Schedule\n 74th Amendment, 1992\n 18 Municipality subjects\n→\n→\n TRICK — “1–52–73–74” amendment ladder\n💡\n9th = 1st Amendment, 10th = 52nd, 11th = 73rd, 12th = 74th.\nLast pair easy: 73rd Panchayat, 74th Municipality.\n8. 8th Schedule Languages — 14 Se 22\n• Original languages = 14\n• Current languages = 22\n• 21st Amendment (1967)\n Sindhi\n→\n• 71st Amendment (1992)\n Konkani, Manipuri, Nepali\n→\n• 92nd Amendment (2003)\n Bodo, Dogri, Maithili, Santhali\n→\n TRICK — “1 + 3 + 4 = 8 additions”\n💡\n14 original + 8 added = 22 current.\nAdditions pattern: 21st\n 1 language; 71st\n 3; 92nd\n 4.\n→\n→\n→\nLast 4: Bodo–Dogri–Maithili–Santhali.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCurrent languages = 22, not 14. Last addition in source notes = 92nd Amendment 2003: Bodo, Dogri, Maithili, Santhali.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Sources, Parts, Articles & Schedules\n9. 7th Schedule — 3 Lists\n• Union List examples\n Banking, Defence, Foreign Affairs, Communication, Railways, Inter-State Trade\n→\n• State List examples\n Health, Agriculture, Police, Trade\n→\n• Concurrent List examples\n Forests, Education, Marriage, Succession, Population Control\n→\n• Union List laws\n Parliament\n→\n• State List laws\n State Legislature (normally)\n→\n• Concurrent List\n Both; conflict mein Parliament prevails\n→\n• Residuary Powers\n Centre / Parliament (borrowed from Canada)\n→\n TRICK — “BDFCR = Union; HAPT = State; FEMSP = Concurrent”\n💡\nUnion: Banking–Defence–Foreign Affairs–Communication–Railways.\nState: Health–Agriculture–Police–Trade.\nConcurrent: Forests–Education–Marriage–Succession–Population Control.\n TRICK — Education shift\n💡\nEducation 42nd Amendment 1976 se State List se Concurrent List mein shift hua.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nResiduary Powers = Centre, source = Canada.\nConcurrent conflict = Parliament prevails.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“11 Sources–22 Parts–12 Schedules–3 Lists\n Rights-USA, DPSP-Ireland, Duties-USSR\n Australia-Concurrent, Canada-\n→\n→\nCentre Residuary, Germany-Emergency, South Africa-Amendment, Japan-Procedure by Law\n Part3 FR 12–35, Part4\n→\nDPSP 36–51, Part4A Duty 51A\n Part9 Panchayat, 9A Municipality, 15 Elections, 18 Emergency 352–360, 20\n→\nAmendment 368\n Schedules TEARS OF OLD PM\n later 9th=1st/1951, 10th=52nd/1985, 11th=73rd/29 subjects,\n→\n→\n12th=74th/18 subjects\n 8th language 14\n22, additions 1+3+4\n 7th Lists: Union/State/Concurrent, Residuary Centre.”\n→\n→\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\nRights-USA / DPSP-Ireland / Duties-USSR | Original 8 Schedules vs Current 12 | Residuary Powers-Centre from\nCanada.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Union & Its Territories\nUNION AND ITS TERRITORIES — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 5 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part 1, Articles 1–4, reorganization committees, state-formation years, Art. 371 series aur case-law\ntraps ko fast recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Part 1 — Articles 1–4 Ka Master Map\n• Article 1\n Name & Territory of India\n→\n• Article 2\n New State ki Admission / Establishment\n→\n• Article 3\n State ki Area / Name / Boundary ka Reorganization\n→\n• Article 4\n Article 2 & 3 wale changes ke liye Article 368 amendment nahi; Simple Majority enough\n→\n TRICK — “1 Naam, 2 Naya, 3 Naksha, 4 No-368”\n💡\nArticle 1 = Naam/Territory.\nArticle 2 = Naya state admit/establish.\nArticle 3 = Naksha badlo — area/name/boundary.\nArticle 4 = No Article 368; simple majority.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArticle 3 ke under boundaries/name/area change = Simple Majority. Article 368 amendment ki zaroorat nahi.\n2. Article 1 — Indestructible Union\n• India ki territory = States + Union Territories + acquired territories\n• States ko secede karne ka right nahi\n• India = Indestructible Union of Destructible States\n TRICK — “Union Pakka, States Ka Naksha Badal Sakta”\n💡\nUnion ko tod nahi sakte, lekin states ki boundaries/name/area badal sakte ho.\nIsliye phrase yaad rakho: Indestructible Union, Destructible States.\n3. Article 2 vs Article 3 — Admit vs Alter\n• Article 2\n Union mein naye / existing outside states ko admit ya establish karna; example: Sikkim\n→\n• Article 3\n Existing state ki area, boundary, name change; states merge/split ho sakte hain\n→\n• Jammu & Kashmir Reorganization Act 2019\n Article 3 example\n→\n TRICK — “2 = Entry Gate, 3 = Editing Table”\n💡\nArticle 2 ko Union ka entry gate samjho — state ko andar lao.\nArticle 3 ko map-editor samjho — andar ke states ka shape/name/area badlo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nState Legislature ka consent binding nahi. Bill Parliament mein introduce hone se pehle President ki prior approval chahiye;\nstate ki rai binding nahi hai.\n4. Berubari vs Maganbhai — Opposite Case Trap\n• Berubari Case (1960)\n Indian territory doosre country ko transfer = Constitutional Amendment required\n→\n• Maganbhai Patel v. Union of India (1969)\n International boundary dispute settlement = Constitutional Amendment\n→\nnot required; executive can settle\n• 100th Amendment (2015)\n India-Bangladesh Land Boundary Agreement\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Union & Its Territories\n TRICK — “Transfer = Berubari = Amendment; Boundary = Maganbhai = No Amendment”\n💡\nT-B-A: Territory Transfer\n Berubari\n Amendment.\n→\n→\nB-M-N: Boundary dispute\n Maganbhai\n No amendment.\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nBerubari aur Maganbhai ko ulta mat karna — source notes mein dono opposite situations ke liye diye gaye hain.\n5. Reorganization Committees — Dhar, JVP, Fazl Ali\n• SK Dhar Committee (June 1948)\n Linguistic basis reject; administrative convenience\n→\n• JVP Committee (Dec 1948)\n Linguistic basis reject\n→\n• JVP = Jawaharlal Nehru + Vallabhbhai Patel + Pattabhi Sitaramayya\n• Fazl Ali Commission (Dec 1953)\n Linguistic basis accept; One Language One State reject\n→\n• Fazl Ali members\n K.M. Panikkar + H.N. Kunzru\n→\n• Fazl Ali recommendation\n 16 States + 3 UTs\n→\n TRICK — “Dhar NO, JVP NO, Fazl YES”\n💡\nSequence: Dhar NO\n JVP NO\n Fazl Ali YES linguistic basis.\n→\n→\nFazl Ali ka extra trap: linguistic basis YES, lekin ‘One Language One State’ NO.\n TRICK — “FA-PK” members\n💡\nFazl Ali + Panikkar + Kunzru = FA-PK.\nChairman Fazl Ali; members K.M. Panikkar & H.N. Kunzru.\n6. 1956 Reorganization + First Linguistic State\n• 1950 mein states = 4 categories A, B, C, D\n• State Reorganization Act = 1956\n• 7th Constitutional Amendment = 1956\n• Result = 14 States + 6 UTs\n• First linguistic state = Andhra Pradesh (1953), carved from Madras State\n• Demand led by Potti Sriramulu\n TRICK — “1953 Andhra First, 1956 Big Reorganization”\n💡\nAndhra Pradesh pehle 1953 mein bana; Fazl Ali report/reorganization ke baad nahi.\n1956 ka final count: 14 States + 6 UTs.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nFirst linguistic state = Andhra Pradesh (1953). Ye Fazl Ali Commission ke baad nahi, usse pehle bana.\n7. States Formed After 1950 — Main Chronology\n• 1953\n Andhra Pradesh\n→\n• 1960\n Maharashtra + Gujarat\n→\n• 1963\n Nagaland\n→\n• 1966\n Haryana\n→\n• 1971\n Himachal Pradesh\n→\n• 1972\n Meghalaya + Manipur + Tripura\n→\n• 1975\n Sikkim\n→\n• 1987\n Goa + Arunachal Pradesh + Mizoram\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Union & Its Territories\n TRICK — “AaM GuNaH Hi Megh ManTra Si GArMi de sakta hai”\n💡\nAa = Andhra, M = Maharashtra, Gu = Gujarat, Na = Nagaland, H = Haryana, Hi = Himachal,\nMegh = Meghalaya, Man = Manipur, Tra = Tripura, Si = Sikkim, G = Goa, Ar = Arunachal, Mi = Mizoram.\n TRICK — Same-year cluster trick\n💡\n1960 = M-G (Maharashtra + Gujarat).\n1972 = M-M-T (Meghalaya + Manipur + Tripura).\n1987 = G-A-M (Goa + Arunachal + Mizoram).\n8. Sikkim — 35\n 36\n 371F\n→\n→\n• 35th Amendment 1974\n Associated State Status\n→\n• 36th Amendment 1975\n Full Statehood; 22nd State\n→\n• Special provision\n Article 371F\n→\n TRICK — “35 Associate, 36 State, F = Sikkim”\n💡\n35th = Associated State; next 36th = Full Statehood.\n371F ko Sikkim ke final special tag ki tarah yaad karo.\n9. Article 371 Series — State Matching\n• 371\n Maharashtra & Gujarat\n→\n• 371A\n Nagaland\n→\n• 371B\n Assam\n→\n• 371C\n Manipur\n→\n• 371D\n Andhra Pradesh & Telangana\n→\n• 371F\n Sikkim\n→\n• 371G\n Mizoram\n→\n• 371H\n Arunachal Pradesh\n→\n• 371I\n Goa\n→\n• 371J\n Karnataka (98th Amendment, 2012)\n→\n TRICK — “A-N, B-A, C-M, D-AP/T, F-S, G-M, H-A, I-G, J-K”\n💡\nLetter-pair chain bolo: A-Nagaland, B-Assam, C-Manipur, D-Andhra/Telangana, F-Sikkim, G-Mizoram, H-Arunachal, I-Goa, J-\nKarnataka.\nPlain 371 = Maharashtra + Gujarat.\n TRICK — “J = Karnataka + 98th”\n💡\n371J ko Karnataka ke saath fix karo; source notes mein 98th Amendment 2012 bhi linked hai.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part1 Art1–4\n 1 Naam/Territory, 2 Admission, 3 Reorganization, 4 No-368/Simple Majority\n India Indestructible Union\n\n→\n→\n→\nBerubari Transfer=Amendment, Maganbhai Boundary=No Amendment\n Dhar NO, JVP NO, Fazl YES + Panikkar/Kunzru\n\n→\n→\nAndhra 1953 first linguistic\n 1956 = 14 States+6 UTs\n State years: AaM GuNaH Hi Megh ManTra Si GArMi\n Sikkim 35\n→\n→\n→\nAssociate, 36 State, 371F\n 371 chain: 371 MG, A-Naga, B-Assam, C-Manipur, D-AP/Telangana, F-Sikkim, G-Mizoram, H-\n→\nArunachal, I-Goa, J-Karnataka.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nArt.3 = Simple Majority/No Art.368 | Andhra Pradesh 1953 = first linguistic state | Berubari transfer needs\namendment vs Maganbhai boundary dispute does not.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Citizenship\nCITIZENSHIP — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 6 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Articles 5–11, Citizenship Act 1955, acquire/lose methods, CAA 2019, OCI–NRI aur exam traps ko\nfast recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Part II + Articles 5–11 — Full Article Chain\n• Part II\n Articles 5–11\n→\n• Single Citizenship\n borrowed from UK\n→\n• Articles 5–8\n Citizenship before 26 Jan 1950\n→\n• Articles 9–11\n Post-Constitution continuation / Parliament law-making framework\n→\n TRICK — “5 Start, 6 Come, 7 Go, 8 Abroad, 9 Stop, 10 Continue, 11 Law”\n💡\n5 = Citizenship at commencement.\n6 = Pakistan\n India migration.\n→\n7 = India\n Pakistan migration.\n→\n8 = Indian origin living abroad.\n9 = Termination, 10 = Continuance, 11 = Parliament makes citizenship law.\n2. Article 6 vs 7 — Direction + Date Trap\n• Article 6\n Pakistan\n India; before 19 July 1948\n→\n→\n• Article 7\n India\n Pakistan; after 1 March 1947\n→\n→\n TRICK — “6 COMES IN, 7 GOES OUT”\n💡\n6 = Pakistan se India aaya (Come In)\n before 19 July 1948.\n→\n7 = India se Pakistan gaya (Go Out)\n after 1 March 1947.\n→\nDirection pehle bolo, phir date.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArticle 6 aur 7 mein direction aur cutoff dono alag hain: 6 = Pak\nIndia / before 19 Jul 1948; 7 = India\nPak / after 1 Mar 1947.\n→\n→\n3. Citizenship Act 1955 — 6 Amendment Years\n• Act enforced\n 30 December 1955\n→\n• 1986\n 1st amendment\n→\n• 1992\n 2nd\n→\n• 2003\n 3rd\n→\n• 2005\n 4th; OCI introduced\n→\n• 2015\n 5th\n→\n• 2019\n 6th; CAA 2019\n→\n TRICK — “86–92–03–05–15–19” year ladder\n💡\nSix amendment years ko pair mein bolo: 86-92 | 03-05 | 15-19.\nDo anchor years: 2005 = OCI, 2019 = CAA.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Citizenship\n4. Citizenship Acquire 5, Lose 3\n5 Ways to Acquire\n• Birth\n• Descent\n• Registration\n 7 years residence\n→\n• Naturalisation\n 12 years residence\n→\n• Incorporation of Territory\n TRICK — “B-D-R-N-I”\n💡\nBirth\n Descent\n Registration\n Naturalisation\n Incorporation.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nNumber hook: Registration = 7, Naturalisation = 12.\n3 Ways to Lose\n• Renunciation\n self-given up\n→\n• Deprivation\n Government deprives\n→\n• Termination\n voluntarily acquiring another country's citizenship\n→\n TRICK — “R-D-T = Renounce, Deprive, Terminate”\n💡\nR = khud chhoda, D = government ne chhin liya, T = doosri citizenship lene se terminate.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nRegistration = 7 years; Naturalisation = 12 years. CAA beneficiaries ke liye source notes naturalisation ko 6 years batate hain.\n5. CAA 2019 — 3 Countries + 6 Communities + 3 Dates\n• 3 countries\n Bangladesh, Afghanistan, Pakistan\n→\n• Arrival cutoff\n on or before 31 December 2014\n→\n• 6 communities\n Hindu, Sikh, Parsi, Buddhist, Christian, Jain\n→\n• Naturalisation\n 12 years reduced to 6 years\n→\n• Implemented across country from\n 11 March 2024\n→\n• Muslims from these 3 countries\n not covered\n→\n• Sri Lanka, Myanmar etc.\n not covered\n→\n TRICK — “BAP + Hi-Si-Par-Bu-Ch-Ja”\n💡\nCountries: B-A-P = Bangladesh, Afghanistan, Pakistan.\nCommunities rhythm: Hindu–Sikh–Parsi–Buddhist–Christian–Jain.\nDate chain: 31 Dec 2014 cutoff\n CAA 2019\n 11 Mar 2024 implementation.\n→\n→\n TRICK — “12\n 6 = Half”\n💡\n→\nCAA beneficiaries ke liye source sheet mein naturalisation period 12 se 6 years diya hai — half ho gaya.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCAA = sirf 3 countries + 6 listed non-Muslim communities. Sri Lanka/Myanmar included nahi; Muslims listed beneficiaries\nmein nahi.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Citizenship\n6. OCI — 2005 Se, Visa Hai; Political Rights Nahi\n• OCI initiated\n 2005 (4th amendment to Citizenship Act)\n→\n• Lifetime visa-free travel to India\n• Educational & cultural benefits\n• Can own non-agricultural property\n• No voting rights\n• Cannot contest elections\n• Cannot hold constitutional posts\n• No agricultural land ownership\n TRICK — “OCI = Overseas Card, not Indian Citizen”\n💡\nOCI ko travel/education/culture benefits milte hain, political citizenship rights nahi.\nEasy contrast: Visa-free YES; Vote NO.\n7. NRI vs OCI — Citizen vs Foreign National\n• NRI = Indian citizen living abroad\n• NRI\n Indian passport + voting rights + full political rights\n→\n• OCI = foreign national; not Indian citizen\n• OCI\n foreign passport + lifetime visa-free entry; no political rights\n→\n TRICK — “NRI = Nation's Real Indian; OCI = Overseas Card Identity”\n💡\nNRI ko pehle 'Indian Citizen' label karo.\nOCI ko pehle 'Foreign National' label karo.\nPhir baaki sab automatically yaad: NRI vote YES; OCI vote NO.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nOCI holder ko lifetime visa-free entry milti hai, lekin voting/election/constitutional post rights nahi. NRI = Indian citizen with\nfull citizenship rights.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part II Art 5–11\n 5 Start, 6 Pak\nIndia, 7 India\nPak, 8 Abroad, 9 Stop, 10 Continue, 11 Parliament Law\n 6 before 19-Jul-48,\n→\n→\n→\n→\n7 after 1-Mar-47\n Citizenship Act 1955; amendments 86-92-03-05-15-19 (05 OCI, 19 CAA)\n Acquire B-D-R-N-I; Registration7,\n→\n→\nNaturalisation12\n Lose R-D-T\n CAA BAP + Hindu-Sikh-Parsi-Buddhist-Christian-Jain; cutoff 31-Dec-2014; 12\n6; 11-Mar-2024\n→\n→\n→\n\n OCI 2005 = visa/education/culture but no politics\n NRI = Indian citizen, OCI = foreign national.”\n→\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\nArt.6 Pak\nIndia vs Art.7 India\nPak dates | CAA = 3 countries + 6 communities | NRI = Indian citizen, OCI = foreign\n→\n→\nnational with no political rights.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Fundamental Rights\nFUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 7 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part III, 6 Fundamental Rights, article map, Equality/Freedom blocks, writs, emergency exceptions\naur exam traps ko fast recall format mein diya gaya hai.\n1. FR Skeleton — Part III, 12–35, 7 Se 6\n• Fundamental Rights = Part III, Articles 12–35\n• Justiciable = Court mein enforce kiye ja sakte hain\n• Source = US Constitution\n• Originally 7 Fundamental Rights; now 6\n• Right to Property removed by 44th Amendment, 1978\n now Legal Right under Article 300A\n→\n• Article 32 = 'Heart & Soul of the Constitution' — Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n TRICK — “III–12/35–7\n6–300A–32 Heart”\n💡\n→\nChapter ka number spine: Part III\n Art 12–35\n 7 se 6 Rights\n Property = 300A\n Art 32 = Heart & Soul.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n44th Amendment 1978 ko Property removal ke saath chipka lo.\n2. 6 Fundamental Rights — Full Range Trick\n• Right to Equality\n Articles 14–18\n→\n• Right to Freedom\n 19–22\n→\n• Right against Exploitation\n 23–24\n→\n• Freedom of Religion\n 25–28\n→\n• Cultural & Educational Rights\n 29–30\n→\n• Constitutional Remedies\n Article 32\n→\n TRICK — “EVERY FOOL EATS RIPE CUSTARD REGULARLY”\n💡\nEvery = Equality, Fool = Freedom, Eats = Exploitation, Ripe = Religion, Custard = Cultural/Educational, Regularly = Remedies.\nArticle ranges naturally climb: 14–18\n 19–22\n 23–24\n 25–28\n 29–30\n 32.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n3. Citizens Only vs Everyone — High-Frequency Trap\n• Only Indian Citizens\n Articles 15, 16, 19, 29, 30\n→\n• Indians + Foreigners both\n Articles 14, 20, 21, 21A, 22–28\n→\n• Article 19 freedoms = citizens only\n• Article 21 Right to Life = available to all\n TRICK — “15-16-19-29-30 = Citizen Club”\n💡\nCitizen-only cluster ko ek number string bana ke bolo: 15, 16, 19, 29, 30.\nSpecial contrast: 19 = citizen only; 21 = everyone.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArt. 19 foreigners ko available nahi; Art. 21 sabke liye available hai.\n4. Right to Equality — Articles 14–18\n• Art 14\n Equality before Law + Equal Protection of Law\n→\n• Art 15\n No discrimination\n→\n• Art 16\n Equal opportunity in public employment\n→\n• Art 17\n Abolition of Untouchability\n→\n• Art 18\n Abolition of Titles\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Fundamental Rights\n TRICK — “DOUBT” for 15–18\n💡\nD = Discrimination (15), O = Opportunity (16), U = Untouchability (17), T = Titles (18).\nArt 14 ko base Equality article ke roop mein alag anchor karo.\nReservation / Equality Hooks\n• 15(3)\n Special provisions for Women & Children\n→\n• 15(4)\n SC/ST/OBC reservation in educational institutions\n→\n• 15(5)\n Reservation in private educational institutions\n→\n• 15(6)\n EWS reservation in educational institutions\n→\n• 16(4)\n SC/ST/OBC reservation in jobs\n→\n• 16(6)\n EWS jobs\n→\n• Art 17 linked in notes with Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955\n TRICK — “15 = Education/Social, 16 = Employment”\n💡\n15 ke reservation clauses ko education/social side samjho; 16 ko public employment/jobs side.\nEWS = 15(6) education + 16(6) jobs.\n5. Right to Freedom — 19, 20, 21, 21A, 22\nArticle 19 — 6 Freedoms\n• Speech & Expression\n• Peaceful Assembly\n• Associations / Unions\n• Move Freely throughout India\n• Reside Anywhere in India\n• Practice Any Profession / Occupation\n TRICK — “S-A-A-M-R-P”\n💡\nSpeech\n Assembly\n Association\n Move\n Reside\n Profession.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nArt. 19 mein originally 7 freedoms thi; property freedom remove hone ke baad 6.\nArticles 20–22\n• Art 20\n No ex-post-facto punishment; No Double Jeopardy; No Self-incrimination\n→\n• Art 21\n Right to Life & Personal Liberty; notes include privacy, sleep, electricity, travel abroad, education, dignity,\n→\nlivelihood\n• Art 21A\n Free & Compulsory Education age 6–14; 86th Amendment 2002; RTE Act 2009, enforced 2010\n→\n• Art 22\n Protection against Arrest & Detention; ordinary arrest safeguards include reason, lawyer, magistrate within\n→\n24 hours\n• Preventive Detention\n maximum 3 months; extension with Advisory Board approval\n→\n TRICK — “20 = Triple NO; 21 = LIFE; 21A = 6–14 School; 22 = Arrest”\n💡\nArt 20 ka 3-NO pack: no ex-post-facto, no double jeopardy, no self-incrimination.\n21A ko age 6–14 se lock karo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPreventive detention mein 24-hour magistrate rule apply nahi hota; source notes is safeguard ko ordinary arrest ke liye\nbatate hain.\nArt. 20 & 21 Emergency mein bhi suspend nahi hote.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Fundamental Rights\n6. Exploitation + Religion + Minority Rights\n• Art 23\n Human trafficking, forced labour, begar prohibited\n→\n• Art 24\n Below 14 children: factories/mines/hazardous work prohibited\n→\n• Art 25\n Conscience; profess, practice & propagate religion\n→\n• Art 26\n Manage religious affairs\n→\n• Art 27\n No taxation for religious promotion\n→\n• Art 28\n Religious instruction in educational institutions\n→\n• Art 29\n Minority language/script/culture protection\n→\n• Art 30\n Minorities can establish & administer educational institutions\n→\n TRICK — “23 Begar, 24 Bachcha; 25–28 Religion; 29–30 Minority”\n💡\n23–24 ko exploitation pair samjho.\n25–28 pure religion block hai.\n29–30 minority cultural/education block hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArt. 24 = hazardous/factory/mine child labour ban; notes ise Constitution mein complete child-labour ban nahi batate.\nArt. 25 ka 'propagate' = forceful conversion ka right nahi.\n7. Article 32 vs 226 — Supreme Court vs High Court\n• Art 32\n Supreme Court; only Fundamental Rights\n→\n• Art 226\n High Courts; Fundamental Rights + Legal Rights\n→\n• High Court jurisdiction wider\n TRICK — “32 = SC/FR only; 226 = HC/FR + Legal”\n💡\n32 ko Supreme Court ka Fundamental-Rights emergency door samjho.\n226 ka scope wider — FR ke saath legal rights bhi.\n8. 5 Writs — Meaning + Purpose\n• Habeas Corpus\n illegal detention; person ko court ke saamne pesh karo\n→\n• Mandamus\n public authority ko duty perform karne ka command\n→\n• Quo-Warranto\n public office kis authority se hold kiya?\n→\n• Certiorari\n lower court order ko quash\n→\n• Prohibition\n lower court ko jurisdiction exceed karne se stop\n→\n TRICK — “Ha-Ma-Qu-Ce-Pro”\n💡\nHabeas\n Mandamus\n Quo-Warranto\n Certiorari\n Prohibition.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nPurpose code: Body\n Command\n Authority\n Quash\n Stop.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n TRICK — “C = Completed, P = Pending”\n💡\nCertiorari = case decided/completed ke baad quash.\nProhibition = case pending rehte prevent/stop.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCertiorari aur Prohibition ka sabse important distinction: decided-after vs pending-before.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Fundamental Rights\n9. Property + Emergency + Articles 33–35\n• Right to Property\n Legal Right, Article 300A; FR nahi\n→\n• 44th Amendment 1978\n Property FR removed\n→\n• Art 33\n Parliament may restrict rights of Armed Forces\n→\n• Art 34\n Restrictions during Martial Law\n→\n• Art 35\n Parliament's power to legislate for enforcement of certain FRs\n→\n• FR suspension during Emergency\n Articles 358 & 359\n→\n• Art 20 & 21\n never suspended even during Emergency\n→\n• FR amendable, but Basic Structure cannot be destroyed\n TRICK — “33 Army, 34 Martial, 35 Law”\n💡\n33 = Armed Forces restrictions, 34 = Martial Law, 35 = Parliament makes enforcement laws.\nEmergency exception ko red-circle karo: 20 & 21 safe.\n10. Equality Cases — Number Hooks\n• Balaji v. State of Mysore (1963)\n Reservation cap introduced\n→\n• Mandal Commission (1979)\n 2nd Backward Class Commission; OBC reservation\n→\n• Indra Sawhney (1992)\n notes link 50% reservation cap / promotion issue\n→\n• Kesavananda Bharati (1973)\n Basic Structure doctrine\n→\n• EWS 10%\n 103rd Amendment, 2019\n→\n TRICK — “63 Balaji, 73 Keshavananda, 79 Mandal, 92 Indra, 2019 EWS”\n💡\nCase/commission years ko timeline ki tarah yaad karo: 63\n 73\n 79\n 92\n 2019.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nTopic hooks: reservation cap / basic structure / OBC commission / reservation / EWS.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part III 12–35, US source, Justiciable, 7\n6 after 44th/1978; Property=300A\n 6 Rights: Every Fool Eats Ripe Custard Regularly\n→\n→\n Citizen club 15-16-19-29-30\n Equality 14–18: DOUBT (15 Discrimination,16 Opportunity,17 Untouchability,18 Titles)\n\n→\n→\n→\nFreedom: 19 SAAMRP; 20 Triple-NO; 21 Life; 21A 6–14/86th; 22 Arrest/Detention\n 23 Begar,24 Child hazardous work\n 25–28\n→\n→\n\nReligion\n 29–30 Minority\n 32 SC only FR vs 226 HC FR+Legal\n Writs Ha-Ma-Qu-Ce-Pro; C=Completed, P=Pending\n 33\n→\n→\n→\n→\nArmed,34 Martial,35 Parliament Law\n Emergency: 20 & 21 safe.”\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\nArt.19 citizens only vs Art.21 everyone | Art.20 & 21 never suspended in Emergency | Certiorari = decided-after,\nProhibition = pending-before.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Directive Principles of State Policy\nDIRECTIVE PRINCIPLES OF STATE POLICY — MEMORY\nTRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 8 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part IV, Articles 36–51, Socialist/Gandhian/Liberal classification, amendments, FR-vs-DPSP cases\naur high-confusion article pairs ko fast recall format mein diya gaya hai.\n1. DPSP Skeleton — Part IV, 36–51, Irish, Non-Justiciable\n• DPSP = Part IV, Articles 36–51\n• Source = Irish Constitution, specifically Article 45\n• Nature = Non-Justiciable; court mein directly enforce nahi kar sakte\n• Goal = Social & Economic Democracy\n• Article 36 = 'State' definition, same as Article 12\n• Article 37 = Not enforceable, but fundamental in governance\n TRICK — “IV–36/51–Irish–No Court–Governance”\n💡\nChapter ka master spine: Part IV\n Art 36–51\n Irish source\n Non-Justiciable\n Fundamental in Governance.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n36 = State definition; 37 = court nahi, governance mein fundamental.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nDPSP enforceable nahi hain, lekin Article 37 ke according governance mein fundamental hain.\n2. Scholar Quotes — Naam Ko Quote Se Lock Karo\n• Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n 'Novel Features of Indian Constitution'\n→\n• Granville Austin\n 'Soul of the Constitution'\n→\n• Prof. K.T. Shah\n 'Cheque payable at the convenience of the bank'\n→\n• K.C. Wheare\n 'Declaration of goals and aspirations'\n→\n• Sir Ivor Jennings\n 'Pious aspiration'\n→\n TRICK — “A-Novel, Austin-Soul, Shah-Cheque, Wheare-Goals, Jennings-Pious”\n💡\nAmbedkar = Novel Features; Granville Austin = Soul.\nShah = Cheque; Wheare = Goals; Jennings = Pious aspiration.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nAmbedkar ne Article 32/FR ko 'Heart & Soul' kaha, DPSP ko 'Novel Features'. DPSP ka 'Soul of Constitution' = Granville Austin.\n3. DPSP Classification — 3 Buckets\nSocialist Principles\n• 38\n Social order / welfare; 38(2) minimize income inequalities\n→\n• 39\n Livelihood, resources, wealth distribution, equal pay, worker/child protection\n→\n• 39A\n Free Legal Aid\n→\n• 41\n Right to work, education, public assistance\n→\n• 42\n Just & humane work conditions + maternity relief\n→\n• 43\n Living wage for workers (Common)\n→\n• 43A\n Workers' participation in industrial management\n→\n• 47\n Nutrition & standard of living (Common)\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Directive Principles of State Policy\n TRICK — “38 Welfare, 39 Policy, 39A Aid, 41 Work, 42 Work-conditions, 43 Wage, 47 Nutrition”\n💡\nSocialist block ko welfare-worker-equality engine samjho.\nKey anchors: 39A Legal Aid, 41 Work, 42 Maternity, 43 Wage, 47 Nutrition.\nGandhian Principles\n• 40\n Village Panchayats / Gram Swaraj\n→\n• 43\n Living wage (Common)\n→\n• 43B\n Cooperative Societies\n→\n• 46\n SC/ST & weaker sections\n→\n• 47\n Nutrition & standard of living (Common)\n→\n• 48\n Agriculture & animal husbandry\n→\n TRICK — “P-W-C-W-N-A” Gandhian chain\n💡\nPanchayat\n Wage\n Cooperative\n Weaker Sections\n Nutrition\n Agriculture.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nGandhian picture = village, cooperative, weaker sections, agriculture.\nLiberal-Intellectual Principles\n• 44\n Uniform Civil Code (UCC)\n→\n• 45\n Early childhood care & education below 6 years\n→\n• 48\n Agriculture & animal husbandry on modern lines\n→\n• 48A\n Environment protection\n→\n• 49\n Monuments of national importance\n→\n• 50\n Separation of Judiciary from Executive\n→\n• 51\n International peace & security\n→\n TRICK — “44 UCC, 45 Child, 48A Environment, 49 Monument, 50 Judiciary, 51 Peace”\n💡\n44–51 ke end block ko rights/environment/institutions/peace chain samjho.\nMost direct anchors: 44 UCC, 48A Environment, 50 Judiciary separation, 51 Peace.\n4. Common DPSPs — Sirf 2 Articles\n• Article 43 = Living wage for workers\n• Article 47 = Nutrition & standard of living\n• Source notes classify both as Socialist + Gandhian common articles\n TRICK — “43 Wage, 47 Plate”\n💡\n43 = Wage; 47 = Plate/Nutrition.\nDono Common DPSPs — Socialist bhi, Gandhian bhi.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCommon articles = 43 & 47. Ye classification question mein frequently trap bante hain.\n5. Article 39(a–f) — Six Clauses Ka Memory Code\n• 39(a)\n Adequate livelihood\n→\n• 39(b)\n Material resources for common good\n→\n• 39(c)\n Avoid concentration of wealth\n→\n• 39(d)\n Equal pay for equal work\n→\n• 39(e)\n Protect workers' health & children's rights\n→\n• 39(f)\n Children grow with freedom & dignity\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Directive Principles of State Policy\n TRICK — “L-M-W-P-H-C”\n💡\nLivelihood\n Material resources\n Wealth concentration\n Pay equal\n Health\n Children.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n39(d) = Equal Pay; 39(f) later amendment list mein bhi yaad aayega.\n6. Amendments — 42, 44, 86, 97\n• 42nd Amendment\n 4 DPSP additions: 39(f), 39A, 43A, 48A\n→\n• 44th Amendment\n Article 38(2): minimize income inequalities\n→\n• 86th Amendment, 2002\n Article 45 modified: now below 6 years childhood care\n→\n• 6–14 years education shifted to Article 21A (Fundamental Right)\n• 97th Amendment\n Article 43B: promotion of cooperative societies\n→\n TRICK — “42 = FOUR adds”\n💡\n42nd ko 4 additions ke saath lock karo: 39(f), 39A, 43A, 48A.\nA-suffix pattern dekho: 39A, 43A, 48A + 39(f).\n TRICK — “44\n38(2), 86\n45, 97\n43B”\n💡\n→\n→\n→\n44th = inequality clause 38(2).\n86th = Article 45 child below 6; 6–14 goes to 21A.\n97th = Cooperative = 43B.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n86th Amendment ke baad Article 45 = below 6 years; 6–14 education = Article 21A (FR).\n7. FR vs DPSP — Part III vs Part IV\n• FR\n Part III, Articles 12–35, Justiciable\n→\n• DPSP\n Part IV, Articles 36–51, Non-Justiciable\n→\n• FR\n Individual-centric; Democratic & Civil Rights\n→\n• DPSP\n Group/Society-centric; Social & Economic Democracy\n→\n• FR\n Restrictions / negative nature\n→\n• DPSP\n Positive duty / directions to State\n→\n TRICK — “III = Individual + Court; IV = Society + Direction”\n💡\nPart III ko individual enforceable rights samjho.\nPart IV ko State ke positive social-economic directions samjho.\n8. FR vs DPSP Cases — Evolution Chain\n• 1951 Champakam Dorairajan\n FR above DPSP\n→\n• 1967 Golaknath\n Parliament cannot curtail FRs to implement DPSPs\n→\n• 1973 Keshavananda Bharati\n Basic Structure Doctrine\n→\n• 1980 Minerva Mills\n FR & DPSP complementary and equal; balance required\n→\n TRICK — “C-G-K-M = Upar\n Protect\n Basic\n Balance”\n💡\n→\n→\n→\nChampakam = FR upar.\nGolaknath = FR protect.\nKeshavananda = Basic Structure.\nMinerva Mills = Balance / both equal.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nFinal balance point in source notes = Minerva Mills (1980): FR and DPSP complementary and equal importance.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Directive Principles of State Policy\n9. DPSP Implemented Through Laws — Real-World Hooks\n• Article 41 Right to Work\n MNREGA Act, 2005\n→\n• Right to Education\n RTE Act, 2009; enforced 2010\n→\n• Maternity / Women\n Maternity Benefit Act, 1961\n→\n• Environment\n Wildlife Protection Act, 1972 + Biological Diversity Act, 2002\n→\n• Democratic Decentralization\n 73rd & 74th Amendments + PESA Act, 1996\n→\n• 73rd = Panchayati Raj (Rural), 74th = Nagar Palikas (Urban); passed 1992, effective 1993\n TRICK — “41 = Work = MNREGA”\n💡\nMNREGA ko Article 41 se direct connect karo — DPSP implementation ka clean exam example.\n73 Rural / 74 Urban ko pair mein revise karo.\n10. High-Yield Article Flash Chain\n• 36 State definition | 37 Governance fundamental\n• 39A Legal Aid | 40 Panchayat | 41 Work | 42 Maternity\n• 43 Wage | 43A Worker participation | 43B Cooperative\n• 44 UCC | 45 Child below 6 | 46 Weaker sections | 47 Nutrition\n• 48 Agriculture | 48A Environment | 49 Monuments | 50 Judiciary separation | 51 Peace\n TRICK — “39A Aid\n 40 Village\n 41 Work\n 42 Maternity\n 43 Wage\n 44 UCC\n 45 Child\n 48A\n💡\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nEnvironment\n 50 Judiciary\n 51 Peace”\n→\n→\nYe anchor chain poore Article 36–51 map ko quickly reconstruct karne mein help karega.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part IV 36–51\n Irish Art45\n Non-Justiciable but Art37 fundamental in governance\n Quotes: Ambedkar-Novel, Austin-Soul\n→\n→\n→\n\n 3 categories: Socialist welfare/workers, Gandhian village/cooperative/agri, Liberal UCC/environment/judiciary/peace\n\n→\n→\nCommon 43 Wage + 47 Nutrition\n 39 clauses L-M-W-P-H-C\n Amendments: 42=4 adds (39f,39A,43A,48A), 44=38(2), 86=45\n→\n→\nbelow6 + 21A for 6–14, 97=43B\n Cases CGKM: Champakam FR>DPSP, Golaknath protect FR, Keshavananda Basic Structure,\n→\nMinerva Balance\n Implementation: 41-MNREGA, RTE, Maternity, Environment, 73 Rural/74 Urban.”\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\nSource = Irish Constitution | Common DPSPs = 43 & 47 | 86th Amendment: Art.45 below 6, while 6–14 moved to Art.21A.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Fundamental Duties\nFUNDAMENTAL DUTIES — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 9 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part IV-A, Article 51A, 11 Duties, 42nd/86th Amendments, Swaran Singh & Verma Committees aur\nFR–DPSP–FD traps ko fast recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — IV-A, 51A, 10\n11, USSR\n→\n• Fundamental Duties = Part IV-A, Article 51A\n• Added by 42nd Amendment, 1976\n• Based on Swaran Singh Committee recommendations\n• Source = USSR (Former Soviet Union) Constitution\n• Originally 10 Duties; currently 11\n• 11th Duty added by 86th Amendment, 2002\n• Nature = Non-Justiciable\n• Applicable only to Indian Citizens, not foreigners\n TRICK — “IV-A\n 51A\n 42\n 10\n 86\n 11\n USSR”\n💡\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nNumber chain bolo: Part IV-A, Article 51A, 42nd Amendment se 10 duties, 86th Amendment se 11th duty.\nSource ko end mein lock karo: USSR.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPart IV = DPSP; Part IV-A = Fundamental Duties. Dono ko swap mat karna.\n2. 11 Duties — Main Mnemonic: SCREAM VAN LIP\n• S\n Abide by Constitution; respect ideals, institutions, National Flag & Anthem — 51A(a)\n→\n• C\n Cherish noble ideals of national freedom struggle — 51A(b)\n→\n• R\n Uphold sovereignty, unity & integrity of India — 51A(c)\n→\n• E\n Defend the country & render national service when called — 51A(d)\n→\n• A\n Promote harmony & common brotherhood — 51A(e)\n→\n• M\n Preserve rich heritage of composite culture — 51A(f)\n→\n• V\n Protect & improve natural environment — 51A(g)\n→\n• A\n Develop scientific temper, humanism, inquiry & reform — 51A(h)\n→\n• N\n Safeguard public property & abjure violence — 51A(i)\n→\n• L\n Strive towards excellence — 51A(j)\n→\n• I\n Provide education opportunity to child/ward aged 6–14 — 51A(k)\n→\n TRICK — “SCREAM VAN LIP”\n💡\nS-C-R-E-A-M | V-A-N | L-I — isi order mein Article 51A(a) se 51A(k) tak duties chalte hain.\nLast I ko 'Instruction/Education' se yaad karo — 6–14 years.\n3. 51A(a) to 51A(k) — Letter-to-Article Chain\n• 51A(a) Constitution / Flag / Anthem\n• 51A(b) Freedom struggle ideals\n• 51A(c) Sovereignty, Unity, Integrity\n• 51A(d) Defence / National Service\n• 51A(e) Harmony / Brotherhood\n• 51A(f) Composite Culture\n• 51A(g) Environment\n• 51A(h) Scientific Temper\n• 51A(i) Public Property / Non-violence","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Fundamental Duties\n• 51A(j) Excellence\n• 51A(k) Child Education 6–14\n TRICK — “a to k = Constitution se Child Education tak”\n💡\nAlphabet ladder ko story ki tarah dekho: Constitution\n Freedom ideals\n Unity\n Defence\n Harmony\n Culture\n\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nEnvironment\n Science\n Property\n Excellence\n Education.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nIs story se clause-letter recall fast hota hai.\n4. Duty #11 — 86th Amendment Ka Special Trap\n• 11th Duty = Parent/Guardian must provide education opportunity to child/ward aged 6–14\n• Added by 86th Amendment, 2002\n• Article = 51A(k)\n• Source notes link it with Article 21A (FR) and Article 45 (DPSP)\n TRICK — “86 = Education Triangle”\n💡\n86th Amendment ko teen places se connect karo: 51A(k) parental duty + Art. 21A Right to Education + Art. 45 DPSP\nconnection.\nAge anchor = 6–14.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n42nd Amendment ne original 10 duties add ki; 11th duty 86th Amendment, 2002 se aayi.\n5. High-Yield Duty Hooks\n• Flag & Anthem\n 51A(a)\n→\n• Sovereignty, Unity, Integrity\n 51A(c)\n→\n• Environment\n 51A(g)\n→\n• Scientific Temper\n 51A(h)\n→\n• Public Property + Non-violence\n 51A(i)\n→\n• Excellence\n 51A(j)\n→\n• Education 6–14\n 51A(k)\n→\n TRICK — “a Flag, c Country, g Green, h Head/Science, i Infrastructure, j Excellence, k Kids”\n💡\nClause letters ko visual hooks se lock karo:\ng = Green environment, h = Head/scientific temper, i = Infrastructure/public property, k = Kids/education.\n6. Non-Justiciable — Lekin Meaningless Nahi\n• Fundamental Duties directly legally enforceable nahi\n• Constitution violation ke liye direct punishment mention nahi karta\n• Courts laws interpret karte waqt Duties ko consider kar sakti hain\n• Verma Committee, 1999\n reviewed and recommended strengthening of Fundamental Duties\n→\n• Applicable only to citizens\n TRICK — “No Direct Enforcement, Yes Court Consideration”\n💡\nNon-Justiciable ka matlab 'ignore' nahi.\nDirect constitutional punishment NO; interpretive relevance YES.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNon-Justiciable ≠ legally meaningless. Source notes specifically court consideration aur Verma Committee 1999 mention\nkarte hain.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Fundamental Duties\n7. Swaran Singh vs Verma Committee\n• Swaran Singh Committee\n Fundamental Duties add karne ki recommendation\n→\n• Verma Committee\n 1999 review / strengthening recommendations\n→\n TRICK — “Swaran = Start, Verma = Verify”\n💡\nSwaran Singh = duties ka START/addition recommendation.\nVerma 1999 = later VERIFY/review/strengthening.\n8. FR vs DPSP vs FD — 3-Part Comparison\n• Fundamental Rights\n Part III, Articles 12–35, Justiciable, individual rights\n→\n• DPSP\n Part IV, Articles 36–51, Non-Justiciable, State's duties\n→\n• Fundamental Duties\n Part IV-A, Article 51A, Non-Justiciable, Citizen's duties\n→\n• FR & DPSP were in Original Constitution; Fundamental Duties added in 1976\n TRICK — “III = Rights, IV = State Directions, IV-A = Citizen Duties”\n💡\nPart III = Individual ke rights.\nPart IV = State ko directions/duties.\nPart IV-A = Citizen ke duties.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nFR source USA aur Duties source USSR — ye country-source pair ko ulta mat karna.\n9. 20-Second Flash Recall\n• Part IV-A | Article 51A\n• 42nd Amendment 1976 | Swaran Singh\n• USSR source | Non-Justiciable | Citizens only\n• 10 original\n 11 current\n→\n• 86th Amendment 2002\n 51A(k), child education 6–14\n→\n• Verma Committee 1999\n review\n→\n• Mnemonic = SCREAM VAN LIP\n TRICK — “42-Swaran-10 / 86-Education-11 / 99-Verma”\n💡\nThree timeline anchors: 1976 = add 10, 2002 = add 11th, 1999 = Verma review.\nExam mein year/committee/count ko isi triangle se answer karo.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part IV-A, Art 51A\n 42nd/1976 + Swaran Singh + USSR\n 10 original duties\n SCREAM VAN LIP: Constitution, Cherish\n→\n→\n→\nstruggle, Sovereignty, Defence, Harmony, Culture, Environment, Scientific temper, Public property, Excellence, Education\n\n→\n86th/2002 adds 51A(k), child 6–14 + link 21A/45\n Non-Justiciable, no direct constitutional punishment, courts may consider\n→\n Verma 1999 review\n FR Part III individual/justiciable; DPSP Part IV State/non-justiciable; FD Part IV-A citizen/non-\n→\n→\njusticiable.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nPart IV-A, not Part IV | 42nd = original 10, 86th = 11th duty | Source USSR, while Fundamental Rights source USA.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.10 President of India\nPRESIDENT OF INDIA — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 10 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part V Articles 52–73, eligibility, electoral college, powers, pardon, impeachment, key Presidents\naur article traps ko fast recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. President Skeleton — Part V, 52–73, Nominal Executive\n• President = Part V, Articles 52–73\n• Head of State + Supreme Commander + First Citizen\n• President = Nominal / De Jure Executive\n• Prime Minister = Real / De Facto Executive\n• President acts on Cabinet advice — Article 74\n• Term = 5 years; re-election = no limit\n TRICK — “President = Paper Power, PM = Practical Power”\n💡\nPresident ko legal/formal head samjho; real executive authority PM + Council of Ministers ke paas.\nArticle 74 = Cabinet advice anchor.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNominal/De Jure = President; Real/De Facto = Prime Minister. Ye sabse common role-swap trap hai.\n2. Eligibility & Personal Facts — 35, 5, CJI, VP\n• Minimum age = 35 years\n• Must be citizen of India\n• Must have Lok Sabha member eligibility\n• Must not hold office of profit\n• Term = 5 years\n• Salary = ₹5 lakh/month (Consolidated Fund of India)\n• Oath = Chief Justice of India, or senior-most judge\n• Resignation = Vice-President\n• Election dispute = Supreme Court, Article 71\n• Naturalized citizen can also become President (as per source notes)\n TRICK — “35–5–CJI–VP–71 SC”\n💡\n35 age, 5-year term, oath CJI, resignation VP, election dispute Art.71 in Supreme Court.\nEk hi line mein revise karo: “35-5, CJI-VP, 71-SC.”\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nResignation Vice-President ko; oath CJI dilwate hain. Speaker/VP-oath ke saath confuse mat karna.\n3. Election Formula — Indirect + PR + STV + Secret\n• Election type = Indirect\n• System = Proportional Representation\n• Voting = Single Transferable Vote\n• Ballot = Secret\n• Security deposit = ₹15,000\n• Proposers + Seconders = 50 + 50\n• Dispute = Supreme Court, Article 71","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.10 President of India\n TRICK — “I-PR-STV-S”\n💡\nIndirect\n Proportional Representation\n Single Transferable Vote\n Secret Ballot.\n→\n→\n→\nNumbers: ₹15,000 deposit + 50 proposers + 50 seconders.\n4. Electoral College — Kaun Vote Karta Hai?\n• Elected Lok Sabha MPs\n YES\n→\n• Elected Rajya Sabha MPs\n YES\n→\n• Elected State MLAs\n YES\n→\n• Elected MLAs of Delhi & Puducherry\n YES\n→\n• MLCs / Vidhan Parishad members\n NO\n→\n• Nominated MPs / MLAs\n NO\n→\n TRICK — “Elected = Entry; Nominated/MLC = No Entry”\n💡\nPresidential election mein elected MPs + elected MLAs main voter block hai.\nUT exception in source notes: Delhi & Puducherry elected MLAs.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nLegislative Council members vote nahi karte. Nominated MPs/MLAs bhi excluded hain.\n5. President Powers — E-L-J-E\n• Executive\n PM/Ministers, Governors, SC/HC Judges, Commission chairpersons, diplomats appointments\n→\n• Legislative\n Summon/Prorogue Parliament, dissolve Lok Sabha, assent/return/withhold bills, ordinances\n→\n• Judicial\n Article 72 pardoning powers\n→\n• Emergency\n Articles 352, 356, 360\n→\n TRICK — “E-L-J-E = Executive, Legislative, Judicial, Emergency”\n💡\nPresident ki 4 power-buckets ko ELJE bolo.\nEmergency number ladder: 352 National\n 356 President's Rule\n 360 Financial.\n→\n→\n6. Article 72 — 5 Pardoning Powers\n• Pardon\n punishment + conviction completely removed\n→\n• Commutation\n one punishment changed into a lighter punishment\n→\n• Reprieve\n temporary postponement, especially death sentence\n→\n• Respite\n special reason se sentence reduce\n→\n• Remission\n punishment quantity reduce; nature same\n→\n TRICK — “P-C-R-R-R”\n💡\nPardon\n Commutation\n Reprieve\n Respite\n Remission.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nMeaning chain: Finish\n Change\n Postpone\n Special Reduce\n Quantity Reduce.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n TRICK — “72 President, 161 Governor”\n💡\nArticle 72 = President pardoning powers.\nArticle 161 = Governor pardoning powers; source notes mark death-sentence pardon as President-only.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArt.72 President vs Art.161 Governor ko swap mat karna.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.10 President of India\n7. Veto Hook — Pocket Veto\n• Giani Zail Singh (1982–87)\n Pocket Veto\n→\n• Postal Bill par assent nahi diya\n• Pocket veto = na assent dena, na return karna\n TRICK — “Zail = Zip the Bill in Pocket”\n💡\nGiani Zail Singh ko Postal Bill pocket mein rakhne wali image se yaad karo.\nNa assent, na return = Pocket Veto.\n8. Impeachment — Article 61\n• Nature = Quasi-judicial\n• Can start in either House\n• 14 days prior notice\n• Special Majority = not less than 2/3 of Total Membership\n• Both Houses must pass\n• Result = President removed\n• No President has been impeached so far (as stated in source notes)\n TRICK — “61–14–2/3–Both”\n💡\nArt.61\n 14-day notice\n 2/3 of total membership\n both Houses.\n→\n→\n→\nYe chaar-number chain impeachment ka core hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nImpeachment majority = 2/3 of TOTAL membership, not merely present & voting.\n9. Election vs Impeachment Voters — Opposite Pair\n• Elected MPs\n Election YES | Impeachment YES\n→\n• Elected MLAs\n Election YES | Impeachment NO\n→\n• Nominated MPs\n Election NO | Impeachment YES\n→\n TRICK — “MLA = Election only; Nominated MP = Impeachment only”\n💡\nDo opposite arrows yaad karo:\nMLA\n Election YES, Impeachment NO.\n→\nNominated MP\n Election NO, Impeachment YES.\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nMLAs and nominated MPs ka election/impeachment role ulta hai — ye direct table-trap hai.\n10. Key Article Ladder — 52 to 62, then 71–74\n• 52\n President of India\n→\n• 53\n Union executive power vested in President\n→\n• 54\n Election of President\n→\n• 55\n Election procedure\n→\n• 56\n Term: 5 years\n→\n• 57\n Re-election\n→\n• 58\n Qualifications\n→\n• 59\n Conditions / office of profit\n→\n• 60\n Oath\n→\n• 61\n Impeachment\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.10 President of India\n• 62\n Vacancy\n→\n• 71\n Election dispute\n→\n• 72\n Pardoning powers\n→\n• 73\n Extent of Union executive power\n→\n• 74\n Council of Ministers aid & advice\n→\n TRICK — “52–62 = President Core; 71 Dispute, 72 Pardon, 74 Advice”\n💡\n52 se 62 tak President ke direct core articles.\nUske baad teen exam anchors: 71 SC dispute, 72 Pardon, 74 Cabinet advice.\n11. Presidents — Firsts & Unique Facts\n• Dr. Rajendra Prasad\n First President; longest serving; 2 terms\n→\n• Dr. S. Radhakrishnan\n First Vice-President to become President\n→\n• Dr. Zakir Hussain\n First to die in office\n→\n• V.V. Giri\n First Acting President, later elected; first elected as Independent candidate\n→\n• Justice Hidayatullah\n First CJI to become Acting President\n→\n• Neelam Sanjiva Reddy\n Elected unopposed\n→\n• Giani Zail Singh\n Pocket Veto\n→\n• K.R. Narayanan\n First Dalit President\n→\n• Dr. APJ Abdul Kalam\n Missile Man / People's President\n→\n• Pratibha Patil\n First Woman President\n→\n• Droupadi Murmu\n First Tribal President; 2nd Woman President\n→\n TRICK — “Rajendra–Zakir–Giri–Reddy–Zail–Narayanan–Patil–Murmu” anchors\n💡\nLong list ratne ke bajay exam-firsts ko anchor banao:\nFirst/Longest = Rajendra | Died in office = Zakir | Independent = Giri | Unopposed = Reddy | Pocket = Zail | Dalit =\nNarayanan | Woman = Patil | Tribal = Murmu.\n12. Rashtrapati Bhawan — Tiny Memory Block\n• Architect = Edwin Lutyens\n• Location = New Delhi\n• Mughal Garden\n Amrit Udyan\n→\n• Durbar Hall\n Gantantra Mandap\n→\n• Ashoka Hall\n Ashok Mandap\n→\n TRICK — “Lutyens + A-G-A”\n💡\nArchitect = Lutyens.\nRename chain: Amrit Udyan\n Gantantra Mandap\n Ashok Mandap.\n→\n→\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part V 52–73\n President Nominal/De Jure, PM Real/De Facto, Art74 advice\n 35 age, 5-year term, CJI oath, VP resignation,\n→\n→\n71-SC dispute\n Election I-PR-STV-Secret, ₹15k, 50+50\n Electoral College = elected MPs + elected MLAs incl.\n→\n→\nDelhi/Puducherry; MLC/nominated NO\n Powers ELJE; Emergency 352-356-360\n Art72 PCRRR pardons\n Zail Singh Pocket\n→\n→\n→\nVeto\n Impeachment Art61: 14 days, 2/3 total, both Houses\n MLA election-only, nominated MP impeachment-only\n 52–62\n→\n→\n→\ncore; 71 dispute, 72 pardon, 74 advice\n Firsts: Rajendra first/longest, Zakir died, Giri independent, Reddy unopposed, Zail\n→\npocket, Narayanan Dalit, Patil woman, Murmu tribal.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nPresident nominal vs PM real | Election: elected MPs/MLAs only, not MLC/nominated | Impeachment = 2/3 of total\nmembership in both Houses.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Prime Minister & Council of Ministers\nPRIME MINISTER & COUNCIL OF MINISTERS — MEMORY\nTRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 11 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Articles 74–78, Council of Ministers structure, 91st Amendment, PM role, Attorney General, +89\nmemory rule aur Prime Minister firsts ko fast recall format mein diya gaya hai.\n1. PM Skeleton — Part V, 74–78, Real Executive\n• Prime Minister = Real / De Facto Executive\n• President = Nominal / De Jure Executive\n• Council of Ministers aids & advises President\n• Part V\n Articles 74–78\n→\n• Council of Ministers = 3 types\n• 91st Amendment\n Ministers capped at 15% of Lok Sabha strength\n→\n TRICK — “PM = Practical Power, President = Paper/Formal Power”\n💡\nPM ko actual executive authority se jodo; President formal/nominal executive hai.\nChapter ka number spine: Part V\n 74–78\n 3 types\n 15% cap.\n→\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nReal Executive = Prime Minister; Nominal Executive = President.\n2. Council of Ministers — 3 Types\n• Cabinet Ministers\n Senior rank, Cabinet meetings, full portfolio\n→\n• Ministers of State (MoS)\n Independent or attached to Cabinet Minister\n→\n• Deputy Ministers\n Assist Cabinet Minister / MoS\n→\n TRICK — “C-M-D = Cabinet\n MoS\n Deputy”\n💡\n→\n→\nHierarchy ko top-to-bottom bolo: Cabinet\n Minister of State\n Deputy.\n→\n→\nCabinet = senior/full portfolio; MoS = middle; Deputy = assist.\n3. Advice to President — First Return, Second Binding\n• Council of Ministers advice\n first time President may return\n→\n• Second time\n advice binding\n→\n• Source notes link this with 44th Amendment, 1978\n• Council of Ministers collectively responsible to Lok Sabha\n• Loss of Lok Sabha confidence\n collective resignation\n→\n TRICK — “1st Return, 2nd Bind”\n💡\nAdvice rule ko sirf do-step picture mein yaad karo:\nFirst time President can return\n second time must accept.\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCollective responsibility = Lok Sabha ke prati, Rajya Sabha ke prati nahi.\n4. Eligibility & Appointment — 25, 30, 6, 15\n• Lok Sabha eligibility age = 25 years\n• Rajya Sabha eligibility age = 30 years\n• Non-MP minister/PM\n must become MP within 6 months\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Prime Minister & Council of Ministers\n• Maximum ministers = 15% of Lok Sabha strength, including PM\n• PM appointed by President\n• Other ministers appointed by President on PM's advice\n• Resignation\n President\n→\n• Salary\n Consolidated Fund of India, Article 75(6)\n→\n• Term\n 5 years or until Lok Sabha dissolved\n→\n TRICK — “25–30–6–15”\n💡\n25 = Lok Sabha, 30 = Rajya Sabha, 6 months = non-MP window, 15% = ministry cap.\nYe four-number chain eligibility + appointment ka core hai.\n TRICK — “91 = 15%”\n💡\n91st Amendment ko seedha 15% ministry limit se lock karo.\nSource example: Lok Sabha 543\n about 81 ministers including PM.\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n15% limit Lok Sabha strength par based hai, Rajya Sabha strength par nahi.\n5. PM's Core Role — Head, Leader, Link, Inform\n• Head of Council of Ministers\n• Leader of the House (Lok Sabha)\n• Key figure in Parliamentary Democracy\n• Real Executive\n• Link between President and Cabinet\n• Article 78\n PM must inform President about Cabinet decisions and furnish information\n→\n TRICK — “H-L-L-I = Head, Leader, Link, Inform”\n💡\nPM role ko 4 words mein yaad karo: Head of CoM\n Leader of House\n Link to President\n Inform under Art.78.\n→\n→\n→\nArticle 78 = President ko information dena.\n6. PM as Chairperson — N-N-N-I\n• NITI Aayog\n• NDMA — National Disaster Management Authority\n• National Integration Council\n• Indian Board of Wildlife\n• NITI Aayog replaced Planning Commission in 2015\n• PM = Ex-Officio Chairperson of NITI Aayog\n TRICK — “N-N-N-I”\n💡\nNITI\n NDMA\n National Integration Council\n Indian Board of Wildlife.\n→\n→\n→\n2015 = NITI Aayog replaces Planning Commission.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNITI Aayog mein PM Chairperson hai; Vice-Chairperson alag post hai.\n7. Key Articles 74–78 — One-Line Map\n• Article 74\n Council of Ministers to aid & advise President\n→\n• Article 75\n Appointment, tenure, qualifications of PM & Ministers\n→\n• 75(1)\n PM by President; other ministers on PM's advice\n→\n• 75(2)\n Ministers hold office during President's pleasure\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Prime Minister & Council of Ministers\n• 75(6)\n Salaries from Consolidated Fund of India\n→\n• Article 76\n Attorney General of India\n→\n• Article 77\n Conduct of Government business in President's name\n→\n• Article 78\n PM's duty to furnish information to President\n→\n TRICK — “74 Advice, 75 Ministers, 76 AG, 77 Business, 78 Information”\n💡\n74–78 ko ek straight ladder mein bolo:\nAdvice\n Ministers\n Attorney General\n Govt Business\n Information.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n8. Attorney General of India — Article 76\n• Article 76\n Attorney General of India\n→\n• Chief Legal Advisor / highest Law Officer of Government of India\n• Appointed by President\n• Qualification\n eligible to be Supreme Court Judge\n→\n• Can participate in proceedings of both Houses of Parliament\n• Cannot vote\n• Private legal practice not barred in source notes\n• Salary fixed by Government\n• First Attorney General\n M.C. Setalvad\n→\n• Source notes list R. Venkataramani as current Attorney General\n TRICK — “AG = Attend, but No Vote”\n💡\nAttorney General Parliament ke dono Houses mein bol/participate kar sakta hai, vote nahi.\nAG = constitutional post, Article 76.\n TRICK — “76 Centre, 165 State”\n💡\nAttorney General (Centre) = Art.76.\nAdvocate General (State) = Art.165.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nAttorney General ≠ Solicitor General. Source notes: AG constitutional post (Art.76); Solicitor General has no constitutional\nbasis.\n9. +89 Rule — Centre to State Memory Trick\n• 74\n 163 : PM/CoM\n CM/State CoM\n→\n→\n• 72\n 161 : President Pardon\n Governor Pardon\n→\n→\n• 76\n 165 : Attorney General\n Advocate General\n→\n→\n• 79\n 168 : Parliament\n State Legislature\n→\n→\n• 80\n 169 : Rajya Sabha\n Legislative Council\n→\n→\n• 81\n 170 : Lok Sabha\n Legislative Assembly\n→\n→\n TRICK — “Centre Article + 89 = State Equivalent”\n💡\nExample: 74+89=163, 76+89=165, 81+89=170.\nYe sirf article-number mnemonic hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n+89 Rule constitutional provision nahi — sirf memory trick hai.\n10. Prime Ministers — High-Yield Firsts & Unique Facts\n• Jawaharlal Nehru\n Longest-serving PM\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.11 Prime Minister & Council of Ministers\n• Gulzarilal Nanda\n First Acting PM\n→\n• Lal Bahadur Shastri\n 'Jai Jawan Jai Kisan'; died in Tashkent\n→\n• Indira Gandhi\n First Woman PM; source notes mark her as from Rajya Sabha\n→\n• Morarji Desai\n First Non-Congress PM\n→\n• Charan Singh\n Never faced Lok Sabha\n→\n• Rajiv Gandhi\n Youngest PM\n→\n• V.P. Singh\n source notes mark first PM to resign after no-confidence vote\n→\n• P.V. Narasimha Rao\n First South Indian PM; LPG reforms 1991\n→\n• Atal Bihari Vajpayee\n Shortest tenure: 13 days (first stint)\n→\n• Dr. Manmohan Singh\n First Sikh PM; Rajya Sabha member\n→\n• Narendra Modi\n source notes mark first BJP-majority PM; 3 consecutive terms\n→\n TRICK — “Nehru Long, Vajpayee Short, Indira Woman, Morarji Non-Congress”\n💡\nFirst-round anchors: Nehru = longest, Vajpayee = 13-day shortest, Indira = first woman, Morarji = first non-Congress.\nSecond-round anchors: Charan = never faced LS, Manmohan = first Sikh, Rao = first South Indian, Nanda = first acting.\n11. FR-like Exam Traps — Fast Contrast\n• PM = Real Executive; President = Nominal Executive\n• CoM responsible to Lok Sabha\n• Advice second time binding (source notes: 44th Amendment)\n• 91st Amendment = 15% ministry cap\n• AG can participate but cannot vote\n• AG constitutional; Solicitor General not constitutional\n• +89 is mnemonic only\n TRICK — “Real–Lok Sabha–Second–15%–No Vote–+89 Trick”\n💡\nYe six trap-words exam se pehle ek baar zaroor bolo.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part V 74–78\n PM Real/De Facto, President Nominal/De Jure\n CoM 3 types C-M-D\n Advice: 1st Return, 2nd Bind;\n→\n→\n→\nresponsible to Lok Sabha\n Eligibility 25 LS, 30 RS, non-MP 6 months, 91st=15% cap\n PM appointed President, others on PM\n→\n→\nadvice, salary Art75(6)\n PM role H-L-L-I; Art78 Inform President\n Chairpersons N-N-N-I, NITI 2015\n Article ladder 74\n→\n→\n→\nAdvice, 75 Ministers, 76 AG, 77 Business, 78 Information\n AG: attend both Houses but no vote; 76 Centre, 165 State\n +89:\n→\n→\n74\n163,72\n161,76\n165,79\n168,80\n169,81\n170\n PM anchors: Nehru longest, Nanda acting, Indira woman, Morarji non-\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nCongress, Charan no LS, Vajpayee 13 days, Manmohan Sikh, Rao South Indian.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nPM = Real Executive | 91st Amendment = max 15% of Lok Sabha strength | Attorney General can participate in\nParliament but cannot vote.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Parliament of India\nPARLIAMENT OF INDIA — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 12 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Lok Sabha vs Rajya Sabha, Speaker/Chairman, sessions, bills, veto, motions, committees,\nbudget/funds aur key Articles ko high-memory-load exam facts ke roop mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Parliament Skeleton — Part V, 79–122, P-L-R\n• Parliament = President + Lok Sabha + Rajya Sabha\n• Part V\n Articles 79–122\n→\n• Bicameral Legislature\n• Lok Sabha = Lower House / House of the People\n• Rajya Sabha = Upper House / Council of States\n• No bill becomes Act without President's assent\n TRICK — “P-L-R = President + Lok Sabha + Rajya Sabha”\n💡\nParliament ko sirf 3 blocks mein dekho: P-L-R.\nArticle 79 ko Parliament formation ka starting anchor banao.\n2. Lok Sabha vs Rajya Sabha — 55/54 vs 25/24 Pattern\n• Lok Sabha: Max 550 | Current 543 | Age 25 | Term 5 years | Dissolution possible\n• Rajya Sabha: Max 250 | Current 245 | Age 30 | Term 6 years per member | Permanent House\n• Rajya Sabha: 1/3 members retire every 2 years\n• Rajya Sabha nominated members = 12\n• Both Houses first session = 13 May 1952\n TRICK — “LS: 550–543–25–5 | RS: 250–245–30–6”\n💡\nLok Sabha numbers end around 5: 550, 543, age 25, term 5.\nRajya Sabha numbers: 250, 245, age 30, term 6.\nRS bonus: 1/3 retire every 2 years + 12 nominated.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nRajya Sabha permanent House hai; Lok Sabha dissolve ho sakti hai. Dono ka first session 13 May 1952.\n3. Lok Sabha Special Numbers — 104, 84, Delimitation\n• 104th Amendment (2020)\n Anglo-Indian reservation / 2 nominated seats ended\n→\n• 1st Delimitation Commission\n 1952\n→\n• Total Delimitation Commissions in notes\n 1952, 1963, 1973, 2002\n→\n• 84th Amendment (2001)\n seats frozen till 2026 (as stated in source notes)\n→\n• Emergency: Lok Sabha term may extend 1 year; not beyond 6 months after emergency ends\n TRICK — “52–63–73–02 = Delimitation Four”\n💡\nDelimitation years ko 4-beat rhythm mein bolo: 52, 63, 73, 02.\n84th Amendment = seat-freeze anchor; 104th = Anglo-Indian nomination ended.\n4. Rajya Sabha — 238 + 12 = 250, Permanent Rotation\n• Max = 250\n 238 elected + 12 nominated\n→\n• Current in source notes = 245\n• Indirect election\n Proportional Representation + Single Transferable Vote\n→\n• MLAs vote in Rajya Sabha election\n• Nominated fields = Literature, Art, Science, Social Service","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Parliament of India\n• Bihar\n Rajya Sabha 16 seats; Lok Sabha 40 seats (source notes)\n→\n TRICK — “238 + 12 = 250; 1/3 every 2; term 6”\n💡\nRS ko rotating wheel samjho: 6-year term, har 2 saal mein 1/3 retirement.\n12 nominated = LASS: Literature, Art, Science, Social Service.\n5. Speaker & Chairman — Who Presides Where?\n• Lok Sabha Speaker + Deputy Speaker elected from LS members\n• Joint Sitting (Art. 108)\n Speaker presides\n→\n• If Speaker absent\n Deputy Speaker\n then Deputy Chairman of Rajya Sabha\n→\n→\n• Speaker has casting vote in tie\n• Speaker resignation\n Deputy Speaker; vice versa\n→\n• Rajya Sabha Chairman = Vice-President, Article 89(1)\n• Vice-President is not a member of either House\n• Pro-tem Speaker\n senior-most MP after LS election; appointed by President; administers oath & conducts Speaker\n→\nelection\n TRICK — “Joint Sitting: S > DS > DC”\n💡\nSpeaker\n Deputy Speaker\n Deputy Chairman.\n→\n→\nJoint Sitting hierarchy mein pehle dono Lok Sabha posts aate hain.\n TRICK — “VP = Chairman, not Member”\n💡\nVice-President Rajya Sabha ka Chairman hai, member nahi.\nTie mein Chairman ke roop mein casting vote ka hook yaad rakho.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nJoint Sitting ko Rajya Sabha Chairman preside nahi karta; hierarchy Speaker\n Deputy Speaker\n Deputy Chairman.\n→\n→\n6. Disqualification, Defection & Privilege\n• Disqualification\n Article 102\n→\n• Defection\n 52nd Amendment, 1985\n 10th Schedule\n→\n→\n• Independent member joins party\n defection\n→\n• Nominated member joins party after 6 months\n defection\n→\n• Whip violation / joining another party\n defection\n→\n• Merger exception\n 2/3 members\n→\n• Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992)\n Presiding Officer decision subject to judicial review\n→\n• Defection decision\n Speaker (LS) / Chairman (RS)\n→\n• Civil arrest privilege\n 40 days before + session + 40 days after; criminal arrest allowed\n→\n TRICK — “52–85–10”\n💡\nDefection triangle: 52nd Amendment\n 1985\n 10th Schedule.\n→\n→\nMerger ka magic number = 2/3.\n TRICK — “40–Session–40, Civil Only”\n💡\nPrivilege memory image: session ke dono side 40-40 days.\nCriminal case mein arrest allowed.\n7. Sessions & Parliamentary Time — B-M-W\n• Budget Session\n Feb–May (Longest)\n→\n• Monsoon Session\n July–September\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Parliament of India\n• Winter Session\n Nov–Dec (Shortest)\n→\n• Summoning\n President\n→\n• Prorogation\n President\n→\n• Adjournment\n Speaker\n→\n• Sine Die\n adjournment without fixing next date\n→\n• Lame Duck\n LS members who lost election; term from USA\n→\n TRICK — “B-M-W = Budget, Monsoon, Winter”\n💡\nYear ko teen parliamentary seasons mein dekho: Budget\n Monsoon\n Winter.\n→\n→\nLongest = Budget; shortest = Winter.\n TRICK — “S-P-P, A-S”\n💡\nSummoning-President, Prorogation-President, Adjournment-Speaker.\nPresident session bulata/end karta; Speaker sitting temporarily rokta.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSummoning = President; Adjournment = Speaker. Dono ko swap mat karna.\n8. Question Hour vs Zero Hour\n• Question Hour\n 11 AM–12 PM\n→\n• Zero Hour\n 12 PM–1 PM\n→\n• Source notes: Zero Hour Constitution mein mentioned nahi; informal practice\n• Question Hour includes starred, unstarred, short-notice questions\n TRICK — “11 Question, 12 Zero”\n💡\n11 baje Question Hour start; 12 baje Zero Hour start.\nZero Hour = zero constitutional mention.\n9. Bills — Ordinary vs Money vs Constitution Amendment\n• Ordinary/Public Bill\n either House; Joint Sitting possible\n→\n• Private Member Bill\n non-minister introduces; 30 days notice\n→\n• Money Bill\n Article 110; Lok Sabha only; prior President consent\n→\n• Money Bill status decided by Lok Sabha Speaker\n• Rajya Sabha can hold Money Bill only 14 days; no amendment power\n• Money Bill\n No Joint Sitting\n→\n• Constitution Amendment Bill\n No Joint Sitting\n→\n• Source notes: President has no veto on Constitution Amendment Bill after 24th Amendment, 1971\n TRICK — “110 = Money > LS > Speaker > 14 > No Joint”\n💡\nMoney Bill ke 5 anchors ek saath bolo: Art.110, Lok Sabha only, Speaker decides, RS 14 days, no Joint Sitting.\nAll Money Bills are Financial Bills; not vice versa (source notes).\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nMoney Bill par Rajya Sabha sirf 14 din recommendations de sakti hai; amendment power aur Joint Sitting nahi.\n10. President's Veto — A-S-P, Q Nahi\n• Absolute Veto\n assent completely withheld; bill lapses\n→\n• Suspensive Veto\n bill returned for reconsideration\n→\n• Pocket Veto\n no action; bill pending indefinitely\n→\n• Qualified Veto\n not available to Indian President in source notes\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Parliament of India\n• Giani Zail Singh\n Pocket Veto on India Post Office (Amendment) Bill\n→\n TRICK — “A-S-P; Qualified = Quit India”\n💡\nIndia ke President ke exam-veto anchors: Absolute, Suspensive, Pocket.\nQualified Veto ko 'Q = Quit/Not available' se yaad karo.\n11. Cut Motions — ₹1, Specified, ₹100\n• Policy Cut\n amount reduced to ₹1\n→\n• Economy Cut\n amount reduced by specified amount\n→\n• Token Cut\n amount reduced by ₹100\n→\n TRICK — “P-E-T = 1 – Specific – 100”\n💡\nPolicy = ₹1, Economy = specified, Token = ₹100.\nPolicy aur Token amounts ulte lagte hain — isi liye ₹1 vs ₹100 ko red-circle karo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPolicy Cut = ₹1; Token Cut = ₹100.\n12. Joint Sittings — 3 Famous Cases\n• Dowry\n 1961\n→\n• Banking\n 1977\n→\n• POTA\n 2002\n→\n TRICK — “D-B-P = 61–77–02”\n💡\nDowry\n Banking\n POTA.\n→\n→\nYears rhythm: 61\n 77\n 02.\n→\n→\n13. Parliamentary Motions — N-C-A Contrast\n• No-Confidence\n only Lok Sabha; minimum 50 members; no reason required; passed\n Government resigns\n→\n→\n• Censure\n against minister/CoM; Government not bound to resign\n→\n• Adjournment Motion\n urgent public-importance issue; minimum 50; Government does not resign\n→\n• Confidence Motion\n ruling party proves majority in Lok Sabha\n→\n• First No-Confidence Motion\n 1963, J.B. Kriplani against Nehru (not passed)\n→\n TRICK — “N = Knock Government, C = Criticise, A = Attention”\n💡\nNo-Confidence = government gir sakti hai.\nCensure = criticism/embarrassment; government compulsory resign nahi.\nAdjournment = urgent issue par attention.\n TRICK — “50 for N + A, not C”\n💡\nNo-Confidence = 50 members; Adjournment = 50 members; Censure = no minimum number in source notes.\n14. Committees — Numbers That Get Swapped\n• Public Accounts Committee (PAC)\n 22 = 15 LS + 7 RS; oldest; expenditure/CAG; headed by Opposition leader\n→\n• Estimates Committee\n 30, all from Lok Sabha; established 1950\n→\n• Privileges Committee\n 15 = 10 LS + 5 RS\n→\n• Select Committee (JPC in source table)\n 30\n→\n• Joint Select Committee\n 45 = 30 LS + 15 RS\n→\n• Committee on Public Undertakings\n 22 = 15 LS + 7 RS; established 1964\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Parliament of India\n TRICK — “PAC & PSU = 22 (15+7); Estimates = 30 All-LS”\n💡\nPAC aur Public Undertakings dono ka number-pack same: 22 = 15 LS + 7 RS.\nEstimates = 30, aur sab Lok Sabha se.\n TRICK — “Privileges 15; Joint Select 45”\n💡\nPrivileges = 10+5 = 15.\nJoint Select = 30+15 = 45.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPAC = oldest + Opposition leader; Estimates Committee = all 30 members Lok Sabha se.\n15. Budget & 3 Funds — 112 / 266 / 267\n• Budget constitutional name = Annual Financial Statement, Article 112\n• Source notes: word 'Budget' Constitution mein mentioned nahi\n• Presented by Finance Minister\n• Rajya Sabha\n discuss only; budget voting rights nahi\n→\n• Lok Sabha\n voting rights on budget\n→\n• Consolidated Fund\n Article 266(1); withdrawal needs Parliament approval\n→\n• Public Accounts\n Article 266(2); not subject to Parliamentary vote\n→\n• Contingency Fund\n Article 267; emergency reserve held by President; sanction later\n→\n TRICK — “112 = Statement; 266(1) Consolidated, 266(2) Public, 267 Contingency”\n💡\nFunds ko article sequence se yaad karo: 266(1) C, 266(2) P, 267 C.\nEasy logic: Consolidated = Parliament first; Contingency = emergency first, sanction later.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nRajya Sabha budget par vote nahi kar sakti — sirf discuss. Consolidated withdrawal needs Parliament approval; Contingency\nemergency withdrawal can precede later sanction.\n16. Key Article Ladder — Parliament Fast Map\n• 79 Parliament | 80 Rajya Sabha | 81 Lok Sabha | 82 Delimitation\n• 83 Duration | 84 Qualifications | 85 Sessions\n• 89 RS Chairman | 93 LS Speaker | 102 Disqualification\n• 107 Bill in either House | 108 Joint Sitting | 110 Money Bill\n• 112 Annual Financial Statement | 114 Appropriation Bill | 117(3) Finance Bill\n• 123 Presidential Ordinance\n• 266(1) Consolidated Fund | 266(2) Public Accounts | 267 Contingency Fund\n TRICK — “79 P, 80 R, 81 L, 82 D — then 108 Joint, 110 Money, 112 Budget”\n💡\nStart ladder: Parliament\n RS\n LS\n Delimitation.\n→\n→\n→\nBill ladder: 108 Joint Sitting\n 110 Money Bill\n 112 Annual Financial Statement.\n→\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.12 Parliament of India\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part V 79–122, Parliament=P-L-R\n LS 550/543/25/5 vs RS 250/245/30/6 + 1/3 every2 + 12 nominated\n Delimitation 52-63-73-\n→\n→\n02; 84 freeze, 104 Anglo-Indian end\n Speaker joint sitting S-DS-DC; VP=RS Chairman not member\n Defection\n→\n→\n52nd/1985/10th + 2/3 merger; privilege Civil 40-session-40\n Sessions B-M-W; Summon/Prorogue President, Adjourn Speaker\n→\n 11 Question,12 Zero\n Money Bill 110-LS-Speaker-14-NoJoint; Constitution Amendment NoJoint/NoVeto in source notes\n\n→\n→\n→\nVeto A-S-P; Cut P-E-T=₹1/specified/₹100\n Joint Sittings DBP=61-77-02\n Motions: NoConfidence Knock, Censure Criticise,\n→\n→\nAdjourn Attention; N+A need 50\n PAC & PSU 22(15+7), Estimates 30 all-LS\n Budget=Art112; Funds 266(1) Consolidated,\n→\n→\n266(2) Public, 267 Contingency.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nRajya Sabha permanent | Money Bill = LS only + RS 14 days + no Joint Sitting | Policy Cut ₹1 vs Token Cut ₹100.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.13 State Executive\nSTATE EXECUTIVE — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 13 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Governor, Chief Minister, State Council of Ministers, Advocate General, Centre-State mirror pairs\naur high-confusion Articles ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. State Executive Skeleton — Part VI, 153–167, 4 Components\n• State Executive = Part VI, Articles 153–167\n• 4 components = Governor + Chief Minister + Council of Ministers + Advocate General\n• Governor = Constitutional / Nominal Head\n• Chief Minister = Real Executive Head\n• Council of Ministers = Aid & Advise Governor\n• Advocate General = Highest Legal Officer of State\n TRICK — “G-C-C-A = Governor, CM, CoM, Advocate General”\n💡\nState Executive ko 4-letter spine se yaad karo: G-C-C-A.\nRole picture: Governor formal, CM real, CoM advises, AG legal.\n2. Centre to State — Mirror Image\n• President -> Governor = Constitutional Head\n• Prime Minister -> Chief Minister = Real Executive\n• Attorney General (Art. 76) -> Advocate General (Art. 165)\n• President pardon Art. 72 -> Governor pardon Art. 161\n• President ordinance Art. 123 -> Governor ordinance Art. 213\n• Parliament Art. 79 -> State Legislature Art. 168\n TRICK — “P-G, PM-CM, AG-AdvG, 72-161, 123-213”\n💡\nState Executive ko Centre ka mirror samjho.\nSabse useful number-pairs: 72/161 pardon aur 123/213 ordinance.\n3. Governor — 35, President, 5-but-Pleasure, HC-CJ\n• Minimum age = 35 years\n• Must be citizen of India\n• Must not be MP/MLA\n• Must not hold office of profit\n• Appointed by President — Article 155\n• Term = 5 years, but at pleasure of President\n• Resignation = to President\n• Salary = ₹3.5 lakh/month\n• Salary source = Consolidated Fund of State\n• Oath = Chief Justice of High Court\n• Governor is also Chancellor of State Universities\n• First Female Governor = Sarojini Naidu, UP (United Provinces), 1947\n TRICK — “35 - President - Pleasure - HC”\n💡\nGovernor memory line: age 35, appointment President, term at President's pleasure, oath HC Chief Justice.\nSalary hook: President ₹5L vs Governor ₹3.5L.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nGovernor ka 5-year term fixed nahi — source notes ke according 'at the pleasure of President'.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.13 State Executive\n4. Governor Oath & Resignation — President Se Contrast\n• President oath = Chief Justice of India\n• Governor oath = Chief Justice of High Court\n• President resignation = Vice-President\n• Governor resignation = President\n TRICK — “President: CJI -> VP | Governor: HC-CJ -> President”\n💡\nDo-line contrast yaad rakho: President ka oath CJI, resignation VP.\nGovernor ka oath HC Chief Justice, resignation President.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nGovernor ka oath CJI nahi dilwata; Chief Justice of High Court dilwata hai.\n5. 7th Amendment 1956 — One Governor, Multiple States\n• 7th Constitutional Amendment, 1956\n• One person can be Governor of more than one State\n• In such cases salary distribution is decided by President (as stated in source notes)\n TRICK — “7th = One Governor, Several States”\n💡\n7 ko multi-state hook banao: ek Governor, ek se zyada States.\n6. Governor Appointments — P-A-E-J-V-F\n• Chairman, State Public Service Commission\n• Advocate General — Article 165\n• Chairman, State Election Commission\n• Judges of Subordinate Courts — Article 233\n• Vice-Chancellors of State Universities — as Chancellor\n• State Finance Commission\n TRICK — “P-A-E-J-V-F”\n💡\nPSC -> Advocate General -> Election Commission -> Judges -> Vice-Chancellors -> Finance Commission.\nArticle hooks: AG = 165, Subordinate Judges = 233.\n7. Governor Powers — Legislature, Ordinance, Pardon, Rule\n• Summon & Prorogue State Legislature\n• Dissolve Legislative Assembly\n• Nominate 1/6 members in Legislative Council\n• Ordinance power = Article 213\n• Pardoning power = Article 161\n• Recommend President's Rule = Article 356\n• Operate State Contingency Fund\n TRICK — “S-D-1/6-213-161-356-Fund”\n💡\nGovernor powers ko number-chain ke saath bolo: 1/6 nomination, 213 ordinance, 161 pardon, 356 President's Rule.\n TRICK — “161 Governor vs 72 President”\n💡\nSource notes: Governor can use suspension, remission, commutation under Art.161.\nSource notes also mark death-penalty pardon and Court Martial jurisdiction as unavailable to Governor.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.13 State Executive\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPardoning trap: President = Art.72; Governor = Art.161. Source notes: Governor death-penalty pardon nahi kar sakta.\n8. Chief Minister — State Ka PM\n• CM = Real Executive Head of State\n• Head of Council of Ministers\n• Leader of majority party in Legislative Assembly\n• Chairman of State Planning Board\n• Minimum age = 25 years\n• Must be citizen of India\n• Must be MLA/MLC or become State Legislature member within 6 months\n• Appointed by Governor — Article 164(1)\n• Term = 5 years, at pleasure of Governor\n• Resignation = Governor\n• Salary = decided by State Legislature\n• Oath = Governor\n TRICK — “25 - 6 - 164(1) - Governor”\n💡\nCM number line: age 25 -> non-member window 6 months -> appointment Art.164(1) -> Governor.\nRole hook: CM = State ka PM = Real Executive.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCM MLA ya MLC dono ho sakta hai; agar member nahi hai to 6 months ke andar State Legislature member banna zaroori.\n9. CM Powers — P-D-A-R-C\n• Presides over Council of Ministers meetings\n• Distributes portfolios among ministers\n• Advises Governor to summon/dissolve Legislative Assembly\n• Recommends appointments & removals of ministers\n• Crisis Manager-in-Chief during emergency\n TRICK — “P-D-A-R-C”\n💡\nPreside -> Distribute -> Advise -> Recommend -> Crisis.\nCM = Governor aur Council ke beech operational centre.\n TRICK — “PM informs President; CM informs Governor”\n💡\nSource notes parallel structure ko highlight karte hain: Centre mein PM-President, State mein CM-Governor.\n10. State Council of Ministers — 15% Max, 12 Min\n• Role = Aid & Advise Governor\n• Members must be State Legislature members, or become within 6 months\n• Appointed by Governor on advice of CM\n• Collectively responsible to State Legislative Assembly\n• Maximum strength = 15% of Legislative Assembly\n• Minimum strength = 12 Ministers\n• 91st Amendment linked with max 15% + min 12\n• Delhi special in source notes = max 10% of Assembly or 7, whichever is less — 69th Amendment","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.13 State Executive\n TRICK — “State CoM = 15 Max, 12 Min”\n💡\nCentre yaad ho to State ka extra number sirf 12 add karo.\n91st Amendment = 15% ceiling + minimum 12 at State level.\n TRICK — “Delhi = 10% or 7”\n💡\nSource notes ka Delhi special rule: 10% of Assembly OR 7, whichever is less.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nState CoM ka minimum 12 source notes mein specifically diya hai.\n11. Advocate General — Article 165\n• Highest Legal Officer of State\n• Article 165\n• Appointed by Governor\n• Qualification = qualified to be Judge of High Court\n• Advises State Government on legal matters\n• Can participate in State Legislature proceedings\n• Cannot vote\n• Term = at pleasure of Governor\n TRICK — “165 = State Legal Officer; HC-Level; Speak, No Vote”\n💡\nAdvocate General ka 3-point hook: Art.165, High Court Judge qualification, Legislature mein participate but no vote.\n TRICK — “76 + 89 = 165”\n💡\nSource notes ka memory relation: Attorney General Art.76 + 89 = Advocate General Art.165.\nAG Centre = SC Judge qualification; Advocate General State = HC Judge qualification.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nAdvocate General ki qualification SC Judge level nahi; source notes mein High Court Judge level hai.\n12. Centre vs State — 7 Fast Contrasts\n• President / Governor = Constitutional Heads\n• PM / CM = Real Executives\n• AG Art.76 / Advocate General Art.165\n• Oath: President CJI / Governor HC Chief Justice\n• Head resignation: President -> VP / Governor -> President\n• Pardon: Art.72 / Art.161\n• Ordinance: Art.123 / Art.213\n TRICK — “CJI-HC | VP-President | 72-161 | 123-213”\n💡\nExam se pehle ye 4 contrast-pairs bolna enough hai to common swaps bach jayein.\n13. Key Article Ladder — State Executive Fast Map\n• 153 = Governors of States\n• 154 = Executive Power of State\n• 155 = Governor appointment by President\n• 157 = Governor qualifications\n• 160 = Duties in contingency","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.13 State Executive\n• 161 = Governor pardoning power\n• 162 = Extent of State executive power\n• 163 = CoM to aid & advise Governor\n• 164(1) = CM appointed by Governor\n• 165 = Advocate General\n• 166 = Governor / conduct of State executive business in source notes\n• 213 = Governor ordinance power\n• 233 = Subordinate Court judges appointment\n TRICK — “153 Start -> 155 Appoint -> 157 Qualify -> 161 Pardon -> 163 Advice -> 164 CM -> 165 AG -> 213\n💡\nOrdinance”\nPoora ladder ratne ke bajay anchor articles pakdo: 155, 157, 161, 163, 164, 165, 213.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part VI 153–167, components G-C-C-A -> Governor nominal, CM real -> Centre mirror: P-G, PM-CM, 76-165, 72-161, 123-213 ->\nGovernor: 35, President appoint, 5-but-pleasure, resignation President, ₹3.5L CF-State, oath HC-CJ -> 7th/1956 one Governor\nmultiple States -> appointments P-A-E-J-V-F -> powers 1/6, 213 ordinance, 161 pardon, 356 rule -> CM: age25, 6-month rule,\nArt164(1), oath/resign Governor -> CM powers P-D-A-R-C -> State CoM 15% max, 12 min, Delhi 10% or 7 -> Advocate General\nArt165, HC-level, participate/no vote -> article anchors 155-157-161-163-164-165-213.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nGovernor oath = HC Chief Justice, not CJI | Governor = at pleasure of President | State CoM = max 15%, minimum 12.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.14 State Legislature\nSTATE LEGISLATURE — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 14 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Vidhan Sabha vs Vidhan Parishad, 6 bicameral states, Parishad election fractions, bills, presiding\nofficers, privileges, sessions aur key Articles ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — Part VI, 168–212\n• State Legislature = Part VI, Articles 168–212\n• Unicameral = only Vidhan Sabha\n• Bicameral = Vidhan Sabha + Vidhan Parishad\n• Source notes: 22 States + 3 UTs unicameral\n• Source notes: only 6 States bicameral\n• Vidhan Sabha minimum = 60 | Vidhan Parishad minimum = 40\n TRICK — “168–212, One House or Two”\n💡\nChapter start anchor: Part VI + Articles 168–212.\nHouse-count trick: Unicameral = Sabha only; Bicameral = Sabha + Parishad.\n2. 6 Bicameral States — K-B-U-M-A-T\n• Karnataka\n• Bihar\n• Uttar Pradesh\n• Maharashtra\n• Andhra Pradesh\n• Telangana\n TRICK — “K B U M A T”\n💡\nKarnataka – Bihar – UP – Maharashtra – Andhra – Telangana.\nMnemonic sentence: “KaBi UP Mein Andhra Telangana.”\nBihar ko extra star karo — source notes BPSC ke liye must-know bolte hain.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSource notes ke according Vidhan Parishad sirf in 6 States mein hai.\n3. Vidhan Sabha vs Vidhan Parishad — Lower vs Upper\n• Vidhan Sabha = Lower House, directly elected, dissolvable\n• Vidhan Parishad = Upper House, permanent\n• VS minimum 60, maximum 500\n• VP minimum 40, maximum not more than 1/3 of VS\n• VS term = 5 years | VP member term = 6 years\n• VP = 1/3 members retire every 2 years\n• VS minimum age = 25 | VP minimum age = 30\n TRICK — “VS = LS; VP = RS”\n💡\nVidhan Sabha ko Lok Sabha ka state-version samjho: Lower, age 25, term 5, dissolve ho sakti.\nVidhan Parishad ko Rajya Sabha ka state-version samjho: Upper, age 30, term 6, permanent, 1/3 every 2 years.\n TRICK — “60/500/25/5 vs 40/1/3/30/6”\n💡\nVS number pack: 60 min, 500 max, age 25, term 5.\nVP number pack: 40 min, max 1/3 of VS, age 30, term 6.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.14 State Legislature\n4. Vidhan Sabha Exceptions — S-G-M\n• Sikkim = 32 seats\n• Goa = 40 seats\n• Mizoram = 40 seats\n• These are exceptions to the general minimum 60 rule in source notes\n• Largest Assembly = Uttar Pradesh, 403 seats\n• Bihar Vidhan Sabha = 243 seats\n TRICK — “SGM = 32–40–40”\n💡\nSikkim 32, Goa 40, Mizoram 40.\nUP = 403 largest; Bihar = 243.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nGeneral VS minimum 60, but source notes list Sikkim, Goa and Mizoram below 60.\n5. Vidhan Sabha Powers — E-L-E-F\n• Executive\n controls State Government & Council of Ministers\n→\n• Legislative\n laws on State List & Concurrent List\n→\n• Election-related\n MLAs participate in President election; elect Rajya Sabha members\n→\n• Financial\n Budget only in Legislative Assembly; taxes/withdrawal need Assembly approval\n→\n TRICK — “E-L-E-F = Executive, Legislative, Election, Financial”\n💡\nVidhan Sabha ke four power-buckets ko ELEF bolo.\nMoney/budget ka final practical control Sabha ke paas.\n6. Vidhan Parishad — Creation/Abolition Process\n• Creation/Abolition power lies with Parliament\n• Process starts in Legislative Assembly\n• Assembly passes resolution by Special Majority\n• Then Parliament passes by Simple Majority\n TRICK — “State Starts Special, Parliament Passes Simple”\n💡\nStep 1: Vidhan Sabha = Special Majority.\nStep 2: Parliament = Simple Majority.\nPower Parliament ke paas, process start Assembly se.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCreate/abolish power ko State Legislature ka final power mat samajhna — final legislative power Parliament ke paas.\n7. Vidhan Parishad Election — 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/12 + 1/12 + 1/6\n• 1/3\n elected by Legislative Assembly members (MLAs)\n→\n• 1/3\n elected by Local Bodies\n→\n• 1/12\n elected by Teachers\n→\n• 1/12\n elected by Graduates\n→\n• 1/6\n nominated by Governor (Literature, Arts, Science, Social Service)\n→\n TRICK — “MLA–Local–Teacher–Graduate–Governor”\n💡\nOrder ko M-L-T-G-G bolo.\nFractions: 1/3, 1/3, 1/12, 1/12, 1/6.\nCheck: 4/12 + 4/12 + 1/12 + 1/12 + 2/12 = 12/12.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.14 State Legislature\n TRICK — “RS = 12 fixed; VP = 1/6 fraction”\n💡\nRajya Sabha mein President 12 nominate karta hai.\nVidhan Parishad mein Governor 1/6 nominate karta hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nGovernor nomination in VP = 1/6 fraction, fixed 12 nahi.\n8. Presiding Officers — Sabha vs Parishad\n• Vidhan Sabha\n Speaker + Deputy Speaker\n→\n• Speaker resignation\n Deputy Speaker\n→\n• Speaker removal\n Legislative Assembly; 14 days prior notice\n→\n• Speaker oath\n Governor\n→\n• Vidhan Parishad\n Chairman + Deputy Chairman\n→\n• VP Chairman elected from among Vidhan Parishad members\n• Chairman absent\n Deputy Chairman presides\n→\n TRICK — “Speaker: Deputy–14–Governor”\n💡\nSpeaker ka 3-hook pack: resign to Deputy Speaker, removal notice 14 days, oath by Governor.\n TRICK — “RS Chairman Non-member; VP Chairman Member”\n💡\nRajya Sabha Chairman = Vice-President of India, non-member.\nVidhan Parishad Chairman = elected from its own members, so member.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nVP Chairman ko Vice-President of India mat samajhna — State Vidhan Parishad Chairman elected member hota hai.\n9. Bills at State Level — 4 Months vs 14 Days\nOrdinary Bill\n• Can be introduced in either House\n• Final authority rests with Legislative Assembly\n• Vidhan Parishad delay = 3 months first time + 1 month after re-passage\n• Total maximum delay = 4 months\n• No Joint Sitting at State level\n TRICK — “Ordinary = 3 + 1 = 4, No Joint”\n💡\nVP ka ordinary bill delay formula: 3 months + 1 month = 4 months max.\nCentre mein Joint Sitting ho sakti hai; source notes ke according State mein NO Joint Sitting.\nMoney Bill\n• Introduced only in Legislative Assembly\n• Vidhan Parishad can withhold for 14 days only\n• VP has no amendment power\n• Budget also only in Legislative Assembly\n• Money Bill status decided by Speaker of Vidhan Sabha\n TRICK — “Money = Sabha Only, Speaker, 14”\n💡\nMoney Bill: Assembly only\n Speaker decides\n Parishad 14 days.\n→\n→\nCentre RS ka 14-day rule aur State VP ka 14-day rule same.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.14 State Legislature\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nState level par Joint Sitting provision nahi; ordinary bill delay 4 months max, Money Bill 14 days.\n10. Privileges — Article 194 vs Parliament 105\n• State Legislature privileges = Article 194\n• Parliament privileges = Article 105\n• Civil arrest immunity in source notes = 40 days before + session + 40 days after\n• Criminal arrest allowed\n• Freedom of Speech in Legislature\n• House proceedings protected from court challenge in source notes\n TRICK — “194 State, 105 Parliament”\n💡\n194 ko State ke saath, 105 ko Parliament ke saath lock karo.\n TRICK — “40–Session–40, Civil Only”\n💡\nCivil arrest protection ko session ke dono side 40–40 days ki image se yaad karo.\nCriminal arrest allowed.\n11. Sessions — Governor vs Presiding Officer\n• Minimum 2 sessions every year\n• Prorogation = Governor\n• Adjournment = Presiding Officer (Speaker/Chairman)\n• Dissolution = Governor, only Vidhan Sabha\n• Vidhan Parishad permanent; not dissolved\n TRICK — “P = Governor, A = Presiding Officer, D = Governor/Sabha only”\n💡\nProrogation Governor; Adjournment Presiding Officer.\nDissolution only Vidhan Sabha ki — Parishad permanent.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nProrogation aur Adjournment ko swap mat karna.\n12. Key Articles — 168 to 212 Fast Map\n• 168\n Composition of State Legislature\n→\n• 169\n Creation/Abolition of Legislative Councils\n→\n• 170\n Structure of Legislative Assembly\n→\n• 172\n Duration of State Legislatures\n→\n• 173\n Qualifications\n→\n• 178\n Speaker & Deputy Speaker\n→\n• 179\n Vacancy/Resignation of Speaker\n→\n• 182\n Chairman & Deputy Chairman of Vidhan Parishad\n→\n• 191\n Disqualification\n→\n• 194\n Privileges\n→\n• 195\n Salaries & Allowances\n→\n• 199\n Money Bill\n→\n• 202\n Annual Financial Statement (State Budget)\n→\n• 212\n Courts not to inquire into legislative proceedings\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.14 State Legislature\n TRICK — “168 Legislature, 169 Parishad, 170 Sabha”\n💡\nFirst 3 anchors straight: 168 State Legislature, 169 Vidhan Parishad create/abolish, 170 Vidhan Sabha.\nFinancial anchors: 199 Money Bill, 202 State Budget.\n13. +89 Memory Bridge — Parliament to State\n• Parliament Art. 79 + 89 = State Legislature Art. 168\n• Rajya Sabha Art. 80 + 89 = Vidhan Parishad Art. 169\n• Lok Sabha Art. 81 + 89 = Vidhan Sabha Art. 170\n TRICK — “79>168, 80>169, 81>170”\n💡\nSource notes ka +89 memory trick state-legislature article numbers yaad karne ke liye use karo.\nYe mnemonic hai, constitutional provision nahi.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part VI 168–212\n Unicameral Sabha only, Bicameral Sabha+Parishad\n 6 states K-B-U-M-A-T\n VS = LS:\n→\n→\n→\n60/500/25/5/dissolve; VP = RS: 40/max1/3/30/6/permanent + 1/3 every2\n VS exceptions SGM=32-40-40, UP403, Bihar243\n\n→\n→\nParishad create: VS Special then Parliament Simple\n VP election M-L-T-G-G = 1/3,1/3,1/12,1/12,1/6\n Speaker: Deputy-14-\n→\n→\nGovernor; VP Chairman = member\n Ordinary Bill 3+1=4, no Joint; Money Bill Assembly only, Speaker, 14 days\n Privileges\n→\n→\n194 vs Parliament105, Civil 40-session-40\n Sessions: Prorogation Governor, Adjourn Presiding Officer\n Articles 168\n→\n→\nLegislature,169 Parishad,170 Sabha,199 Money,202 Budget\n +89 bridge 79-168,80-169,81-170.”\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\n6 bicameral States = K-B-U-M-A-T | VP nomination = Governor 1/6, not fixed 12 | State level has NO Joint Sitting.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.15 Supreme Court of India\nSUPREME COURT OF INDIA — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 15 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Supreme Court ke judge-strength, Collegium/NJAC, qualifications, retirement, removal process,\njurisdictions aur key Articles ko exam-confusion ke hisaab se fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — Part V, 124–147, 34, 65, Delhi\n• Supreme Court = Part V, Articles 124–147\n• Highest Court of India\n• Total judges in source notes = 34 (1 CJI + 33 others)\n• Retirement age = 65 years\n• Seat = Delhi — Article 130\n• Court of Record = Article 129\n• SC decisions binding on all courts = Article 141\n• Judge strength can be increased/decreased by Parliament; Constitution does not fix the number\n TRICK — “124–147 | 34 | 65 | 129 Record | 130 Delhi | 141 Binding”\n💡\nChapter ka number spine isi line mein yaad karo.\nJudge-count trap: originally 8, source notes mein current strength 34; number Parliament decide karta hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSC judge strength Constitution mein fixed nahi — Parliament decide karta hai.\n2. Supreme Court Firsts — K-B-M-R\n• Harilal J. Kania\n First Chief Justice of India, 1950\n→\n• Fathima Beevi\n First Woman Supreme Court Judge, 1989\n→\n• Indu Malhotra\n First woman advocate directly elevated from Bar to SC, 2018\n→\n• Justice V. Ramaswami\n First removal/impeachment attempt, 1991; not passed\n→\n TRICK — “Kania–Beevi–Malhotra–Ramaswami”\n💡\nFirsts ko K-B-M-R chain mein bolo.\nRole anchors: First CJI\n First Woman Judge\n Direct from Bar\n First Removal Attempt.\n→\n→\n→\n3. Collegium System — CJI + 4\n• Other judges appointed by President\n• Source notes describe CJI appointment through Collegium System / senior-most judge convention\n• Collegium = CJI + 4 senior-most Supreme Court judges\n• Third Judges Case, 1998\n Collegium System confirmed\n→\n TRICK — “CJI + FOUR = Collegium Five”\n💡\nCJI ko centre mein rakho aur 4 senior-most judges uske around — total 5.\nYear hook: Third Judges Case = 1998.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCollegium ka core number = CJI + 4 senior-most SC judges.\n4. NJAC — 99th Added, 2015 Struck\n• NJAC = National Judicial Appointment Commission\n• Added by 99th Constitutional Amendment","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.15 Supreme Court of India\n• Struck down by Supreme Court in 2015\n• Case in source notes = Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India\n• After strike-down, Collegium System remained valid\n TRICK — “99 Added — 15 Invalid”\n💡\n99th Amendment se NJAC aaya; 2015 mein SC ne strike down kiya.\nMemory line: “Ninety-Nine came, Twenty-Fifteen went.”\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSource notes ke according NJAC valid system nahi; Collegium remains valid.\n5. SC Judge Qualification — 5 / 10 / Jurist\n• Citizen of India\n• High Court Judge for 5 years\n• High Court Advocate for 10 years\n• OR distinguished jurist in President's opinion\n• No minimum age prescribed in Constitution (as highlighted in source notes)\n• Retirement age = 65 years\n• HC judge retirement age comparison in notes = 62 years\n TRICK — “5 Judge, 10 Advocate, Jurist Option — Retire 65”\n💡\nQualification number pair: Judge 5 years, Advocate 10 years.\nAge trap: minimum age NO; retirement 65.\n TRICK — “SC 65 vs HC 62”\n💡\nSC judge retirement 65; HC judge retirement 62.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSC Judge ke liye minimum age source notes mein prescribed nahi; retirement age 65 hai.\n6. Conditions & Salary — President, 65, CFI\n• Oath / Resignation\n President (as stated in source notes)\n→\n• Acting CJI\n appointed by President when CJI absent\n→\n• Tenure\n until 65 years of age\n→\n• Salary source\n Consolidated Fund of India\n→\n• Salary cannot be reduced during service\n• Source notes' highlighted trap: retired SC judge cannot practice in Supreme Court — Article 124(7)\n• CJI salary in source notes = ₹2.8 lakh/month\n• Other SC Judges = ₹2.5 lakh/month\n TRICK — “President–65–CFI–2.8/2.5”\n💡\nOffice-condition pack: President, age 65, Consolidated Fund of India.\nSalary pair: CJI 2.8, Others 2.5.\n TRICK — “5 > 3.5 > 2.8 > 2.5” Salary Ladder\n💡\nSource notes comparison: President ₹5L > Governor ₹3.5L > CJI ₹2.8L > SC Judge ₹2.5L.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSource notes repeatedly highlight Art.124(7): retired SC judge Supreme Court mein practice nahi kar sakta.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.15 Supreme Court of India\n7. Removal of SC Judge — 100 / 50 / 3 / Special\n• Motion in Lok Sabha\n minimum 100 members sign\n→\n• Motion in Rajya Sabha\n minimum 50 members sign\n→\n• Admission\n Speaker (LS) / Chairman (RS), discretion\n→\n• Inquiry Committee\n 3 members\n→\n• Special Majority\n 2/3 members present & voting + majority of total membership\n→\n• President issues removal order\n• Grounds = misconduct or incapacity\n• Governed by Judges Inquiry Act, 1968\n TRICK — “100–50–3–2/3–President”\n💡\nRemoval chain: LS100 / RS50\n 3-member inquiry\n special majority\n President's order.\n→\n→\n→\nAct year = 1968.\n TRICK — “Ramaswami 1991 = Attempt, Not Success”\n💡\nSource notes: Justice V. Ramaswami first removal attempt; motion did not pass.\nSource notes state no SC/HC judge removal/impeachment has succeeded so far.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPresident judge ko apni marzi se remove nahi karta; Parliament process/recommendation necessary.\n8. Jurisdiction Map — O-A-R-W-A/T\n• Original Jurisdiction\n Article 131\n Centre-State / State-State disputes\n→\n→\n• Appellate Jurisdiction\n Articles 132–134\n→\n• Review Jurisdiction\n Article 137\n→\n• Writ Jurisdiction\n Article 32\n→\n• Advisory Jurisdiction\n Article 143\n→\n• Power of Transfer / expansion\n Article 138\n→\n• Binding decisions\n Article 141\n→\n TRICK — “131 Original, 132-134 Appeal, 137 Review, 143 Advice”\n💡\nJurisdiction ko ascending article chain mein dekho.\n131 = Original; 132/133/134 = Constitutional/Civil/Criminal appeals; 137 = Review; 143 = Advice.\n9. Appellate Jurisdiction — 132 C, 133 Civil, 134 Crime\n• Article 132\n Constitutional appellate matters\n→\n• Article 133\n Civil appellate matters\n→\n• Article 134\n Criminal appellate matters\n→\n TRICK — “132 Constitution, 133 Civil, 134 Criminal”\n💡\nC-C-C sequence ko meaning ke saath bolo: Constitution\n Civil\n Criminal.\n→\n→\nNumbers seedhe 132, 133, 134.\n10. Article 143 Advisory — Ask, SC Can Refuse\n• President can seek SC opinion on public-importance issues\n• Advisory opinion is NOT binding\n• President can ask; SC can refuse","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.15 Supreme Court of India\n TRICK — “143 = ASK, Not ORDER”\n💡\nAdvisory ko 'advice' hi samjho — binding command nahi.\nPresident ASK kar sakta hai; SC REFUSE kar sakta hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArticle 143 opinion binding nahi.\n11. Article 32 vs 226 — SC Narrower, HC Wider\n• Article 32\n Supreme Court writ jurisdiction\n→\n• Article 32\n Fundamental Rights violations only\n→\n• Article 226\n High Court\n Fundamental Rights + Legal Rights\n→\n→\n• Source notes: HC jurisdiction wider\n• Article 32 itself is a Fundamental Right\n TRICK — “32 = FR Only; 226 = FR + Legal”\n💡\nSC Article 32 ko FR-only gate samjho.\nHC Article 226 ka gate wider hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArt.32 aur Art.226 ko equal scope mat samjho; source notes HC scope wider batate hain.\n12. Court of Record, Seat, Binding — 129 / 130 / 141\n• Article 129\n Supreme Court as Court of Record; contempt power\n→\n• Article 130\n Seat of Supreme Court = Delhi\n→\n• Article 141\n SC decisions binding on all courts in India\n→\n TRICK — “129 Record, 130 Room/Delhi, 141 Rule for All”\n💡\n129 = Record, 130 = Seat/Delhi, 141 = binding law for all courts.\n13. Key Article Ladder — 124 to 147\n• 124\n Composition & Appointment of SC Judges\n→\n• 125\n Salary of SC Judges\n→\n• 129\n Court of Record\n→\n• 130\n Seat of SC\n→\n• 131\n Original Jurisdiction\n→\n• 132\n Constitutional Appeal\n→\n• 133\n Civil Appeal\n→\n• 134\n Criminal Appeal\n→\n• 137\n Review\n→\n• 138\n Expansion / Transfer\n→\n• 139\n Grant of Writ Powers\n→\n• 141\n Decisions binding\n→\n• 143\n Advisory Jurisdiction\n→\n• 147\n Interpretation of Constitution\n→\n TRICK — “124 Compose > 129 Record > 130 Delhi > 131 Original > 132-134 Appeals > 137 Review > 141 Bind\n💡\n\n> 143 Advise”\nExam ke liye ye anchor articles enough hain poora map reconstruct karne ke liye.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.15 Supreme Court of India\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part V 124–147\n SC highest, 34 judges, retire65, Delhi130, Record129, Binding141, strength Parliament\n Firsts Kania-Beevi-\n→\n→\nMalhotra-Ramaswami\n Collegium CJI+4, Third Judges 1998\n NJAC 99th, struck 2015\n Qualification 5-year HC Judge / 10-\n→\n→\n→\nyear HC Advocate / Jurist, no min age, SC65 vs HC62\n Conditions President-65-CFI, salary 2.8/2.5\n Removal 100 LS / 50 RS / 3\n→\n→\n\ncommittee / 2/3+total majority / President, Act1968\n Jurisdiction 131 Original, 132-134 Appeals, 137 Review, 32 Writ, 143\n→\nAdvisory, 138 Transfer, 141 Binding\n 32 FR-only vs 226 FR+Legal.”\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\nJudge strength Parliament decides | SC judge minimum age not prescribed, retirement 65 | Art.143 advisory NOT\nbinding.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.16 High Courts & Subordinate Courts\nHIGH COURTS & SUBORDINATE COURTS — MEMORY TRICK\nSHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 16 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — High Court ke key Articles, SC-vs-HC differences, writ jurisdiction, UT mappings, subordinate-court\nappointments, special courts aur high-yield dates ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — Part VI, 214–232, 25, 7, 62\n• High Courts = Part VI, Articles 214–232\n• Total High Courts in source notes = 25\n• Multi-State High Courts = 7\n• Retirement age = 62 years\n• Court of Record = Article 215\n• Delhi & J&K/Ladakh = UTs with their own High Courts\n• Patna High Court = established 1916; Principal Bench Patna\n TRICK — “214–232 | 25 HCs | 7 Multi | 62 Retire | 215 Record”\n💡\nPoore chapter ka number spine: 214–232\n 25\n 7\n 62\n Art.215.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nBihar anchor: Patna HC = 1916.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSC retirement 65 vs HC retirement 62 — 3-year difference.\n2. Key Article Ladder — 214 to 233\n• 214\n High Court in each State\n→\n• 215\n High Court as Court of Record\n→\n• 216\n Constitution of High Court\n→\n• 217\n Appointment & Qualifications of HC Judges\n→\n• 219\n Oath/Affirmation\n→\n• 222\n Transfer of HC Judges\n→\n• 225\n Jurisdiction of HC\n→\n• 226\n Writ Jurisdiction\n→\n• 227\n Superintendence over Subordinate Courts\n→\n• 228\n Transfer of certain cases to HC\n→\n• 231\n One HC for two or more States\n→\n• 233\n District Judge appointment\n→\n TRICK — “214 Start, 215 Record, 217 Judge, 226 Writ, 227 Watch, 233 District”\n💡\nExam anchors ko isi order mein yaad karo.\n226 = Writ; 227 = Subordinate Courts par supervision; 233 = District Judge.\n3. HC Judge Qualification — 10 + 10, Retire 62\n• Citizen of India\n• 10 years as Advocate in a High Court\n• OR 10 years in Judicial Office in India\n• Appointment by President in consultation with CJI + Governor\n• Tenure until 62 years\n• Resignation to President\n• Oath administered by Governor","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.16 High Courts & Subordinate Courts\n• Acting Chief Justice appointed by President\n TRICK — “10 Advocate + 10 Judicial + 62 Retirement”\n💡\nHC judge qualification ka easiest code = 10/10/62.\nSC se contrast: SC qualification source notes mein 5-year HC Judge OR 10-year HC Advocate.\n TRICK — “Appoint President, Oath Governor, Resign President”\n💡\nAppointment aur resignation President; oath Governor.\nIs 3-step contrast ko ek line mein bolo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nHC judge ka oath Governor dilwata hai — President/CJI nahi.\n4. Salary & Pension — State Pays Salary, India Pays Pension\n• HC Chief Justice salary in source notes = ₹2.5 lakh/month\n• HC Other Judge = ₹2.25 lakh/month\n• Salary\n Consolidated Fund of State\n→\n• Pension\n Consolidated Fund of India\n→\n• Source notes comparison: SC Judge salary = HC Chief Justice salary = ₹2.5 lakh\n TRICK — “Salary STATE, Pension INDIA”\n💡\nHC ke liye salary aur pension ka source alag hai.\nSalary = State Fund; Pension = India Fund.\n TRICK — “2.8 > 2.5 = 2.5 > 2.25”\n💡\nSource salary ladder: SC CJI 2.8 > SC Judge 2.5 = HC CJ 2.5 > HC Judge 2.25.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nHC salary source aur pension source ko same mat samajhna.\n5. HC Jurisdiction — O-W-A-R-S\n• Original Jurisdiction\n FR enforcement, constitutional-case transfer, MP/MLA election disputes\n→\n• Writ Jurisdiction\n Article 226\n→\n• Appellate Jurisdiction\n Civil + Criminal matters\n→\n• Court of Record\n Article 215\n→\n• Superintendence over Subordinate Courts\n Article 227\n→\n• Death sentence must be confirmed by High Court before execution (as emphasized in source notes)\n TRICK — “O-W-A-R-S = Original, Writ, Appeal, Record, Superintendence”\n💡\nHC powers ko 5 buckets mein yaad karo.\nArticle anchors: Writ 226, Record 215, Superintendence 227.\n TRICK — “Death = HC Confirm”\n💡\nSource notes ka direct trap: death sentence execution se pehle HC confirmation mandatory.\n6. Article 226 vs Article 32 — Wider vs Fundamental\n• Article 226 (HC)\n Fundamental Rights + Other Legal Rights\n→\n• Article 32 (SC)\n Fundamental Rights only\n→\n• Source notes: HC writ jurisdiction is wider","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.16 High Courts & Subordinate Courts\n• Chandra Kumar Case\n Article 226 writ jurisdiction is part of Basic Structure\n→\n• Article 32 itself is a Fundamental Right\n TRICK — “226 = FR + Legal; 32 = FR Only”\n💡\nScope ke hisaab se 226 wider.\nNature ke hisaab se Article 32 khud Fundamental Right hai.\n TRICK — “Chandra Kumar = 226 Basic Structure”\n💡\nCase-name ko article se direct chipkao: Chandra Kumar\n Art.226\n Basic Structure.\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n226 aur 32 ko same scope mat samjho.\n7. SC vs HC — 3 Must-Know Differences\n• Oath\n SC Judge: President | HC Judge: Governor\n→\n• Retirement\n SC: 65 | HC: 62\n→\n• Salary Source\n SC: CF of India | HC: CF of State\n→\n• Pension Source\n both in source notes: CF of India\n→\n• Writ\n SC Art.32 FR only | HC Art.226 FR + Legal Rights\n→\n• Court of Record\n SC Art.129 | HC Art.215\n→\n TRICK — “Oath–Age–Salary = P/G, 65/62, India/State”\n💡\nExam se pehle sirf ye 3 contrast bolo:\nSC oath President / HC Governor; SC65 / HC62; SC salary India / HC salary State.\n8. UT to High Court Mapping — L-P-A First\n• Lakshadweep\n Kerala High Court (Kochi)\n→\n• Puducherry\n Madras High Court\n→\n• Andaman & Nicobar\n Calcutta High Court\n→\n• Dadra & Nagar Haveli + Daman & Diu\n Bombay High Court\n→\n• Chandigarh\n Punjab & Haryana High Court\n→\n• Delhi\n Delhi High Court (own)\n→\n• J&K + Ladakh\n J&K & Ladakh High Court (own)\n→\n TRICK — “L-K, P-M, A-C”\n💡\nMost common 3 pairs from source notes:\nLakshadweep-Kerala | Puducherry-Madras | Andaman-Calcutta.\n TRICK — “Dadra/Daman-Bombay; Chandigarh-P&H; Delhi/J&K Own”\n💡\nBaaki mapping ko 3 mini-blocks mein yaad karo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSource notes: own-HC UTs = Delhi and J&K/Ladakh.\n9. Subordinate Courts — Articles 233–237\n• District Judge appointment\n Governor in consultation with High Court\n→\n• Qualification\n at least 7 years as advocate\n→\n• Restriction in source notes\n must not be in Government service\n→\n• Recommendation\n High Court\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.16 High Courts & Subordinate Courts\n• Article 233\n District Judge appointment\n→\n• Article 235\n Control of High Court over Subordinate Courts\n→\n TRICK — “Governor Appoints, HC Recommends, 7 Years”\n💡\nDistrict Judge ke liye G-H-7 hook yaad karo:\nGovernor appoints\n High Court consult/recommend\n 7-year advocate.\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nDistrict Judge appoints Governor, President nahi.\n10. Subordinate Court Hierarchy — Civil, Criminal, Revenue\nCivil Side\n• District Judge\n• Subordinate / Civil Judge\n• Munsif / Junior Civil Judge\nCriminal Side\n• Chief Judicial Magistrate\n• Judicial Magistrate First Class\n• Judicial Magistrate Second Class\nRevenue Side\n• Revenue Board\n Commissioner\n Collector\n Tehsildar\n Naib Tehsildar\n→\n→\n→\n→\n TRICK — “Civil: D-S-M | Criminal: C-1-2 | Revenue: B-C-C-T-N”\n💡\nCivil ladder = District\n Subordinate\n Munsif.\n→\n→\nCriminal = CJM\n JM First\n JM Second.\n→\n→\nRevenue = Board\n Commissioner\n Collector\n Tehsildar\n Naib.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n11. Special Courts — Year & Purpose Hooks\n• Gram Nyayalayas\n village-level small disputes; State Govt in consultation with HC\n→\n• Lok Adalat\n quick/low-cost, mutual consent, award final & binding\n→\n• First Lok Adalat in source notes\n 6 Oct 1985, Delhi; Justice P.N. Bhagwati\n→\n• Fast-Track Courts\n speedy criminal cases; example POCSO Courts\n→\n• Mobile Court\n 'Justice on Wheels'; first at Mewat, Haryana (2007); APJ Abdul Kalam credited\n→\n• Consumer Courts\n consumer disputes\n→\n• NGT\n environment protection; established 2010; source notes say not a constitutional body\n→\n• Juvenile Courts\n child/juvenile justice; 3-member Board\n→\n• Court Martial\n military matters\n→\n TRICK — “Lok 1985, Mobile 2007, NGT 2010”\n💡\nSpecial-court dates ko increasing ladder mein bolo: 1985\n 2007\n 2010.\n→\n→\nLok = Bhagwati, Mobile = Kalam, NGT = Environment.\n TRICK — “Lok Award = Final, No Appeal”\n💡\nSource notes ka direct memory hook: Lok Adalat award binding/deemed decree; appeal nahi.\n12. High Court Timeline — Oldest, Bihar, Newest\n• Oldest 3 High Courts\n Madras, Calcutta, Bombay — all 1862\n→\n• Allahabad\n 1866; source notes call it largest HC by number of judges\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.16 High Courts & Subordinate Courts\n• Patna High Court\n 1916\n→\n• 2000 High Courts\n Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand, Jharkhand\n→\n• 2013 High Courts\n Meghalaya, Manipur, Tripura\n→\n• Newest in source notes\n Telangana + Andhra Pradesh, both 2019\n→\n TRICK — “MCB 1862 > Patna 1916 > C-U-J 2000 > M-M-T 2013 > T-A 2019”\n💡\nTimeline ko year-clusters mein yaad karo.\nOldest 3 = Madras-Calcutta-Bombay; Bihar anchor = Patna 1916.\n13. Bihar Focus — Patna High Court\n• Patna High Court = Bihar's High Court\n• Established = 1916\n• Principal Bench = Patna\n TRICK — “Bihar = Patna = 1916”\n💡\nBPSC ke liye ye 3-word fact direct lock karo.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part VI 214–232\n 25 HCs, 7 multi, retire62, Record215, Patna1916\n Articles: 214 HC, 215 Record, 217 Judges, 226 Writ, 227\n→\n→\nSuperintend, 233 District\n HC Judge 10 Advocate / 10 Judicial, appointment President+CJI+Governor, oath Governor, resign\n→\nPresident\n Salary State, Pension India; 2.5/2.25\n HC powers O-W-A-R-S; death sentence HC confirm\n 226 FR+Legal vs 32\n→\n→\n→\nFR only; Chandra Kumar=226 Basic Structure\n UT pairs L-K, P-M, A-C; Delhi/J&K own\n District Judge Governor + HC + 7\n→\n→\nyears\n Special: Lok1985-Bhagwati, Mobile2007-Kalam, NGT2010\n Oldest MCB1862, Patna1916, newest Telangana/AP2019.”\n→\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\nSC 65 vs HC 62 | HC oath by Governor, not President | Art.226 = FR + Legal Rights, wider than Art.32.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.17 Centre-State Relations\nCENTRE-STATE RELATIONS — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 17 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part XI, 3 relations, 3 Lists, Parliament-on-State-List situations, Finance Commission, Inter-State\nCouncil, Zonal Councils aur river-water disputes ke high-confusion facts ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — Part XI, 245–293, 3 Relations\n• Centre-State Relations = Part XI, Articles 245–293\n• 3 types = Legislative + Administrative + Financial\n• 3-List System based on Government of India Act, 1935\n• Division of subjects = Article 246 + 7th Schedule\n• Legislative Relations = Articles 245–255\n• Administrative Relations = Articles 256–263\n• Financial Relations = Articles 268–293\n TRICK — “L-A-F = Legislative, Administrative, Financial”\n💡\nArticle blocks yaad karo: 245–255 L, 256–263 A, 268–293 F.\nChapter number spine: XI\n 245–293\n L-A-F\n 3 Lists.\n→\n→\n→\n2. Centre-State Commissions — 66, 69, 83, 07\n• 1966\n Setalvad Committee\n Administrative Reforms Commission\n→\n→\n• 1969\n Rajamannar Committee\n Tamil Nadu government; more State autonomy\n→\n→\n• 1983\n Sarkaria Commission\n most important; recommended Inter-State Council\n→\n→\n• 2007\n Punchhi Commission\n reviewed Sarkaria recommendations\n→\n→\n TRICK — “Se-Ra-Sa-Pu = 66–69–83–07”\n💡\nSetalvad\n Rajamannar\n Sarkaria\n Punchhi.\n→\n→\n→\nMost important anchor = Sarkaria 1983.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSarkaria recommended Inter-State Council in 1983; Council itself was established in 1990.\n3. 3 Lists — Current vs Original Numbers\n• Union List\n Current 98 | Original 97\n→\n• State List\n Current 59 | Original 66\n→\n• Concurrent List\n Current 52 | Original 47\n→\n• Union examples\n Defence, Atomic Energy, Foreign Affairs, Citizenship, Banking\n→\n• State examples\n Public Order, Police, Health, Water, Land/Agriculture\n→\n• Concurrent examples\n Criminal Law, Marriage, Education, Forests, Labour\n→\n TRICK — “Current 98–59–52 | Original 97–66–47”\n💡\nCurrent numbers: 98 / 59 / 52.\nOriginal numbers: 97 / 66 / 47.\nState List ka number ghata; Union/Concurrent side additions hue — source notes ka memory point.\n TRICK — “U = Defence-Bank | S = Police-Health | C = Education-Forest”\n💡\nExamples ko category anchors se yaad karo, full list ratne ki zaroorat nahi.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.17 Centre-State Relations\n4. Concurrent Conflict — Article 254\n• Parliament + State Legislature both make law on Concurrent List\n Parliament law prevails\n→\n• Article 254\n• Exception in source notes\n State law with President's assent may prevail in that State\n→\n TRICK — “254 = Centre Wins, President-Assent Exception”\n💡\nDefault: Parliament prevails.\nException: President-assented State law can prevail within that State.\n5. Parliament on State List — National, States, Treaty, Emergency, Rule\n• Article 249\n National Interest\n Rajya Sabha 2/3 majority; valid 1 year, renewable\n→\n→\n• Agreement between two or more States\n States request Parliament (source table leaves article cell blank; overview\n→\nflags Art. 252 among exam articles)\n• Article 253\n International Treaties & Agreements\n→\n• Article 352\n National Emergency\n Parliament can legislate on entire State List (as stated in source notes)\n→\n→\n• Article 356\n President's Rule\n Parliament acts as State Legislature\n→\n→\n• Article 245\n Parliament can make laws for entire country + extra-territorial operation; State Legislature only within\n→\nState boundaries\n TRICK — “249 National – States Agree – 253 Treaty – 352 Emergency – 356 Rule”\n💡\nSituation chain yaad rakho: National Interest\n States' Agreement\n International Treaty\n Emergency\n President's Rule.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n249 ka special number pack: Rajya Sabha 2/3 + 1 year renewable.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArt.249 ke liye Rajya Sabha 2/3 majority chahiye; Lok Sabha majority enough nahi.\n6. Administrative Relations — 256–257 + All India Services\n• Article 256\n Centre can direct States to comply with Central laws\n→\n• Article 257\n Union control over States in certain cases; States cannot impede Union executive power\n→\n• All India Services in source notes = IAS, IPS, IFoS\n• Centre full control\n appointment, disciplinary action, promotion, training\n→\n• State immediate control\n suspension only\n→\n• Source notes: State cannot dismiss, remove or compulsorily retire AIS officer\n TRICK — “256 Direction, 257 Control”\n💡\n256 = Centre says 'follow Central law'.\n257 = Union executive power ko State obstruct na kare.\n TRICK — “State = Suspend Only; Centre = Career Control”\n💡\nAIS trap: State ka immediate power = suspension only.\nAppointment/discipline/promotion/training = Centre.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSource notes AIS par State ko dismissal/removal/compulsory retirement power nahi dete.\n7. Financial Relations — 268, 269A, 279A, 280\n• Article 268\n Duties levied by Union, collected by States; example Stamp Duty\n→\n• Article 269A\n Tax distribution between Union & States\n→\n• Article 279A\n GST Council\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.17 Centre-State Relations\n• GST Council\n 101st Amendment, 2016\n→\n• GST Council Chairman = Union Finance Minister\n• Article 280\n Finance Commission\n→\n TRICK — “268 Levy-Union/Collect-State > 269A Tax > 279A GST > 280 FC”\n💡\nFinancial articles ko ascending ladder mein bolo.\nGST anchor: 279A + 101st Amendment + 2016 + Union Finance Minister.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArt.268 mein source notes ka key trap: Union levies, States collect and retain.\n8. Finance Commission — 280, 5 Years, 1+4\n• Article 280\n• Nature = Quasi-judicial body\n• Constituted by President every 5 years or earlier\n• Composition = 1 Chairman + 4 Members\n• First Chairman = K.C. Neogy\n• Source notes list 16th Finance Commission Chairman = Arvind Panagariya\n• Functions = tax distribution, grants-in-aid, augment State funds for Panchayats/Municipalities\n• Recommendations = advisory, not binding\n TRICK — “280 – President – 5 – 1+4 – Neogy”\n💡\nFinance Commission number pack: Art.280, every 5 years, 1 Chairman + 4 members.\nNature = Quasi-judicial; recommendation = advisory.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nFinance Commission recommendations binding nahi — advisory hain.\n9. Inter-State Council — Sarkaria 1983, Established 1990\n• Article 263\n• Established by President\n• Recommended by Sarkaria Commission (1983–87)\n• Established in 1990 under PM V.P. Singh\n• Source notes: reconstituted 19 May 2022\n• Meetings = at least 3 times a year\n• Chairman = Prime Minister\n• Members include all State CMs, CMs of UTs with legislatures, UT administrators, 6 Cabinet Ministers\n TRICK — “263 > 83 Recommend > 90 Establish > PM Chair”\n💡\nISC ka four-step chain: Article 263\n Sarkaria 1983\n established 1990\n Chairman PM.\n→\n→\n→\nMeetings hook = at least 3/year.\n TRICK — “PM = Inter-State Council; Home Minister = Zonal Councils”\n💡\nChairman confusion ko isi pair se lock karo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n1983 recommendation year hai; establishment year 1990.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.17 Centre-State Relations\n10. Zonal Councils — 5 Zones + Separate NE Council\n• Formed under States Reorganization Act, 1956\n• Chairman of all Zonal Councils = Union Home Minister\n• 5 Zonal Councils = Northern, Central, Eastern, Western, Southern\n• North-Eastern Council = separate; not one of the 5\n• Northern HQ = New Delhi\n• Central HQ = Prayagraj\n• Eastern HQ = Kolkata\n• Western HQ = Mumbai\n• Southern HQ = Chennai\n TRICK — “N-C-E-W-S = Delhi–Prayagraj–Kolkata–Mumbai–Chennai”\n💡\nZone order: Northern, Central, Eastern, Western, Southern.\nHQ order: Delhi, Prayagraj, Kolkata, Mumbai, Chennai.\n TRICK — “Bihar = EAST = Kolkata”\n💡\nEastern Zone = Bihar + Jharkhand + Odisha + West Bengal.\nBPSC anchor: Bihar\n Eastern Zonal Council\n HQ Kolkata.\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNE Council ko 6th Zonal Council mat samjho — source notes ise separate body batate hain.\n11. North-Eastern Council — 1972, 8 States\n• Established 1972 under North-Eastern Council Act, 1971\n• Initially 7 States\n• Currently 8 States in source notes\n• Sikkim added in 2002\n• Members in source notes\n 1 Chairman + 3 other Members + 8 CMs & Governors\n→\n TRICK — “NEC: Act 71, Start 72, Sikkim 02, Total 8”\n💡\nYear chain: 1971 Act\n 1972 established\n 2002 Sikkim added\n 8 States.\n→\n→\n→\n12. Inter-State River Water Disputes — Article 262\n• Constitutional provision = Article 262\n• Statutory basis = Inter-State River Water Disputes Act, 1956; source notes mention amendment 2017\n• Composition in source notes = 1 Chairperson + 1 Vice-Chairperson + maximum 6 members\n• Chairperson term = 5 years; VP/members till dispute resolved\n• Article 262 allows Parliament by law to exclude Supreme Court jurisdiction\n TRICK — “262 = River Dispute, SC Can Be Shut Out”\n💡\nArt.262 ka biggest trap = Parliament SC jurisdiction exclude kar sakti hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nRiver-water dispute mein SC jurisdiction exclusion source notes ka special exam point hai.\n13. River Tribunal Timeline — 1969 Triple\n• Krishna Water Dispute I\n 1969\n→\n• Godavari Water Dispute\n 1969\n→\n• Narmada Water Dispute\n 1969\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.17 Centre-State Relations\n• Ravi & Beas\n 1986\n→\n• Cauvery\n 1990\n→\n• Mahanadi\n 2018\n→\n TRICK — “KGN = 1969 Triple”\n💡\nKrishna + Godavari + Narmada = same year 1969.\nThen Ravi-Beas 1986\n Cauvery 1990\n Mahanadi 2018.\n→\n→\n TRICK — “Cauvery = K-K-T-P”\n💡\nSource notes states: Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Puducherry.\n TRICK — “Mahanadi = O-C”\n💡\nOdisha + Chhattisgarh; source notes list 2018.\n14. 20-Second Article Flash Chain\n• 245\n territory / extra-territorial Parliament power\n→\n• 246\n subject division + 7th Schedule\n→\n• 249\n National Interest, RS 2/3, 1 year\n→\n• 253\n International Treaties\n→\n• 254\n Concurrent conflict\n→\n• 256\n Centre directions\n→\n• 257\n Union control\n→\n• 262\n River disputes\n→\n• 263\n Inter-State Council\n→\n• 268\n Union levy / State collection\n→\n• 279A\n GST Council\n→\n• 280\n Finance Commission\n→\n TRICK — “245 Territory – 246 Lists – 249 National – 253 Treaty – 254 Conflict – 256 Direction – 257 Control\n💡\n\n– 262 River – 263 Council – 268 Tax – 279A GST – 280 FC”\nArticle numbers ko subject-word ke saath rhythm mein bolo; poora chapter reconstruct ho jayega.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part XI 245–293\n L-A-F relations; 3 Lists from 1935 Act, Art246+7th\n Commissions Se-Ra-Sa-Pu 66-69-83-07\n Lists current\n→\n→\n→\n98-59-52 vs original 97-66-47; conflict254 Parliament prevails\n State List situations: 249 National (RS2/3,1yr), States agree,\n→\n253 Treaty, 352 Emergency, 356 Rule; 245 extra-territorial\n Admin 256 Direction,257 Control; AIS State suspend-only\n\n→\n→\nFinancial 268 Union levy/State collect,269A tax,279A GST/101st-2016/FM,280 FC\n FC 5\n→\nyears,1+4,Neogy,quasi-judicial/advisory\n ISC 263: Sarkaria83, established90, PM chair\n Zonal Councils 5, Home Minister;\n→\n→\nN-C-E-W-S HQ Delhi-Prayagraj-Kolkata-Mumbai-Chennai; Bihar East-Kolkata; NEC separate 1972/8\n River Art262, SC\n→\nexclusion; KGN1969 triple, Ravi86, Cauvery90, Mahanadi2018.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nSarkaria 1983 recommended ISC, established 1990 | Art.249 needs RS 2/3 | Inter-State Council Chair = PM, Zonal Councils\nChair = Union Home Minister.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.18 Emergency Provisions\nEMERGENCY PROVISIONS — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 18 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part XVIII, Articles 352–360, 3 emergencies, 44th Amendment safeguards, Art.358 vs 359,\nPresident's Rule, Financial Emergency aur key case-law traps ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — Part XVIII, 352–360, 3 Emergencies\n• Part XVIII\n Articles 352–360\n→\n• 3 types\n National Emergency, President's Rule, Financial Emergency\n→\n• Source\n Weimar Constitution (Germany) + Government of India Act, 1935\n→\n• Proclamation by President\n entire country or any part thereof\n→\n• National Emergency = Article 352\n• President's Rule = Article 356\n• Financial Emergency = Article 360\n TRICK — “352–356–360 = National–State–Financial”\n💡\nTeen articles ko 4-4 ke jump mein yaad karo: 352\n 356\n 360.\n→\n→\nType mapping: National\n President's Rule\n Financial.\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nEmergency source = Germany + GoI Act 1935 — dono yaad rakho.\n2. National Emergency — 3 Times Declared\n• 1962\n Chinese Aggression\n President Dr. S. Radhakrishnan\n→\n→\n• 1971\n India-Pakistan War\n President V.V. Giri\n→\n→\n• 1975\n Internal Disturbance\n President Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed\n→\n→\n• 1975 PM = Indira Gandhi\n• 44th Amendment, 1978 replaced 'Internal Disturbance' with 'Armed Rebellion'\n TRICK — “62 China – 71 Pakistan – 75 Internal”\n💡\nYears ko conflict ke saath lock karo: 1962 China, 1971 Pakistan, 1975 Internal Disturbance.\n1975 person-trap: President Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed; PM Indira Gandhi.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n1975 Emergency ka ground source notes mein 'Internal Disturbance' hai; baad mein 44th Amendment ne 'Armed Rebellion'\nkiya.\n3. Article 352 Grounds — W-E-A\n• War\n• External Aggression\n• Armed Rebellion\n TRICK — “W-E-A = War, External, Armed”\n💡\nNational Emergency grounds ko WEA bolo.\nArmed Rebellion = 44th Amendment safeguard replacement.\n4. National Emergency Process — 1 Month, 6 Months, Special, Simple\n• Declaration only on written recommendation of Cabinet\n• Approval\n Special Majority in both Houses\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.18 Emergency Provisions\n• Approval deadline\n within 1 month\n→\n• If Lok Sabha dissolved\n within 30 days of first sitting of new Lok Sabha\n→\n• Renewal\n every 6 months\n→\n• Duration\n unlimited as long as Parliament keeps approving\n→\n• Revocation\n Simple Majority of Lok Sabha\n→\n TRICK — “Written–1–6–Unlimited–LS Simple”\n💡\nWritten Cabinet recommendation\n approval in 1 month\n renewal every 6 months\n no fixed max duration.\n→\n→\n→\nApproval = Special Majority; revocation = Lok Sabha Simple Majority.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNational Emergency: Approval Special Majority, Revocation Simple Majority of Lok Sabha — classic majority trap.\n5. National Emergency Effects — What Changes, What Survives\n• Article 19 freedoms can be suspended under source-notes framework\n• Centre can legislate on State List\n• President can direct State executives\n• Lok Sabha term can be extended by 1 year\n• Revenue sharing can be altered\n• Articles 20 & 21 remain protected\n• Post-44th Amendment, Habeas Corpus protection highlighted in source notes\n• Federal structure continues partly\n TRICK — “State List + LS Year + Revenue Change; 20–21 Safe”\n💡\nEffects ko power-shift ke roop mein dekho: Centre stronger, State autonomy narrower.\nEmergency safety lock: Articles 20 & 21 never suspended.\n6. Judicial Review — Minerva Mills + Article 355\n• Minerva Mills Case, 1980\n Emergency subject to Judicial Review\n→\n• Supreme Court can review validity of grounds for Emergency\n• Article 355\n Union's duty to protect States from external aggression & internal disturbance and ensure constitutional\n→\ngovernance\n TRICK — “Minerva = Review; 355 = Duty”\n💡\nCase-word: Minerva Mills\n Emergency reviewable.\n→\nArticle contrast ahead: 355 = duty, 356 = President's Rule power.\n7. President's Rule — Articles 356 & 365\n• Article 356\n Failure of Constitutional Machinery in State\n→\n• Article 365\n State fails to comply with Union's directions\n→\n• First imposed\n Punjab, 20 June 1951\n→\n• Most times in source notes\n Manipur & Uttar Pradesh, 10 times each\n→\n• Approval\n Simple Majority in both Houses\n→\n• Deadline\n 2 months\n→\n• Renewal\n every 6 months\n→\n• Maximum duration\n 3 years\n→\n• Revocation\n President anytime; no Parliament approval needed\n→\n TRICK — “356 Failure, 365 Defiance”\n💡\n356 = constitutional machinery fails.\n365 = State Union directions nahi maanta.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.18 Emergency Provisions\n TRICK — “2–6–3–President”\n💡\nPresident's Rule process: approval in 2 months\n renew every 6 months\n max 3 years\n President can revoke anytime.\n→\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPresident's Rule approval = Simple Majority, not Special Majority.\n8. President's Rule Effects — FR Safe\n• Centre takes over; division of power affected\n• State Council of Ministers\n dissolved/suspended\n→\n• Legislative Assembly\n suspended or dissolved\n→\n• Fundamental Rights\n no effect\n→\n• Laws made during President's Rule\n remain valid for 6 months after revocation (source notes)\n→\n TRICK — “Government/Assembly Hit, FR Not Hit”\n💡\nPresident's Rule state machinery ko affect karta hai, Fundamental Rights ko suspend nahi karta.\n9. S.R. Bommai Case — 1994\n• S.R. Bommai Case, 1994\n• Article 356 proclamation subject to Judicial Review\n• President's Rule cannot be imposed arbitrarily\n• Floor test should be used to test majority\n• President's Rule = last resort, not first option\n TRICK — “Bommai = 356 + Floor Test + Review”\n💡\nBommai ko Article 356 ke saath permanently lock karo.\n10. Financial Emergency — Article 360\n• Ground\n Threat to financial stability\n→\n• Status in source notes\n Never imposed in India's history\n→\n• Declaration\n President on Cabinet advice\n→\n• Approval\n Simple Majority within 2 months\n→\n• Duration\n Unlimited with Parliamentary continuation\n→\n• Revocation\n President anytime\n→\n• Union-State tax distribution may be suspended\n• Salaries/allowances of Union & State employees may be reduced\n• Source notes explicitly include SC & HC Judges' salaries\n• President may direct States on financial matters\n• State Money Bills may be reserved for President's consideration\n TRICK — “360 = Money Crisis, Never Used”\n💡\nFinancial Emergency ka biggest hook = never imposed.\nExam twist: SC/HC Judges ki salary bhi reduce ho sakti hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nFinancial Emergency theoretical provision hi raha hai in source notes — never imposed.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.18 Emergency Provisions\n11. Article 358 vs 359 — Most Important Comparison\n• Article 358\n suspends only Article 19\n→\n• Article 358\n applies only during War & External Aggression\n→\n• Article 358\n automatic; no separate Presidential Order\n→\n• Article 358\n does NOT apply to Armed Rebellion\n→\n• Article 359\n applies to all FRs except Articles 20 & 21\n→\n• Article 359\n Presidential Order required\n→\n• Article 359\n applies during War/External Aggression and Armed Rebellion\n→\n• Article 359 may apply to whole country or part\n TRICK — “358 = 19 Auto, War/External Only”\n💡\n358 ko ‘19-Auto’ se lock karo.\nArmed Rebellion mein 358 NO.\n TRICK — “359 = President Order, All Except 20–21”\n💡\n359 ke liye Presidential Order chahiye.\n20 & 21 always protected.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nArmed Rebellion mein Art.358 apply nahi; Art.359 apply kar sakta hai.\n12. Articles 20 & 21 — Never Suspended\n• Article 20\n Protection from conviction\n→\n• Article 21\n Right to Life & Liberty\n→\n• 44th Amendment, 1978 explicitly protected both during Emergency\n• Source notes connect this safeguard with the experience of 1975 Emergency and ADM Jabalpur\n TRICK — “20–21 = Emergency-Proof Pair”\n💡\nYe pair poore emergency chapter ka safest memory anchor hai.\n13. All 3 Emergencies — 1–2–2 / Special–Simple–Simple\n• National (352)\n Approval Special Majority; deadline 1 month; max duration unlimited; revoke LS Simple Majority\n→\n• President's Rule (356)\n Approval Simple Majority; deadline 2 months; max 3 years; revoke President anytime\n→\n• Financial (360)\n Approval Simple Majority; deadline 2 months; max unlimited; revoke President anytime\n→\n TRICK — “Deadline = 1–2–2”\n💡\nNational = 1 month.\nPresident's Rule = 2 months.\nFinancial = 2 months.\n TRICK — “Majority = S–Simp–Simp”\n💡\nNational = Special Majority.\n356 and 360 = Simple Majority.\n TRICK — “Revocation = LS / President / President”\n💡\n352\n Lok Sabha Simple Majority.\n→\n356\n President anytime.\n→\n360\n President anytime.\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.18 Emergency Provisions\n14. Criticism Quotes — 3 Names\n• T.T. Krishnamachari\n Fundamental Rights meaningless; democratic foundation undermined\n→\n• Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n Risk of misuse for political objectives\n→\n• Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n President's Rule is a 'dead letter'\n→\n• K.M. Nambiar\n Emergency powers of President = betrayal of Constitution\n→\n TRICK — “TTK–Ambedkar×2–Nambiar”\n💡\nAmbedkar ke 2 separate quotes yaad rakho: misuse + dead letter.\n15. 44th Amendment Safeguards — A-W-L-20/21-6\n• Armed Rebellion replaced Internal Disturbance\n• Written Cabinet recommendation mandatory\n• Lok Sabha can revoke by Simple Majority\n• Articles 20 & 21 explicitly protected\n• 6-month renewal required for continuation\n• Source notes: 44th Amendment passed in 1978 during Morarji Desai government\n TRICK — “A-W-L-20/21-6”\n💡\nA = Armed Rebellion, W = Written Cabinet advice, L = LS revocation, 20/21 protected, 6-month renewal.\nYe 1975 ke baad ka safeguard package hai.\n16. 20-Second Article Flash Chain\n• 352\n National Emergency\n→\n• 355\n Union's duty to protect States\n→\n• 356\n President's Rule\n→\n• 358\n Art.19 automatic suspension framework\n→\n• 359\n Presidential Order affecting FR enforcement except 20/21\n→\n• 360\n Financial Emergency\n→\n• 365\n State fails to comply with Union directions\n→\n TRICK — “352 Emergency, 355 Duty, 356 Rule, 358 Art19, 359 Order, 360 Finance, 365 Defiance”\n💡\nArticle-word pairing se poora chapter quickly reconstruct ho jayega.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part XVIII 352–360, source Germany+GoI1935\n 3 types 352 National,356 President's Rule,360 Financial\n National years 62\n→\n→\nChina,71 Pakistan,75 Internal; Fakhruddin President\n WEA grounds; 44th makes Armed Rebellion\n 352 process Written\n→\n→\nCabinet, 1 month Special, 6-month renewal, unlimited, LS Simple revoke; 20–21 safe; Minerva1980 review\n 355 Duty vs 356\n→\nRule; President's Rule 2-month Simple, 6-month renew, max3yr, President revoke; first Punjab1951; Bommai1994 floor\ntest/review\n 360 never imposed, 2-month Simple, unlimited, President revoke; judges' salary can reduce\n 358 = Art19 Auto\n→\n→\n+ War/External only; 359 = Presidential Order + all FR except 20/21 + Armed Rebellion too\n All-3 compare: deadlines 1-2-2;\n→\nmajority Special-Simple-Simple; revocation LS-President-President\n 44th safeguards A-W-L-20/21-6.”\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\n352 approval Special vs 356/360 Simple | Art.358 = only Art.19 + War/External | Articles 20 & 21 never suspended.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.19 Local Self-Government\nLOCAL SELF-GOVERNMENT — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 19 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Lord Ripon, Panchayati Raj committees, 73rd/74th Amendments, 11th/12th Schedules, municipal\nbodies, PESA, Nyaya Panchayats, Cooperative Societies aur Bihar-specific traps ko fast recall format mein compress kiya\ngaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — State List, Rural + Urban\n• Local Self-Government = State List subject\n• Aim = decentralization of power at grassroots level\n• Rural institution = Panchayati Raj Institutions (PRIs)\n• Urban institution = Municipalities\n• 73rd Amendment, 1992\n Panchayats\n→\n• 74th Amendment, 1992\n Municipalities\n→\n• Bihar special = first State to implement 50% women reservation in Panchayats, 2006\n TRICK — “73 Rural, 74 Urban”\n💡\n73rd ko Panchayat/Rural se lock karo.\n74th ko Municipality/Urban se lock karo.\nChapter ka Bihar star = 50% women reservation, 2006.\n2. Ripon 1882 vs Panchayati Raj 1959\n• Father of Local Self-Government = Lord Ripon\n• Ripon Resolution = 1882\n• Called 'Magna Carta of Local Self-Government'\n• Panchayati Raj launched = 2 October 1959\n• Place = Nagaur, Rajasthan\n• Launched by Jawaharlal Nehru\n• Source notes also mention simultaneous launch in Andhra Pradesh\n TRICK — “1882 = Ripon Idea, 1959 = Panchayat Launch”\n💡\nRipon ko origin/father se jodo; actual Panchayati Raj launch ko 2 Oct 1959 se.\nNagaur + Nehru ek pair hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n1882 aur 1959 ko same event mat samajhna.\n3. Historical Timeline — 82, 52, 53, 57, 59, 92, 93\n• 1882\n Ripon Resolution\n→\n• 1952\n Community Development Scheme\n→\n• 1953\n National Extension Service Scheme\n→\n• 1957\n Balwant Rai Mehta Committee\n→\n• 1959\n Panchayati Raj launch\n→\n• 1992\n 73rd & 74th Amendments passed\n→\n• 24 Apr 1993\n 73rd effective\n→\n• 1 Jun 1993\n 74th effective\n→\n• 2010\n Panchayati Raj Diwas celebrations began\n→\n TRICK — “Ripon–CDS–NES–Balwant–Launch–73/74”\n💡\nTimeline ko year se zyada event-chain ki tarah bolo.\n1992 passed, 1993 effective — dono amendments ke liye.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.19 Local Self-Government\n4. Panchayati Raj Committees — B-A-S-T-G\n• Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957)\n Architect; democratic decentralization; 3-tier system\n→\n• Ashok Mehta Committee (1977–78)\n 2-tier structure; Mandala Panchayat 15,000–20,000 + District Council\n→\n• L.M. Singhvi Committee (1986)\n Constitutional Status + Nyaya Panchayats\n→\n• P.K. Thungan Committee (1988–89)\n timely elections + fixed 5-year tenure\n→\n• V.N. Gadgil Committee (1988)\n direct election to all 3 tiers\n→\n TRICK — “B-A-S-T-G = 3, 2, Status, Tenure, Direct”\n💡\nBalwant = 3-tier.\nAshok = 2-tier.\nSinghvi = Constitutional Status + Nyaya.\nThungan = 5-year tenure.\nGadgil = direct election all tiers.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nBalwant 3-tier implemented; Ashok Mehta 2-tier source notes mein NOT implemented.\n5. 73rd Amendment — Panchayat Pack\n• 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992\n• PM = P.V. Narasimha Rao\n• Effective = 24 April 1993\n Panchayati Raj Diwas\n→\n• Part IX\n• Articles 243–243O\n• 11th Schedule = 29 subjects\n• Gram Sabha = Article 243(A)\n• 3-tier Panchayat = Article 243(B)\n• Term = 5 years\n• Minimum age = 21 years\n• Women reservation = minimum 1/3\n• SC/ST reservation = proportional to population\n• State Finance Commission = every 5 years\n• State Election Commission = conducts elections\n TRICK — “73 = IX + 243–O + 11th/29 + 24 April”\n💡\n73rd ka full pack ek saath bolo: Part IX\n Art.243–243O\n 11th Schedule 29\n effective 24 Apr 1993.\n→\n→\n→\nAge = 21, term = 5, women = 1/3.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPanchayat minimum age = 21; Parliament 25/30 se confuse mat karna.\n6. 3-Tier Panchayat — G-P-Z + D-I-I\n• Tier 1\n Gram Panchayat\n Village\n Direct Election\n→\n→\n→\n• Tier 2\n Panchayat Samiti\n Block\n Indirect Election\n→\n→\n→\n• Tier 3\n Zila Panchayat\n District\n Indirect Election\n→\n→\n→\n TRICK — “G-P-Z = Gram, Panchayat Samiti, Zila”\n💡\nLevel chain: Village\n Block\n District.\n→\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.19 Local Self-Government\n TRICK — “D-I-I = Direct, Indirect, Indirect”\n💡\nSirf Gram Panchayat direct; Block aur District indirect.\nGadgil ne all-three direct recommend kiya tha, adopted pattern source notes mein D-I-I hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nRecommendation aur adopted pattern ko swap mat karna.\n7. Panchayat Special Cases — 20L, WB, M-M-N\n• States below 20 lakh population\n middle tier/Kshetra Panchayat not compulsory\n→\n• West Bengal\n 4-tier Panchayati Raj system in source notes\n→\n• Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland\n no Panchayats; Tribal Councils exist\n→\n TRICK — “<20L No Middle; WB Four; M-M-N Tribal”\n💡\nTeen exceptions ko one-line contrast mein yaad karo.\n8. Reservation — 1/3 Constitution, Bihar 50% First\n• Constitutional minimum women reservation = 1/3\n• SC/ST reservation = proportional to population\n• 1/3 of SC/ST reserved seats also for women\n• Reservation rotates\n• Source notes: Centre allowed 50% women reservation in 2009\n• Bihar = first State to implement 50% in 2006\n• Source notes mention 21+ States currently with 50%\n TRICK — “1/3 India Minimum, Bihar 50 in 2006”\n💡\nBPSC ke liye Bihar-year ko direct lock karo: Bihar\n 50%\n 2006.\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nConstitutional minimum 1/3 hai; Bihar special 50% implementation alag fact hai.\n9. 11th vs 12th Schedule — 29 vs 18 = 47\n• 11th Schedule\n Panchayats\n 29 subjects\n→\n→\n• 12th Schedule\n Municipalities\n 18 subjects\n→\n→\n• Source notes total = 47 subjects\n TRICK — “11>29, 12>18, Total 47”\n💡\nSimple number chain: 29 + 18 = 47.\n11th = Rural/Panchayat; 12th = Urban/Municipality.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n29 aur 18 ko ulta mat karna.\n10. 74th Amendment — Municipality Pack\n• 74th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992\n• Effective = 1 June 1993\n• Part IXA\n• Articles 243P–243ZG\n• 12th Schedule = 18 subjects","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.19 Local Self-Government\n• 1st Municipal Corporation = Madras, 1687\n• Ward Committee = 3 lakh+ population areas, Article 243S\n• District Planning Committee = Article 243ZD\n• Metropolitan Planning Committee = Article 243ZE; 10 lakh+ population\n TRICK — “74 = IXA + P–ZG + 12th/18 + 1 June”\n💡\n74th ka pack: Part IXA\n 243P–243ZG\n 12th Schedule 18\n effective 1 Jun 1993.\n→\n→\n→\nPopulation hooks: Ward = 3L+, Metro Planning = 10L+.\n TRICK — “Madras 1687 = First Municipal Corporation”\n💡\nBombay/Calcutta later; source notes first = Madras.\n11. Municipality Population Ladder — 10K, 20K, 3L\n• Nagar Panchayat = 10,000–20,000\n• Municipal Council = 20,000–3 lakh\n• Municipal Corporation = above 3 lakh\n• Ward Committee mandatory = 3 lakh+ areas\n TRICK — “10K–20K–3L”\n💡\nNagar Panchayat starts 10K.\n20K ke baad Municipal Council.\n3L ke baad Corporation.\n12. Urban Local Bodies — 8 Types\n• Municipal Corporations\n• Municipal Councils\n• Cantonment Boards\n• Town Area Committee\n• Special Purpose Agencies\n• Port Trusts\n• Townships\n• Notified Area Councils\n TRICK — “MC–MC–C–T–S–P–T–N”\n💡\nFirst two Municipal pair, then Cantonment, Town Area, Special, Port, Township, Notified.\nSource trap: Cantonment Boards = military areas, Ministry of Defence; NOT under 74th Amendment.\n13. PESA 1996 — 5th Schedule, Bhuria, 10 States\n• PESA = Panchayats (Extension to Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996\n• Applies to 5th Schedule tribal areas\n• Recommended by Bhuria Commission\n• Implemented in 10 States in source notes\n• Source notes: 5th Schedule areas were outside automatic 73rd Amendment coverage\n• 6th Schedule areas have different tribal-governance arrangements\n TRICK — “PESA = 96 + 5th + Bhuria + 10”\n💡\nPESA number pack: 1996, 5th Schedule, Bhuria Commission, 10 States.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nPESA = 5th Schedule; 6th Schedule tribal areas ka arrangement alag.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.19 Local Self-Government\n14. Nyaya Panchayat vs Gram Nyayalaya\n• Nyaya Panchayat\n village-level justice\n→\n• Handles civil + criminal cases in source notes\n• Fine range = ₹50–₹1,000\n• Recommended by L.M. Singhvi Committee, 1986\n• Gram Nyayalaya\n separate formal court under Gram Nyayalayas Act, 2008\n→\n TRICK — “Singhvi 1986 = Nyaya Panchayat; Act 2008 = Gram Nyayalaya”\n💡\nInformal Panchayat justice vs formal court-under-Act ko separate rakho.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNyaya Panchayat aur Gram Nyayalaya same nahi hain.\n15. Cooperative Societies — 97th Amendment Trifecta\n• 97th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2011\n• Part IX(B) added\n• Articles 243(ZH)–243(ZT)\n• Constitutional status to Cooperative Societies\n• Connected in source notes with FR Article 19(c) — right to form associations\n• DPSP Article 43B added\n TRICK — “97 = FR + DPSP + Part IXB”\n💡\nOne amendment, three hooks:\nFR side 19(c)\n DPSP 43B\n Part IX(B)/243ZH–ZT.\n→\n→\n16. Panchayat Article Flash Chain\n• 243(A)\n Gram Sabha\n→\n• 243(B)\n 3-tier Panchayats\n→\n• 243(D)\n Reservation\n→\n• 243(E)\n Duration 5 years\n→\n• 243(I)\n State Finance Commission, every 5 years\n→\n• 243(K)\n State Election Commission\n→\n• 243(M)\n non-application to certain areas\n→\n• 243(O)\n court interference restricted in elections\n→\n TRICK — “A Sabha, B Build, D Reservation, E 5 Years, I Finance, K Election”\n💡\nHigh-yield letters ko subject ke saath yaad karo.\n17. Municipality Article Flash Chain\n• 243(P)\n Definitions\n→\n• 243(S)\n Ward Committees, 3L+\n→\n• 243(ZD)\n District Planning Committee\n→\n• 243(ZE)\n Metropolitan Planning Committee, 10L+\n→\n• 243(ZG)\n bar to court interference in elections\n→\n• 243(ZH–ZT)\n Cooperative Societies\n→\n TRICK — “S Ward, ZD District, ZE Metro, ZG Election Bar”\n💡\nPlanning pair: ZD = District, ZE = Metropolitan.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.19 Local Self-Government\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“State List + decentralization\n Ripon1882 Magna Carta vs Panchayat launch 2Oct1959 Nagaur-Nehru\n Committees B-A-S-T-\n→\n→\nG = Balwant3, Ashok2, Singhvi Status/Nyaya, Thungan5yr, Gadgil Direct-all\n 73rd: Part IX, 243–O, 11th/29, 24Apr93, age21,\n→\nterm5, women1/3\n 3-tier G-P-Z with D-I-I; exceptions <20L/WB4/MMN tribal\n Bihar first 50% women 2006\n 11th29 vs\n→\n→\n→\n12th18 = 47\n 74th: Part IXA, 243P–ZG, 12th/18, 1Jun93, Madras1687, Ward3L+, Metro10L+\n municipalities 10K-20K /\n→\n→\n20K-3L / 3L+\n PESA1996 = 5th Schedule + Bhuria + 10 States\n Nyaya Panchayat Singhvi1986 vs Gram Nyayalaya Act2008\n\n→\n→\n→\n97th2011 = FR19(c)+DPSP43B+Part IXB\n Panchayat articles A Sabha, B 3-tier, D reservation, E term, I Finance, K Election.”\n→\n Top 3:\n⚠\nRipon 1882 vs Panchayat launch 1959 | 73rd = 11th Schedule/29 vs 74th = 12th/18 | Bihar first 50% women reservation in\nPanchayats = 2006.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.20 Official Language\nOFFICIAL LANGUAGE — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 20 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part XVII, Articles 343–351, 8th Schedule languages, Classical Languages, Hindi Diwas, Official\nLanguage Commission/Committee aur exam traps ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — XVII, 343–351, 22, 11\n• Official Language provisions = Part XVII, Articles 343–351\n• Official Language of Union = Hindi in Devanagari script — Article 343\n• India has NO National Language in the Constitution\n• 8th Schedule = 22 languages; originally 14\n• Added later = 8 languages\n• Classical Languages = 11 in source notes (as of 2024)\n• Hindi Diwas = 14 September\n TRICK — “XVII–343/351–22–11–14 Sep”\n💡\nChapter number spine: Part XVII\n 343–351\n 22 scheduled\n 11 classical\n Hindi Diwas 14 Sep.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nHindi = Official Language, NOT National Language.\n2. Commission & Committee — 1955 Kher, 1957 Pant, 30 Members\n• 1955\n 1st Official Language Commission\n→\n• Chairperson = B.G. Kher\n• Nov 1957\n 1st Joint Parliamentary Committee\n→\n• Chairperson = Govind Vallabh Pant\n• Parliamentary Committee on Official Language = 30 members\n• Composition = 20 Lok Sabha + 10 Rajya Sabha\n• Function = review progress of Hindi in government work & recommend\n TRICK — “55 Kher, 57 Pant, 20+10=30”\n💡\n1955 Commission = B.G. Kher.\n1957 Committee = Govind Vallabh Pant.\nCommittee number pack = 20 LS + 10 RS = 30.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCommission 1955 aur Committee 1957 ko swap mat karna.\n3. Official Language Act Amendment — 1967\n• 1967\n Amendment in Official Language Act\n→\n• Source notes: English continues for government work until non-Hindi speaking States adopt Hindi voluntarily\n TRICK — “1967 = English Continues”\n💡\nOfficial Language Act amendment ko ek hi phrase se lock karo: 1967\n English continues.\n→\n4. 8th Schedule — 14 + 8 = 22\n• Original Scheduled Languages = 14\n• Current Scheduled Languages = 22\n• Added later = 8","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.20 Official Language\n TRICK — “14 + 8 = 22”\n💡\nYe simple arithmetic poore 8th Schedule question ko solve kar deta hai.\n5. Added Languages — 21st, 71st, 92nd\n• 21st Amendment, 1967\n Sindhi (1)\n→\n• 71st Amendment, 1992\n Konkani, Manipuri, Nepali (3)\n→\n• 92nd Amendment, 2003\n Bodo, Dogri, Santhali, Maithili (4)\n→\n TRICK — “21–71–92 = 1–3–4”\n💡\n21st = 1 language.\n71st = 3 languages.\n92nd = 4 languages.\n1 + 3 + 4 = 8 additions.\n TRICK — “K-M-N / B-D-S-M”\n💡\n71st = Konkani–Manipuri–Nepali.\n92nd = Bodo–Dogri–Santhali–Maithili.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nMaithili = 92nd Amendment, 2003 — Bihar ke liye direct exam fact.\n6. English vs Sanskrit — Schedule Trap\n• English is NOT in 8th Schedule\n• Sanskrit IS in 8th Schedule\n• Source notes: Kashmiri and Sindhi have no exclusive State/UT territory\n• Source notes: Scheduled-language speakers may use that language in UPSC exam besides Hindi/English\n TRICK — “English OUT, Sanskrit IN”\n💡\nBas ye opposite pair yaad rakho.\n7. Classical Languages — 2004 to 2024 Timeline\n• 2004\n Tamil (first classical language)\n→\n• 2005\n Sanskrit\n→\n• 2008\n Telugu + Kannada\n→\n• 2013\n Malayalam\n→\n• 2014\n Odia\n→\n• 2024\n Pali + Marathi + Prakrit + Assamese + Bengali\n→\n• Total in source notes = 11\n TRICK — “T-S-TK-M-O + PM-PAB”\n💡\nOld 6: Tamil\n Sanskrit\n Telugu/Kannada\n Malayalam\n Odia.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n2024 five: Pali, Marathi, Prakrit, Assamese, Bengali.\n TRICK — “2024 = Five New, Total 11”\n💡\nPali–Marathi–Prakrit–Assamese–Bengali = 5 additions in 2024.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nClassical Language status Constitution mein mentioned nahi — source notes ise government decision batate hain.\nMaithili 8th Schedule mein hai, lekin source notes ke according classical language nahi.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.20 Official Language\n8. Classical Language Criteria — 1500–2000 + Literature + Texts\n• Language must be 1500–2000 years old\n• Related to Traditional Literature\n• Availability of Ancient Texts & Literature\n• Source notes: process based on recommendations from committees formed by Sahitya Akademi\n TRICK — “Age–Literature–Texts”\n💡\nClassical criteria ko 3 words mein yaad karo: Age\n Literature\n Ancient Texts.\n→\n→\n9. Hindi Diwas vs World Hindi Day\n• Hindi Diwas = 14 September 1949\n• Hindi adopted as Official Language by Constituent Assembly\n• Celebrated annually on 14 September\n• World Hindi Day = 10 January\n• First World Hindi Conference = Nagpur, 1975\n• World Hindi Day celebrated globally on 10 January\n TRICK — “14 Sep = India; 10 Jan = World”\n💡\nHindi Diwas = National/India.\nWorld Hindi Day = International/global.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n14 September aur 10 January ko swap mat karna.\n10. Article 343–351 — Fast Map\n• 343\n Official Language of Union = Hindi in Devanagari\n→\n• 344\n Commission & Committee on Official Language\n→\n• 345\n Official Language of State\n→\n• 346\n Communication between States and Union\n→\n• 348\n Language of Judiciary & Legislation\n→\n• 350(A)\n Education in mother tongue at primary stage\n→\n• 351\n Promotion & development of Hindi\n→\n TRICK — “343 Hindi, 344 Committee, 345 State, 346 Communication, 348 Court, 350A Mother Tongue, 351\n💡\n\nPromote Hindi”\nArticle-word pairing se poora Part XVII reconstruct ho jayega.\n11. Article 348 — Court Language Trap\n• Source notes: Supreme Court & High Court proceedings = English\n• Unless President authorizes otherwise\n TRICK — “348 = High Courts Speak English”\n💡\n348 ko Judiciary-English se lock karo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSource notes explicitly warn: highest courts ki constitutional language English hai.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.20 Official Language\n12. Article 351 — Centre's Duty\n• Article 351\n Promotion & development of Hindi language\n→\n• Centre's duty to develop Hindi\n TRICK — “351 = Hindi Promote”\n💡\n343 = Hindi official; 351 = Hindi promote/develop.\n13. 20-Second Flash Recall\n• Part XVII, Articles 343–351\n• Hindi = Official, not National\n• 8th Schedule = 22 = 14 + 8\n• 21st/71st/92nd = 1/3/4 additions\n• Maithili = 92nd Amendment, 2003\n• Classical = Tamil first 2004; total 11 in source notes\n• Hindi Diwas = 14 Sep; World Hindi Day = 10 Jan\n• 1955 Kher; 1957 Pant; Committee 20+10=30\n• 343 Hindi, 348 Judiciary-English, 351 Promote Hindi\n TRICK — “Hindi–22–1/3/4–11–14Sep/10Jan–55/57–343/348/351”\n💡\nExam se pehle sirf ye compressed chain ek baar bolna enough hai.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part XVII 343–351\n Art343 Hindi in Devanagari, NO National Language\n 1955 B.G. Kher Commission, 1957 Govind Vallabh\n→\n→\n\nPant Committee, 20LS+10RS=30\n 1967 English continues\n 8th Schedule 14+8=22; amendments 21-71-92 = 1-3-4: Sindhi / K-\n→\n→\nM-N / B-D-S-M; Maithili 92nd-2003\n English OUT, Sanskrit IN\n Classical: Tamil2004 first, Sanskrit05, Telugu-Kannada08,\n→\n→\nMalayalam13, Odia14, 2024 Pali-Marathi-Prakrit-Assamese-Bengali = total11; classical not constitutional\n Hindi Diwas\n→\n14Sep1949 vs World Hindi Day 10Jan\n Articles: 344 Committee,345 State,346 Communication, 348 Judiciary-English,350A\n→\nMother Tongue,351 Hindi Promotion.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nHindi is Official, not National | 21st/71st/92nd = 1/3/4 additions | Hindi Diwas 14 Sep vs World Hindi Day 10 Jan.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.21 Constitutional Amendment\nCONSTITUTIONAL AMENDMENT — MEMORY TRICK\nSHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 21 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Part XX, Article 368, 3 majority types, amendment procedure, important amendments, Basic\nStructure, scholars aur exam traps ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Master Skeleton — XX, 368, South Africa, 106\n• Constitutional Amendment = Part XX, Article 368\n• Source = South Africa\n• Source notes: 106 Amendments so far\n• Constitution = rigid + flexible\n• Basic Structure cannot be destroyed by amendment\n• Key case = Keshavananda Bharati, 1973\n TRICK — “XX–368–South Africa–106–Keshavananda”\n💡\nChapter ka master spine isi line mein yaad karo.\nK.C. Wheare = rigidity aur flexibility ka fine balance.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nAmendment power wide hai, but Basic Structure destroy nahi ki ja sakti.\n2. Amendment Bill — Kahan, Kaun, Kya Nahi\n• Bill can be introduced in either House of Parliament\n• Can be introduced by any minister or private member\n• Prior President approval NOT required\n• President's veto NOT available after 24th Amendment, 1971\n• Joint Sitting NOT available\n• State Legislature cannot introduce Amendment Bill\n• States only ratify when required\n TRICK — “Either House + Any Member; No Prior Approval, No Veto, No Joint”\n💡\nAmendment Bill ka negative pack: NO prior President approval, NO veto, NO joint sitting.\nState ka role = ratification only.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n24th Amendment ke baad President must assent; veto nahi.\n3. Standard Procedure — I-P-S-A\n• I = Introduce in either House\n• P = Pass by Special Majority in each House separately\n• S = States ratify if required — minimum 1/2 State Legislatures\n• A = Assent by President is mandatory\n TRICK — “I-P-S-A = Introduce, Pass, States, Assent”\n💡\nProcedure ko four-step chain mein yaad karo.\nJoint Sitting absent — dono Houses separately pass karte hain.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.21 Constitutional Amendment\n4. Special Majority — Double Condition\n• 2/3 of members present & voting\n• AND majority of total membership of the House\n• Both conditions must be satisfied simultaneously\n TRICK — “2/3 P&V + 50%+ Total”\n💡\nSpecial Majority = double-lock.\nSirf 2/3 present & voting enough nahi; total membership majority bhi chahiye.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSpecial Majority ki dono conditions simultaneously meet honi chahiye.\n5. 3 Types of Majority — Simple / Special / Special+States\nSimple Majority\n• Entry & formation of new States\n• Legislative Council formation/abolition\n• MP salaries/allowances\n• Grant/removal of citizenship\n• 5th & 6th Schedules\n• Source notes: these changes are outside Article 368\n TRICK — “State–Council–Salary–Citizenship–5/6”\n💡\nSimple category ko S-C-S-C-5/6 se yaad karo.\nMost important trap: Simple Majority changes Art.368 ke under constitutional amendments ke roop mein count nahi hote.\nSpecial Majority\n• Fundamental Rights\n• DPSPs\n• All provisions not in Category 1 or 3\n• Example in source notes = 103rd Amendment, EWS 10%\n• Most Constitutional amendments fall here\n TRICK — “FR + DPSP + Most Others = Special”\n💡\nJab neither simple nor federal-state category ho, default memory = Special Majority.\nSpecial Majority + State Ratification\n• Presidential Election & Procedure\n• Centre-State executive power\n• Supreme Court & High Courts\n• GST\n• 7th Schedule subjects\n• Representation of States in Parliament\n• Minimum ratification = half of State Legislatures\n TRICK — “President–Federal–Courts–GST–7th–State Representation”\n💡\nFederal nature ke provisions ko State Ratification se jodo.\nSource notes current arithmetic: 28 States\n minimum 14 ratifications.\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nState ratification = minimum 50% State Legislatures.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.21 Constitutional Amendment\n6. Old Important Amendments — 1, 7, 15, 24, 26, 36, 40\n• 1st (1951)\n 9th Schedule; land reform laws\n→\n• 7th (1956)\n State Reorganization\n→\n• 15th (1963)\n HC retirement age 60 to 62\n→\n• 24th (1971)\n President must assent to Amendment Bill; no veto\n→\n• 26th (1971)\n Privy Purses abolished\n→\n• 36th (1975)\n Sikkim full State, 22nd State\n→\n• 40th (1976)\n Article 297 amended; Exclusive Economic Zone\n→\n TRICK — “1-9th, 7-States, 15-HC62, 24-NoVeto, 26-Purse, 36-Sikkim, 40-EEZ”\n💡\nNumber ko keyword se jodo; full sentence ratne ki zaroorat nahi.\n7. 42nd vs 44th — Opposite Direction Pair\n• 42nd Amendment, 1976 = 'Mini Constitution'\n• Added Socialist, Secular, Integrity in Preamble\n• Fundamental Duties added — Part IV-A\n• President bound by Cabinet advice\n• Source notes: Emergency duration 6 months to 1 year\n• 44th Amendment, 1978\n Right to Property removed from FR; became Legal Right\n→\n• Articles 20 & 21 protected\n• 'Armed Rebellion' replaced 'Internal Disturbance'\n• President can return Cabinet advice once; after resubmission binding\n TRICK — “42 Adds, 44 Repairs/Protects”\n💡\n42 = add/expand changes.\n44 = rollback/protection type changes in source notes.\nCore pair: 42 SSI + Duties | 44 Property Legal + 20/21 + Armed Rebellion.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n42nd = Mini Constitution; 44th ko uske kuch changes reverse/protect karne wale amendment ke roop mein yaad rakho.\n8. 61–88 Amendment Ladder\n• 61st (1989)\n Voting age 21 to 18\n→\n• 69th (1991)\n Delhi = NCT; Vidhan Sabha 70 seats; CoM max 10% / 7\n→\n• 73rd (1992)\n Panchayati Raj constitutional status; 11th Schedule, 29 subjects\n→\n• 74th (1992)\n Municipalities constitutional status; 12th Schedule, 18 subjects\n→\n• 86th (2002)\n Article 21A, free education 6–14 + new Fundamental Duty\n→\n• 88th (2003)\n Service Tax\n→\n TRICK — “61 Vote18, 69 Delhi, 73 Panchayat, 74 Municipality, 86 Education, 88 Service Tax”\n💡\nYe ladder direct exam recall ke liye enough hai.\n9. Recent Amendments 100–106 — Straight Chain\n• 100th (2015)\n Bangladesh Land Boundary Agreement\n→\n• 101st (2016)\n GST; Articles 246A, 269A, 279A\n→\n• 102nd (2018)\n Constitutional status to NCBC\n→\n• 103rd (2019)\n 10% EWS reservation\n→\n• 104th (2020)\n Anglo-Indian reserved seats abolished\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.21 Constitutional Amendment\n• 105th (2021)\n States empowered to identify OBCs for own list\n→\n• 106th (2023)\n 1/3 women reservation in Lok Sabha & State Assemblies; Nari Shakti Vandan Act\n→\n TRICK — “100 Land, 101 GST, 102 NCBC, 103 EWS, 104 Anglo, 105 OBC, 106 Women”\n💡\n100 se 106 tak har number ko ek single keyword se lock karo.\n TRICK — “101–103–106 = GST–EWS–Women”\n💡\nRecent trio most asked in source notes: 101 GST, 103 EWS 10%, 106 Women 1/3.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSource notes: 106th women reservation effective after next delimitation (2026+).\n10. Basic Structure — Keshavananda 1973\n• Supremacy of Constitution\n• Democratic & Republican form\n• Secular character\n• Separation of Powers\n• Judicial Review\n• Article 32 / Writ Jurisdiction\n• Federalism\n• Unity & Integrity\n TRICK — “S-D-S-S-J-A-F-U”\n💡\nSupremacy\n Democracy\n Secular\n Separation\n Judicial Review\n Art.32\n Federalism\n Unity.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nBasic Structure = amendment-proof core.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nBasic Structure ko destroy karne wala amendment valid nahi.\n11. Important Acts — 54, 56/05, 61, 71\n• 1954\n Special Marriage Act\n inter-faith civil marriage\n→\n→\n• 1956 Hindu Succession Act; 2005 amendment gave daughters equal HUF property rights\n• 1961\n Dowry Prohibition Act\n→\n• 1971\n Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act\n Flag/Anthem disrespect penalized\n→\n→\n• Source notes connect National Honour Act with Article 51A(a)\n TRICK — “54 Marriage, 56/05 Daughter, 61 Dowry, 71 Honour”\n💡\nAct-year memory chain: Marriage\n Succession\n Dowry\n National Honour.\n→\n→\n→\n12. Scholars — Wheare vs Austin\n• K.C. Wheare\n 'fine balance between rigidity & flexibility'\n→\n• Granville Austin\n amendment procedure = most accepted & diverse part of Constitution\n→\n TRICK — “Wheare = Balance, Austin = Accepted & Diverse”\n💡\nScholar-name ko keyword se jodo.\n13. Flexibility vs Rigidity\n• Flexible side\n many provisions by Simple Majority\n→\n• Flexible side\n no separate constituent body needed\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.21 Constitutional Amendment\n• Source notes\n 106 amendments in 75 years\n→\n• Rigid side\n Basic Structure cannot be amended\n→\n• Rigid side\n Special Majority for most changes\n→\n• Rigid side\n State ratification for federal provisions\n→\n TRICK — “Flexible = Simple/No Separate Body; Rigid = Special/States/Basic”\n💡\nExam comparison ko 2-column picture mein yaad karo.\n14. 20-Second Flash Recall\n• Part XX | Art.368 | South Africa | 106\n• No prior President approval | No veto | No Joint Sitting\n• Special Majority = 2/3 present & voting + majority of total membership\n• 3 categories = Simple / Special / Special + States\n• 24th = no veto | 42nd = Mini Constitution | 44th = Property legal + 20/21\n• 61st = vote18 | 73rd = Panchayat | 74th = Municipality | 86th = Education\n• 101 GST | 103 EWS | 106 Women\n• Keshavananda 1973 = Basic Structure\n TRICK — “368–NoVeto–NoJoint–2/3+Total–42/44–101/103/106–Basic”\n💡\nExam se pehle isi chain ko 2 baar bolna.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Part XX Art368, source South Africa, 106 amendments, rigid+flexible\n Bill either House by minister/private member; no\n→\nprior President approval, no veto after 24th/1971, no Joint Sitting, States ratify only\n procedure I-P-S-A\n Special majority =\n→\n→\n2/3 present&voting + majority total\n 3 types: Simple outside368; Special most; Special+States federal provisions, minimum\n→\nhalf States\n old anchors 1-9th,7-StateReorg,15-HC62,24-NoVeto,26-PrivyPurse,36-Sikkim,40-EEZ\n 42 Mini Constitution: SSI +\n→\n→\n\nDuties; 44 Property Legal + 20/21 + Armed Rebellion\n 61 Vote18,69 Delhi,73 Panchayat,74 Municipality,86 Education,88\n→\nServiceTax\n 100 Land,101 GST,102 NCBC,103 EWS,104 Anglo,105 OBC,106 Women\n Keshavananda1973 protects Basic\n→\n→\nStructure; scholars Wheare-Balance, Austin-Accepted&Diverse.”\n Top 3:\n⚠\nAmendment Bill = no President veto + no Joint Sitting | Special Majority = 2/3 present & voting + majority of total\nmembership | 42nd = Mini Constitution vs 44th = key rollback/protection amendment.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.22 Constitutional & Non-Constitutional Bodies\nCONSTITUTIONAL & NON-CONSTITUTIONAL BODIES —\nMEMORY TRICK SHEET\nIndian Polity · Chapter 22 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — Constitutional, Statutory aur Non-Statutory bodies ke Article/Act, tenure, appointment-removal,\nchairperson traps aur high-yield comparisons ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. 3 Categories — Constitution / Act / Resolution\n• Constitutional Bodies\n Constitution mein mentioned; powers Constitution se\n→\n• Statutory Bodies\n Act of Parliament se created\n→\n• Non-Constitutional & Non-Statutory\n Cabinet Resolution / Executive decision\n→\n• Constitutional examples\n ECI 324, UPSC 315, CAG 148, AG 76, NCSC/NCST/NCBC 338/338A/338B, GST Council 279A,\n→\nLinguistic Minorities 350B\n• Statutory examples\n NHRC 1993, CIC RTI 2005, CVC Act 2003, Lokpal Act 2013, NCW Act 1990, CBI under DSPE Act\n→\n1946\n• NITI Aayog\n Non-Constitutional + Non-Statutory\n→\n TRICK — “Constitution = Article, Statutory = Act, NITI = Resolution”\n💡\nBody type identify karne ka fastest rule.\nNITI vs Lokpal vs NHRC: NITI non-statutory; Lokpal statutory; NHRC statutory.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNITI Aayog ko statutory body mat mark karna.\n2. Election Commission — 324–329, 25 Jan, 3 Members\n• ECI = Part XV, Articles 324–329\n• Established = 25 Jan 1950\n• 3 members = 1 CEC + 2 ECs\n• Quasi-judicial body\n• Single-member till 16 Oct 1989; multi-member from 1 Oct 1993\n• 1st CEC = Sukumar Sen\n• National Voters' Day = 25 January since 2011\n• Tenure = 6 years or 65 years, whichever earlier\n TRICK — “324–329 | 25 Jan | 1+2 | 6/65”\n💡\nECI number pack ko ek line mein yaad karo.\nAppointment + Removal\n• Appointed by President\n• Source notes' 2023 committee = PM + LoP (LS) + Union Cabinet Minister nominated by PM\n• CEC removal = like Supreme Court Judge, Special Majority\n• Other ECs removal = on CEC recommendation\n• Resignation = President\n TRICK — “CEC Hard, EC Easy”\n💡\nCEC removal = SC Judge level.\nOther EC removal = CEC recommendation.\n TRICK — “ECI = Parliament/State Legislature + President/VP; SEC = Panchayat/Municipality”\n💡\nPanchayat/Municipality elections State Election Commission Art.243K ke under.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.22 Constitutional & Non-Constitutional Bodies\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nECI local-body elections conduct nahi karta.\n3. Electoral Reform Committees — 74, 90, 98\n• Tarkunde Committee\n 1974\n→\n• Dinesh Goswami Committee\n 1990\n→\n• Indrajit Gupta Committee\n 1998\n→\n TRICK — “T-D-I = 74–90–98”\n💡\nTarkunde\n Dinesh\n Indrajit; years increasing order.\n→\n→\n4. UPSC vs SPSC — 315–323, 6/65 vs 6/62\n• UPSC = Part XIV, Articles 315–323\n• Established 1926; recommended by Lee Commission\n• UPSC tenure = 6 years / 65 age\n• SPSC tenure = 6 years / 62 age\n• UPSC appointment = President; SPSC appointment = Governor\n• UPSC removal = President after SC inquiry\n• SPSC removal = President; Governor only suspends\n• UPSC salary = CF of India; SPSC salary = CF of State\n• UPSC report = President; SPSC report = Governor\n• Second term = not eligible for both\n TRICK — “UPSC 65, SPSC 62; Appoint P/G, Remove P/P”\n💡\nAppointment differs: President vs Governor.\nRemoval same final authority: President.\n TRICK — “UPSC Chairman = No Govt Job; SPSC Chairman = UPSC/other SPSC possible”\n💡\nPost-retirement difference direct exam trap hai.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nSPSC Chairman ko Governor remove nahi karta — President removes.\n5. Civil Services Hooks — 1926 / Cornwallis / Tagore / 21 April\n• UPSC established = 1926, Lee Commission\n• Father of Civil Services = Lord Cornwallis\n• 1st Indian ICS = Satyendra Nath Tagore\n• Source notes: 1st Civil Services Exam = London & Allahabad, 1922\n• Civil Services Day = 21 April\n• All India Services only 3 in source notes = IAS, IPS, Indian Forest Service\n• Article 312 gives Parliament power to create All India Services\n TRICK — “Cornwallis–Tagore–21 April; AIS = IAS–IPS–Forest”\n💡\nIFS (Foreign) ko All India Service nahi mark karna.\n6. CAG — 148, 6/65, No Reappointment\n• CAG = Article 148\n• Tenure = 6 years / 65 years\n• Appointed by President","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.22 Constitutional & Non-Constitutional Bodies\n• Guardian of public finances\n• Audits Union + State expenditure/revenue and Consolidated Fund expenditure\n• Removal = like SC Judge, Special Majority\n• No reappointment after retirement\n• 1st CAG = V. Narahari Rao (1948)\n• PAC examines CAG reports; source notes say PAC chairman = Opposition Leader\n TRICK — “148–6/65–SC Removal–NO Return”\n💡\nCAG ka four-hook pack.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCAG reappointment = NO.\n7. Attorney General — 76 + 89 = 165\n• AG = Article 76, Part V\n• Chief Legal Advisor / 1st Law Officer of India\n• Qualification = eligible to be Supreme Court Judge\n• Appointed by President\n• Can speak in Parliament but cannot vote\n• Private legal practice not barred in source notes\n• 1st AG = M.C. Setalvad\n TRICK — “AG = Speak YES, Vote NO, Practice YES”\n💡\nAG ko Judge ke saath confuse mat karo.\n TRICK — “76 + 89 = 165”\n💡\nAttorney General Art.76\n Advocate General Art.165.\n→\nAG qualification SC Judge-level; Advocate General HC Judge-level.\n8. GST Council — 279A, 101st, Finance Minister\n• GST Council = Article 279A\n• Created by 101st Amendment, 2016\n• Constituted by President\n• Chairman = Union Finance Minister\n• Members include Union MoS Finance + State Finance Minister/nominee\n• Vice-Chairman elected among themselves\n TRICK — “279A–101–FM”\n💡\nGST Council ka straight 3-point code.\n TRICK — “GST = FM; Inter-State Council = PM; NITI = PM”\n💡\nChairperson confusion isi contrast se solve karo.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nGST Council Chairman PM nahi, Union Finance Minister hai.\n9. NCSC–NCST–NCBC — 338 / 338A / 338B\n• NCSC = Art.338\n 65th Amendment, 1990\n→\n• NCST = Art.338A\n 89th Amendment, 2003; effective 2004\n→\n• NCBC = Art.338B\n 102nd Amendment, 2018\n→","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.22 Constitutional & Non-Constitutional Bodies\n• NCSC & NCST originally combined; 89th Amendment bifurcated them\n• NCBC earlier Statutory (1993); constitutional status in 2018\n• All 3\n Chairperson + VP + 3 members\n→\n• All appointed by President; tenure = 3 years\n TRICK — “338–338A–338B = SC–ST–BC”\n💡\nBase article 338; A = Tribe, B = Backward Classes.\n TRICK — “65–89–102”\n💡\nNCSC 65th\n NCST 89th\n NCBC 102nd.\n→\n→\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nTeen bodies ke amendment numbers alag-alag hain.\n10. Linguistic Minorities — 350B, 7th Amendment\n• Article 350B\n• 7th Constitutional Amendment, 1956\n• Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities\n• One-membered body\n• Appointed by President\n• Works under Union Ministry of Minority Affairs\n• HQ = New Delhi\n TRICK — “350B = Bhasha Officer, One Member”\n💡\nB ko Bhasha se yaad karo.\n11. NHRC — Statutory 1993, 3/70, Former CJI\n• NHRC = Statutory\n• Human Rights Protection Act, 1993\n• Established 12 Oct 1993; source notes also mention in effect 28 Sep 1993\n• 12 members = Chairperson + 11\n• Tenure = 3 years / 70 years\n• Chairperson must be former CJI\n• 1st Chairperson = Ranganath Misra\n• International Human Rights Day = 10 December\n TRICK — “NHRC = 1993–12 Members–3/70–Former CJI–10 Dec”\n💡\nNHRC ka high-yield number pack.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNHRC max age 70; CAG/UPSC 65. Human Rights Day 10 Dec, not 12 Oct.\n12. CIC — RTI 2005, 12 Oct, 3/65\n• CIC = Statutory; RTI Act 2005\n• RTI effective = 12 Oct 2005\n• 1 CIC + up to 10 Information Commissioners\n• Tenure = 3 years / 65 age\n• Appointed by President; resignation to President\n• Union level = CIC; State Information Commission = appointed by Governor","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.22 Constitutional & Non-Constitutional Bodies\n TRICK — “CIC = RTI–12 Oct 2005–3/65”\n💡\nDate + tenure ko ek line mein lock karo.\n13. CVC vs CBI — Same Santhanam, Different Job\n• CVC established 1964; K. Santhanam Committee\n• CVC statutory status = CVC Act 2003; Central Agency 2004\n• CVC tenure = 4 years / 65 age\n• CVC composition = 1 Chairman + up to 2 Vigilance Commissioners\n• CVC selection = PM + LoP + Union Home Minister\n• CBI founded 1963; recommended by K. Santhanam Committee\n• CBI governed by DSPE Act, 1946\n• CBI motto = Industry, Impartiality, Integrity\n• CBI under Ministry of Personnel, Pension & Public Grievances\n• CBI Director recommendation committee headed by PM\n TRICK — “Santhanam Twins: CVC Supervises, CBI Investigates”\n💡\nSame committee, different role.\n TRICK — “CVC = PM+LoP+Home; CEC = PM+LoP+Cabinet Minister”\n💡\nLoP common; third member different.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nCVC committee mein Home Minister; CEC committee source notes mein nominated Cabinet Minister.\n14. NCW + NCM — 7 Members Each\n• NCW Act 1990; established Jan 1992\n• NCW = 7 members: Chairperson + 5 Members + Member Secretary\n• NCW appointed by Central Government; Chairperson term 3 years\n• 1st NCW Chairperson = Jayanti Patnaik\n• NCM established 1978 by Central Government resolution\n• NCM statutory status = Commission for Minorities Act, 1992\n• NCM = 7 members: Chairperson + VP + 5\n• 6 minorities in source notes = Jain, Buddhist, Muslim, Sikh, Christian, Parsi\n TRICK — “NCW 1990/92 + 7; NCM 1978/92 + 7”\n💡\nDono 7-member bodies; years alag.\n TRICK — “J-B-M-S-C-P = 6 Minorities”\n💡\nJain, Buddhist, Muslim, Sikh, Christian, Parsi.\n15. Lokpal — 2013, 5/70, 1+8, 50–50\n• Lokpal = Statutory; Lokpal & Lokayuktas Act, 2013\n• Tenure = 5 years / 70 age\n• 1 Chairperson + up to 8 members\n• At least 50% from SC/ST/OBC/Minority/Women\n• 50% from Judicial background\n• Appointed by President\n• Covers Group A, B, C, D officers\n• Lokpal Centre; Lokayuktas States","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.22 Constitutional & Non-Constitutional Bodies\n• 1st Lokpal India = Pinaki Chandra Ghose, 2019\n• 1st Lokayukta India = Maharashtra, 1971\n• Source notes: PM inquiry requires 7/8 full-bench approval; some subjects excluded\n TRICK — “Lokpal = 2013–5/70–1+8–50/50”\n💡\nTenure + composition + reservation/background ka one-line pack.\n TRICK — “PM = Included, but Restricted”\n💡\nSource notes: 7/8 approval; International Peace, External Affairs, Atomic Energy excluded.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nLokpal PM ko completely exclude nahi karta; source notes mein conditional coverage hai.\n16. NITI Aayog — 1 Jan 2015, PM, Non-Statutory\n• Full name = National Institution for Transforming India\n• Established = 1 Jan 2015\n• Replaced Planning Commission (1950)\n• Nature = Non-Constitutional + Non-Statutory; Cabinet Resolution\n• Chairperson = Prime Minister ex-officio\n• 1st Vice-Chairperson = Arvind Panagariya\n• Source notes list current VC = Suman Bery; CEO = B.V.R. Subrahmanyam\n• Planning Commission also non-statutory in source notes\n• Planning Commission had binding allocations; NITI role advisory\n TRICK — “NITI = 2015–PM–Resolution–Advisory”\n💡\nNo Article, no Act — Cabinet Resolution.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\nNITI Aayog non-constitutional AND non-statutory.\n17. Tenure/Age Super-Comparison\n• ECI CEC\n 6 years / 65\n→\n• UPSC\n 6 / 65\n→\n• SPSC\n 6 / 62\n→\n• CAG\n 6 / 65\n→\n• NCSC/NCST/NCBC\n 3 years\n→\n• NHRC\n 3 / 70\n→\n• CIC\n 3 / 65\n→\n• CVC\n 4 / 65\n→\n• Lokpal\n 5 / 70\n→\n• NCW Chairperson\n 3 years\n→\n TRICK — “6/65 Club = ECI–UPSC–CAG; 70 Club = NHRC–Lokpal”\n💡\nSPSC outlier = 6/62.\nCVC = 4/65; CIC = 3/65.\n EXAM TRAP\n⚠\n70-year bodies in source comparison = NHRC and Lokpal.","ExamFusion Prep · Indian Polity Memory Tricks · Ch.22 Constitutional & Non-Constitutional Bodies\n18. Appointment/Removal Super-Comparison\n• CEC\n President appoints; removal like SC Judge\n→\n• Other ECs\n removal on CEC recommendation\n→\n• UPSC\n President appoints/removes after SC inquiry\n→\n• SPSC\n Governor appoints; President removes; Governor suspends\n→\n• CAG\n President appoints; removal like SC Judge\n→\n• AG\n President; at President's pleasure\n→\n• NHRC/CIC/CVC/Lokpal\n source comparison lists President appointment\n→\n• NCW\n Central Government\n→\n• NITI VC\n source comparison lists PM / at PM's pleasure\n→\n TRICK — “Governor Appoints SPSC, President Removes SPSC”\n💡\nSabse common appointment-removal mismatch.\n19. Major Political Parties — Year Chain from Source Notes\n• INC\n 1885\n→\n• CPI(M)\n 1964\n→\n• BJP\n 1980\n→\n• BSP\n 1984, Kanshi Ram\n→\n• AAP\n 2012, Arvind Kejriwal\n→\n• NPP\n 2013, P.A. Sangma; source notes say national-party recognition in 2019\n→\n TRICK — “1885–64–80–84–12–13”\n💡\nINC\n CPI(M)\n BJP\n BSP\n AAP\n NPP.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n20. 20-Second Article/Act Flash Chain\n• ECI 324 | UPSC 315 | CAG 148 | AG 76\n• NCSC 338 | NCST 338A | NCBC 338B\n• GST 279A | Linguistic Minorities 350B\n• NHRC 1993 | CIC RTI 2005 | CVC Act 2003 | CBI DSPE 1946\n• NCW Act 1990 | Lokpal Act 2013 | NITI = Cabinet Resolution\n TRICK — “324–315–148–76 / 338-A-B / 279A–350B / Acts 93–05–03–46–90–13 / NITI Resolution”\n💡\nArticle-body mapping aur Act-body mapping ko alag blocks mein revise karo.\n Master Memory Chain — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\n🔗\n 1-Line Revision Chain\n🔗\n“Types: Constitution-Article, Statutory-Act, NITI-Resolution\n ECI 324-329, 25Jan, 1+2, 6/65; CEC SC-judge removal, EC on CEC;\n→\nECI not local polls\n UPSC/SPSC 315-323: 6/65 vs 6/62, appoint P/G but remove P/P; UPSC Chairman no govt job\n CAG148 6/65\n→\n→\nSC-removal no reappointment\n AG76 speak/no-vote/private practice; +89=AdvG165\n GST279A/101st/FM\n 338/338A/338B =\n→\n→\n→\n\nNCSC/NCST/NCBC with 65/89/102 amendments\n 350B linguistic one-member\n NHRC1993 3/70 former CJI, Human Rights\n→\n→\nDay10Dec\n CIC RTI2005 12Oct 3/65\n CVC1964/Act2003 PM-LoP-Home vs CBI1963 DSPE1946, both Santhanam\n\n→\n→\n→\nNCW1990/92 7; NCM1978/92 7 + 6 minorities\n Lokpal2013 5/70 1+8 50/50, PM conditional\n NITI1Jan2015 PM non-\n→\n→\nconstitutional/non-statutory advisory\n 6/65 club ECI-UPSC-CAG; 70 club NHRC-Lokpal.”\n→\n Top 5:\n⚠\nNITI = non-statutory | SPSC removal = President | CAG no reappointment | GST Council Chair = Finance Minister |\nNHRC & Lokpal age = 70."];
